window.THEMESETS = [{"id":"t1a","name":"Foundations, design and ethics - Set A","short":"Foundations, design and ethics - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on what you are allowed to claim: the three things a causal claim needs, what a p-value really says, what a non-significant result with low power means, research integrity, and the design words you must be able to tell apart.","theme":1,"icon":"🧭","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Joost Veenstra, a researcher at a Dutch insurance firm, finds that staff who use the company sleep app report better sleep than staff who do not. He also notices that app users exercise more and start work later. He wants to write that the app improves sleep. What does he still have to establish before that sentence is allowed?","o":["A sample large enough for the result to reach p below .05.","That app use and sleep go together, that the app use came first, and that other explanations for the link have been ruled out.","That the correlation between app use and sleep quality is at least .50.","That the same pattern appears again in a second group of staff."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"causal_criteria","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The stem describes a link and then asks what is needed before saying one thing CAUSES the other. Words like \"he wants to write that X improves Y\" or \"before claiming\" are the signal. The answer is always the same three-part list.","plain":"In plain words: he has a correlation and wants a cause. The question is asking you to recite the entry ticket for a causal claim.","work":"Step 1. Covariation. Yes, app users do sleep better, so the variables go together.\nStep 2. Temporal order. Not shown. Poor sleepers may have downloaded the app because they slept badly.\nStep 3. Alternative explanations. Not ruled out. Exercise and a later start time both plausibly improve sleep, and both differ between the groups.\nStep 4. So two of the three requirements are open. The only option that names all three requirements is the correct one.\nMemory hook: TOC. Together, Order, Clear of confounders.","wrongs":["A bigger sample makes the estimate more precise. It does nothing about the direction of the effect or about confounders. A huge sample can give you a very precise measurement of a confounded link.","","Size of a correlation is not a criterion. A strong correlation is still a correlation. This is the classic \"correlation read as causation\" mistake dressed up with a number.","Replication shows the link is real, not that it is causal. Repeating a confounded study just reproduces the confound."],"trap":"Students reach for sample size or significance because those feel like \"more evidence\". Neither touches causality. Only design does.","tri":"Symptom: a link is found and a causal sentence is being proposed. -&gt; Diagnosis: criteria for a causal claim. -&gt; Treatment: check the three requirements: 1 covariation (they move together), 2 temporal order (the cause came first), 3 no plausible alternative explanation (confounders ruled out, usually by random assignment)."},{"n":2,"q":"Lucia Mendes studies a free breakfast club at a Brazilian primary school. Attendance is voluntary and is recorded over a whole term, and reading is tested at the end of that term. Children who attended more often read better. Which one of the three requirements for a causal claim is clearly still unmet here?","o":["The two variables do not go together, so there is nothing to explain.","The supposed cause does not come before the supposed effect.","Alternative explanations have not been ruled out, because the children chose whether to attend.","All three requirements are met, so she may claim that breakfast causes better reading."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"causal_criteria","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The stem hands you a study and asks WHICH requirement is missing, not what the requirements are. Read the design: does it have covariation, does it have time order, was assignment random?","plain":"In plain words: tick off the three requirements one by one against this study and find the one that fails.","work":"Step 1. Covariation. Met. More attendance goes with better reading.\nStep 2. Temporal order. Met. Attendance was recorded over the term, the reading test came at the end, so the supposed cause did come first.\nStep 3. Alternative explanations. Not met. Attendance was voluntary, so the children who came may differ in home support, income, or motivation, and any of those could raise reading scores by itself.\nStep 4. So the missing requirement is the third one.\nExplain it to a friend: the kids were not shuffled into groups by chance, they sorted themselves, so the groups were never comparable to begin with.","wrongs":["They clearly do go together. That is exactly what she found.","Time order is fine here. Attendance was recorded across the term and the test came afterwards. This option would be right if both had been measured on the same morning.","","This is the correlation read as causation mistake. Self-selected groups can never support a causal claim on their own."],"trap":"Many students pick \"no temporal order\" out of habit. Read the timeline in the stem first: here it is spelled out, so the weak link is self-selection.","tri":"Symptom: one study, and a question about which causal requirement fails. -&gt; Diagnosis: criteria for a causal claim, applied. -&gt; Treatment: test each requirement in turn and stop at the one that fails. Voluntary participation almost always kills requirement 3."},{"n":3,"q":"Haruka Ito compares exam marks of students who did and did not join a new tutoring scheme and reports p = .03. A colleague asks her what that number means. Which statement is correct?","o":["There is a 3 per cent chance that the tutoring scheme has no effect.","If the tutoring scheme truly had no effect, the chance of getting a difference at least as large as hers is 3 per cent.","There is a 97 per cent chance that the tutoring scheme works.","The tutoring scheme raised marks by 3 per cent."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"pvalue_mean","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. A p-value is quoted and you are asked what it means. The correct option always starts from the assumption that the null is true and then talks about the DATA. Any option that puts a probability on the hypothesis is wrong.","plain":"In plain words: a p-value answers \"how surprising are these data IF nothing is going on?\". It never answers \"how likely is it that nothing is going on?\".","work":"Step 1. Write down what p is: p = P(data this extreme or more extreme | H0 is true).\nStep 2. Note the direction of the conditioning. H0 is the GIVEN, the data are the uncertain part.\nStep 3. Scan the options for that shape. Only one conditions on \"if the scheme truly had no effect\".\nStep 4. That is the answer.\nMemory hook: the p-value is \"data given hypothesis\", never \"hypothesis given data\". The arrow points one way only.","wrongs":["This flips the conditioning round. It puts the probability on the hypothesis, which a p-value never does.","","This is 1 minus p read as a probability that the effect is real. Same flipped conditioning, same error.","This confuses the p-value with the size of the effect. A p-value is not a difference in marks or a percentage change."],"trap":"The tempting wrong answer is always \"there is a 3 per cent chance the null is true\". Catch it by asking: what is assumed, and what is being counted? The hypothesis is assumed; the data are counted.","tri":"Symptom: a p-value is given and four sentences interpret it. -&gt; Diagnosis: meaning of a p-value. -&gt; Treatment: find the option of the form \"assuming H0 is true, the probability of data this extreme or more extreme is p\". Reject every option that assigns a probability to a hypothesis or that reads p as an effect size."},{"n":4,"q":"Markus Lehner compares reaction times of 2,000 left-handed and 2,000 right-handed adults. The two group means differ by 4 milliseconds and he reports p = .001. Which one of the following statements about his result is FALSE?","o":["The difference he found would be very unusual if the two groups really had the same mean.","With this sample size, even a tiny difference can reach a very small p-value.","A small p-value says nothing about whether a 4 millisecond difference matters in practice.","Because p is so small, the difference between the two groups must be large."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"pvalue_mean","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. \"Which statement is FALSE\" plus a very large sample and a tiny difference. The examiner is testing whether you know that a p-value is not an effect size.","plain":"In plain words: three of these sentences are true things about p-values and one is the usual myth. Find the myth.","work":"Step 1. Check each true-looking statement.\nStep 2. \"Very unusual if the groups had the same mean\" is just the definition of a small p-value. True.\nStep 3. \"With this n even a tiny difference reaches a small p\" is true, because the standard error shrinks as n grows, so even 4 ms becomes many standard errors away from zero.\nStep 4. \"A small p says nothing about practical importance\" is true. Statistical significance and practical significance are different questions.\nStep 5. \"p is small so the difference must be large\" is false. The difference is 4 ms, which is tiny. It is small p because n is huge.\nExplain it to a friend: with 4,000 people, a measuring tape this precise can detect a hair. Detecting it does not make the hair thick.","wrongs":["True, and it is simply what a small p-value means.","True. A larger n gives a smaller standard error, so small differences become significant.","True. Significance and importance are separate judgements.",""],"trap":"Students read \"highly significant\" as \"big effect\". Always look at the actual difference and the sample size before judging size.","tri":"Symptom: a large n, a small effect, a tiny p, and \"which is false\". -&gt; Diagnosis: meaning of a p-value, significance against importance. -&gt; Treatment: remember that p depends on the effect AND on n. Any sentence that turns a small p into a big effect is the false one."},{"n":5,"q":"Adaeze Okonkwo tests whether a short mindfulness exercise lowers exam anxiety. She uses 24 students per group and finds a difference in the expected direction with p = .21, so she does not reject the null. Afterwards she works out that her study had a power of .42 for the effect she expected. What was the probability that her study would miss an effect of that size, and what may she conclude? (probability to 2 decimals)","o":["0.58, and she may not conclude the exercise has no effect, only that this study could not detect one.","0.42, and she may conclude the exercise has no effect.","0.05, and she may conclude the exercise has no effect because p is above alpha.","0.21, and she may conclude the exercise has no effect because that is the probability of a Type II error."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"power","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. A non-significant result plus a power figure. That pairing is almost always a Type II error question. Look for \"did not reject\", \"small sample\", \"power of\".","plain":"In plain words: power is the chance of SPOTTING a real effect. So the chance of missing it is whatever is left over. And a miss is not proof that there is nothing there.","work":"Step 1. Power is the probability of rejecting H0 when H0 is false, so power = 0.42.\nStep 2. A Type II error is failing to reject H0 when H0 is false. Its probability is beta.\nStep 3. beta = 1 minus power = 1 minus 0.42 = 0.58.\nStep 4. With a 58 per cent chance of missing a real effect, a non-significant result is weak information. The honest conclusion is \"this study could not detect an effect\".\nMemory hook: power plus beta always make 1. If you can only see 42 per cent of the room, you miss 58 per cent of it.","wrongs":["","0.42 is the power itself, not the miss rate. The second half is also wrong: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.","0.05 is alpha, the Type I error rate. That is the chance of a false alarm, not the chance of a miss.","0.21 is the p-value. A p-value is not the probability of a Type II error. Mixing those two up is the most common slip in this question type."],"trap":"Writing \"there is no effect\" after a non-significant test. With power of .42 the study was more likely to miss a real effect than to find it.","tri":"Symptom: a non-significant result with low power. -&gt; Diagnosis: Type II error and power. -&gt; Treatment: beta = 1 minus power. Then state the conclusion as \"not enough evidence\", never as \"no effect\"."},{"n":6,"q":"Markus Lehner measures six different wellbeing outcomes in a trial of a new app. One of the six reaches p = .04 and the other five do not. In his paper he presents that one outcome as the hypothesis he had planned to test and does not mention the other five. What is this, and what practice would have prevented it?","o":["Data fabrication, prevented by storing the raw data in a repository.","An honest exploratory analysis, prevented by nothing because reporting the strongest result is standard.","A sampling error, prevented by drawing a random sample.","Selective reporting, prevented by pre-registering the hypothesis and the outcome measures before collecting data."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"ethics","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. Many outcomes measured, one reported, and the stem says it was presented as the planned one. That is the fingerprint of selective reporting. Related cues: dropping participants after seeing the result, rounding p = .053 down.","plain":"In plain words: he fished until something turned up and then pretended he had been aiming at it all along. The question wants the name of that and the fix.","work":"Step 1. Ask what he did. He tested six outcomes and reported one.\nStep 2. Ask what he claimed. That the one he reported was the planned test. That claim is untrue.\nStep 3. Name the misconduct. Inventing numbers would be fabrication; changing numbers would be falsification; hiding analyses and reframing an exploratory finding as confirmatory is selective reporting.\nStep 4. Name the fix. Pre-registration: write down the hypothesis, the primary outcome and the analysis plan in a public register before data collection, so the record shows what was planned.\nStep 5. Note why it matters. With six tests at .05 the chance of at least one false alarm is far above 5 per cent, so the reported p = .04 is misleading.\nExplain it to a friend: it is like firing six arrows, then painting the target round the one that landed well.","wrongs":["Fabrication means inventing data that were never collected. His data are real. Open data is good practice but it is not the fix for a hidden analysis plan.","Exploratory analysis is fine when you LABEL it exploratory. Presenting it as the planned confirmatory test is the problem.","Nothing here is about how the sample was drawn.",""],"trap":"Thinking that only invented numbers count as misconduct. Hiding the analyses you ran is also a serious integrity breach, because it makes the p-value you do report uninterpretable.","tri":"Symptom: several analyses run, only the significant one reported as planned. -&gt; Diagnosis: research integrity, selective reporting. -&gt; Treatment: name it as selective reporting and name pre-registration as the safeguard. Pre-registration fixes the hypothesis and the outcomes in public BEFORE the data arrive."},{"n":7,"q":"Elin Bergqvist draws a random sample of 900 adults from the Swedish population register and randomly assigns half of them to a twelve-week online exercise programme and half to a waiting list. Mood scores at the end are higher in the programme group. Which inferences are justified?","o":["Only a causal inference about the programme. The sample tells her nothing about the population.","Only a generalisation to Swedish adults. Mood is self-reported, so no causal claim is possible.","Both a generalisation to Swedish adults and a causal claim that the programme raised mood.","Neither, because an online programme cannot be studied experimentally."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"inference_type","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The stem describes HOW people got into the study and HOW they got into the groups. Two separate words to hunt for: random SAMPLING and random ASSIGNMENT. They buy two different things.","plain":"In plain words: random sampling lets you talk about the wider population; random assignment lets you talk about cause. Here she did both, so she gets both.","work":"Step 1. How were people selected? A random sample from the national register. That supports a statistical inference from sample to population, so the result generalises to Swedish adults.\nStep 2. How were conditions allocated? Randomly, to programme or waiting list. That makes the groups comparable at the start, so a difference afterwards can be attributed to the programme. That is a causal inference.\nStep 3. Both random procedures are present, so both inferences are justified.\nMemory hook: SAMPLING is about WHO, so it buys generalisation. ASSIGNMENT is about WHAT HAPPENS TO THEM, so it buys cause.","wrongs":["Random sampling from the population register is exactly what licenses a statement about the population.","Self-report makes a measure noisier, it does not remove the licence to make a causal claim from a randomised experiment.","","Online programmes are randomised all the time. Nothing in the design blocks either inference."],"trap":"Treating random sampling and random assignment as one idea. In the exam the two are deliberately separated: a study can have one, the other, both or neither.","tri":"Symptom: a study described with the words \"random sample\" and \"randomly assigned\". -&gt; Diagnosis: statistical inference against causal inference. -&gt; Treatment: random sampling gives generalisation. Random assignment gives causation. Check which of the two words appears and award only what was earned."},{"n":8,"q":"Pietro Ferraro is planning a study on how background noise affects proofreading accuracy and is choosing between two designs. Consider these two statements.\nI. A within-subject design usually needs fewer participants than a between-subject design to reach the same power.\nII. In a between-subject design each participant is measured under every condition.\nWhich is true?","o":["Both statements are true.","Statement I is true, statement II is false.","Statement I is false, statement II is true.","Both statements are false."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"design_statements","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. Two numbered sentences and the four-way true/false grid. Do not read them as a pair. Judge statement I on its own, write T or F next to it, then judge statement II on its own, then pick the matching row.","plain":"In plain words: decide separately whether each sentence is true, then match your two answers to the option that says the same.","work":"Step 1. Statement I. In a within-subject design each person is their own control, so the differences between people are removed from the error term. Less error means more power for the same number of people, which is why fewer people are needed. TRUE.\nStep 2. Statement II. Being measured under every condition is the definition of a WITHIN-subject design. In a between-subject design each person sits in exactly one condition. The statement has the two designs swapped. FALSE.\nStep 3. I true, II false, so the second row.\nMemory hook: withIN means the comparison happens INSIDE one person. BETWEEN means it happens BETWEEN different people.","wrongs":["Statement II is false, so not both.","","This has it exactly the wrong way round on both counts.","Statement I is true, so not both false."],"trap":"Statement I sounds like an advantage and statement II sounds like a textbook definition, so students tick \"both true\". Statement II is the definition of the OTHER design.","tri":"Symptom: \"I. ... II. ... Which is true?\" with the both/one/other/neither shape. -&gt; Diagnosis: a two-statement design item. -&gt; Treatment: mark each statement T or F independently, then read off the row. Never let a correct statement I tempt you into option a."},{"n":9,"q":"Giulia Rossi runs a twelve-week reading programme in an Italian secondary school. She tests everyone before the programme and tests again at the end. Her summary of the two testing moments is printed below. She reports that the programme raised the mean score from 48.2 to 53.9. Which threat to the validity of that conclusion does this table point to most directly?","o":["Instrumentation, because the reading test may have been scored differently the second time.","History, because something else may have happened in the school during the twelve weeks.","Attrition, because the pupils who left had lower pretest scores, so the rise can come from who stayed rather than from the programme.","Maturation, because the pupils simply got older and better at reading over twelve weeks."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"validity_threats","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. A pretest and posttest table where the two n values differ is the printed signature of attrition. Scan the n column first. Equal n with a changed measure points to instrumentation; equal n with time passing points to maturation.","plain":"In plain words: 28 pupils disappeared between the two tests, and they were the weakest ones. So the average could have gone up even if the programme did nothing.","work":"Step 1. Read the n column. 80 pupils at pretest, 52 at posttest. So 28 left.\nStep 2. Read the pretest means. Those who stayed averaged 53.9 at posttest, but the leavers had averaged only 37.6 at pretest.\nStep 3. Work out what that does. Removing 28 weak readers raises the average by itself. The whole-group pretest mean of 48.2 includes them, the posttest mean of 53.9 does not.\nStep 4. So the comparison is between two different groups of pupils. That is attrition, also called subject mortality.\nStep 5. The fix is to compare pretest and posttest only within the 52 who completed both.\nExplain it to a friend: if the slowest runners quit halfway, the average finish time improves without anyone running faster.","wrongs":["Instrumentation would need a change in the test or in how it was scored. Nothing in the table suggests that, and the table does show something else clearly.","History is a real worry in any pre-post design, but the table gives no evidence about outside events. It does give direct evidence about dropout.","","Maturation is also possible over twelve weeks, but the table does not show it. The table shows unequal n values and a lower pretest mean among leavers."],"trap":"All four threats are plausible in a pre-post school study, so students pick their favourite. The question asks which one the TABLE shows. Let the numbers decide: unequal n means attrition.","tri":"Symptom: pre and post means with different sample sizes and a hint about who dropped out. -&gt; Diagnosis: attrition (dropout). -&gt; Treatment: compare the n values, then compare the pretest scores of leavers and stayers. If the leavers differ systematically, the posttest mean is not comparable to the pretest mean.","out":{"cap":"Reading programme: the two testing moments","h":["Group","n","Mean reading score"],"r":[["All pupils at pretest","80","48.2"],["Completed the posttest","52","53.9"],["Left after the pretest only","28","37.6"]]}},{"n":10,"q":"Pablo Serrano finds that Spanish companies which offer height-adjustable desks report fewer back complaints than companies which do not. He also notes that the companies with such desks were founded much more recently and have a considerably younger workforce. What role does the age of the workforce play in his finding?","o":["It is a confounding variable, because it offers an alternative explanation for the difference in back complaints.","It is a mediator, because it shows the mechanism by which the desks reduce back complaints.","It is irrelevant, because the difference in back complaints is statistically significant.","It is measurement error, because age was not measured as precisely as the back complaints."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"confound","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The stem gives you a link and then volunteers a third variable that differs between the groups and also plausibly causes the outcome. That is the exam handing you the confounder. Your job is to name it.","plain":"In plain words: younger staff have fewer back problems anyway. So the desks may be getting credit for the age of the staff.","work":"Step 1. Check arrow one: is workforce age related to having the desks? Yes, the newer companies have both.\nStep 2. Check arrow two: could workforce age cause fewer back complaints by itself? Yes, back problems rise with age.\nStep 3. Both arrows hold, so age sits underneath the whole link and provides a rival explanation. That is a confounder.\nStep 4. The fix is to compare companies with similar workforce ages, or better, to assign desks at random.\nMemory hook: a confounder is a variable with TWO arrows out of it, one to the supposed cause and one to the outcome.","wrongs":["","A mediator sits BETWEEN the cause and the effect, on the causal path: desks change something, which changes back complaints. Workforce age is not caused by the desks, it was there first, so it cannot be on the path.","Significance tells you the link is unlikely to be chance. It says nothing about whether the link is causal. This is the classic significance-as-proof mistake.","Measurement error is about imprecise measuring of a variable, not about a rival explanation for a difference."],"trap":"Confusing a confounder with a mediator. Ask which came first. A variable that existed before the supposed cause cannot be a mediator.","tri":"Symptom: an observed link plus a third variable linked to BOTH the supposed cause and the outcome. -&gt; Diagnosis: confounding variable. -&gt; Treatment: check the two arrows. Does the third variable differ between the groups, and could it cause the outcome on its own? Two yes answers means confounder."}]},{"id":"t1b","name":"Foundations, design and ethics - Set B","short":"Foundations, design and ethics - Set B","blurb":"Ten more questions on the same ideas, in different shapes: pick the design that earns a causal claim, read a table and name the threat, spot the integrity breach, and say what you may and may not conclude.","theme":1,"icon":"🧭","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Mette Lund screens all 400 pupils in a Danish school on a maths test, selects the 30 who scored lowest, gives them four weeks of extra coaching and tests them again. Their mean score is clearly higher the second time. Which threat gives the strongest alternative explanation for that rise?","o":["Attrition, because weaker pupils tend to drop out of extra coaching.","Regression to the mean, because a group picked for extreme low scores will tend to score closer to average on a second test even without coaching.","Instrumentation, because the second maths test was a different form.","History, because the national curriculum changed during the four weeks."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"validity_threats","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The words \"selected the lowest scorers\" or \"the highest scorers\", or \"extreme group\", followed by a retest, are the fingerprint of regression to the mean. Nothing else in the stem needs to be unusual.","plain":"In plain words: the very worst score of the day is partly bad luck. On a second try the bad luck does not repeat, so the score goes up by itself.","work":"Step 1. Why were these 30 chosen? Because they had the lowest scores on one occasion.\nStep 2. What makes a score the lowest on one occasion? Genuinely weak maths, plus bad luck on the day: a misread item, a bad night, a lucky guess that did not come off.\nStep 3. The weak maths persists. The bad luck does not. So at the retest the average moves up towards the middle even with no coaching at all.\nStep 4. So the rise cannot be credited to the coaching. The fix is to randomly split the 30 lowest scorers into a coached and an uncoached half and compare them.\nExplain it to a friend: if you pick the 30 unluckiest coin tossers out of 400, they will all look luckier next round. Nothing changed about them.","wrongs":["Nothing in the stem says anyone left the study, and all 30 were retested.","","The stem does not mention a different test form. Instrumentation needs a change in the measuring instrument.","The stem does not mention any outside event. History needs one."],"trap":"Students see \"extra coaching worked\" and look for a confounder in the teaching. The threat here is created by the SELECTION rule itself, before any teaching happened.","tri":"Symptom: a group chosen because their scores were extreme, then measured again. -&gt; Diagnosis: regression to the mean. -&gt; Treatment: say that extreme scores contain a chunk of chance, that chance does not repeat, so the second measurement drifts towards the average. Then say the fix: a randomly assigned control group of equally low scorers."},{"n":2,"q":"Rohan Iyer compares Indian hospitals that use a new digital triage system with hospitals that do not, and finds that the hospitals using the new system have higher death rates among emergency admissions. The hospitals using the system are also the large regional trauma centres, which receive the most severely injured patients. How is the severity of the patients best described here?","o":["As a mediator, because the triage system works through patient severity.","As a moderator, because the effect of the system is stronger in severe cases.","As random error, because severity varies unpredictably between hospitals.","As a confounding variable, because it is tied to which hospitals use the system and it raises death rates by itself."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"confound","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The stem shows a surprising link in the \"wrong\" direction, then explains that the groups differ on a third variable. Whenever a third variable predicts both group membership and the outcome, it is a confounder.","plain":"In plain words: the big trauma centres get the sickest patients. Of course they have more deaths. The triage system is being blamed for the patients it serves.","work":"Step 1. Arrow one, severity to group membership: the trauma centres are the ones with the system, and they get the severe cases. So severity and system use go together.\nStep 2. Arrow two, severity to outcome: severely injured patients die more often whatever the triage system. So severity causes the outcome on its own.\nStep 3. Both arrows hold, so severity is a confounder and it can produce the whole pattern.\nStep 4. It even reverses the apparent direction. A helpful system can look harmful when it is used where patients are sickest.\nMemory hook: before you believe a surprising link, ask who ended up in which group and why.","wrongs":["A mediator lies on the causal path from the system to the deaths. Patient severity is fixed before the patient arrives, so the system cannot have caused it.","A moderator changes the SIZE of an effect across levels of a third variable. The stem does not report different effects in different severity groups, it reports that the groups of hospitals differ in severity.","Random error averages out and does not line up systematically with group membership. Here the difference is systematic: trauma centres have both.",""],"trap":"Confounders can make a helpful treatment look harmful. Do not assume the confound always inflates the effect; it can also flip its sign.","tri":"Symptom: a third variable that differs between groups and causes the outcome. -&gt; Diagnosis: confounding variable. -&gt; Treatment: test the two arrows. Does severity differ between system and non-system hospitals? Yes. Does severity cause deaths by itself? Yes. Confounder. Fix: compare like with like, for example only within trauma centres."},{"n":3,"q":"Emre Yildiz wants to be able to write that a ten-minute walk break in the middle of the working day causes better afternoon concentration in Turkish office staff. Which of these studies would allow him to write that sentence?","o":["A survey of 5,000 office staff asking about their walking habits and their afternoon concentration on the same day.","An observational study in which walkers and non-walkers are matched on age, sex and job type, and then compared.","A study in which willing staff are randomly allocated to take a ten-minute walk break or to stay at their desk, with concentration tested each afternoon afterwards.","A study following the same staff for a year and showing that weeks with more walking are also weeks with better concentration."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"causal_criteria","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. Four designs and a causal sentence to defend. Hunt for the words \"randomly allocated\" or \"randomly assigned\". Only those words rule out the confounders you have not thought of.","plain":"In plain words: which of these four designs actually earns the word \"causes\"? Only the one where chance, not the person, decides who walks.","work":"Step 1. Option with the survey. Both variables measured at the same moment, so no time order, and walkers differ from non-walkers in many ways. Fails two requirements.\nStep 2. Option with matching. It handles age, sex and job type, which are the confounders he thought of. It does nothing about sleep, fitness, caffeine or motivation. Matching can only control what you measure.\nStep 3. Option with random allocation. Covariation will be visible in the comparison, the walk comes before the concentration test so time order holds, and random allocation makes the two groups comparable on everything, measured or not. All three requirements met.\nStep 4. Option with the year of follow-up. Time order is better, but weeks with more walking may also be less stressful or less busy weeks. Alternatives remain open.\nMemory hook: matching controls the confounders you can name. Randomising controls the ones you cannot.","wrongs":["A big sample and a cross-sectional survey: no time order and no control of confounders. Size does not help.","Matching only removes the variables you matched on. Unmeasured differences such as fitness or sleep remain.","","A within-person longitudinal pattern fixes time order but still leaves alternative explanations such as workload open."],"trap":"Believing that careful matching is as good as randomising. It is not, because it cannot touch the confounders nobody measured.","tri":"Symptom: a causal claim plus a choice between designs. -&gt; Diagnosis: criteria for a causal claim. -&gt; Treatment: for each design check covariation, time order, and ruling out alternatives. Only random assignment delivers the third one against unknown confounders."},{"n":4,"q":"Noah Tremblay tests whether a Canadian school breakfast scheme changes attendance and obtains p = .08, using an alpha of .05. Which conclusion is correct?","o":["There is not enough evidence to reject the null hypothesis, and this does not show that the scheme has no effect.","The null hypothesis is accepted, so the scheme has been shown to have no effect on attendance.","The result is significant, because .08 is close enough to .05 to count as a trend.","There is an 8 per cent probability that the null hypothesis is true."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"pvalue_mean","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. A p-value above alpha, and four conclusions. The right one always says \"not enough evidence to reject\" and stops there. Options that prove the null, rescue the result as a trend, or put a probability on a hypothesis are all wrong.","plain":"In plain words: p = .08 is above .05, so the data are not surprising enough. That means you failed to find something, not that there is nothing to find.","work":"Step 1. Compare. p = .08, alpha = .05, so p is greater than alpha.\nStep 2. Decision. Do not reject H0.\nStep 3. Wording. \"Do not reject\" means the data were consistent with H0, not that H0 is true. A real effect may have been missed, especially if power was low.\nStep 4. Pick the option that says exactly that and claims nothing more.\nMemory hook: a court that fails to convict has not proved innocence.","wrongs":["","This is the \"accept H0\" mistake. You never accept a null hypothesis, you only fail to reject it.","This is a one-sided rescue attempt dressed as a trend. The decision rule was set at .05 before the data; .08 is above it.","This flips the conditioning. The p-value is the probability of data like these GIVEN H0, not the probability of H0 given the data."],"trap":"Softening a non-significant result into \"a trend towards significance\". Examiners put that option in deliberately.","tri":"Symptom: p above alpha, asked for the conclusion. -&gt; Diagnosis: meaning of a p-value and of failing to reject. -&gt; Treatment: compare p with alpha, then phrase the conclusion as \"do not reject H0, not enough evidence\". Never \"accept H0\", never \"proven\"."},{"n":5,"q":"Mette Lund plans a follow-up study with 18 pupils per group and calculates that it would have a power of only .31 for the effect she expects. She wants to make it more likely that a real effect will show up. Which change would do that?","o":["Lowering alpha from .05 to .01 so the test is stricter.","Increasing the number of pupils in each group.","Adding four more outcome measures so there are more chances of a significant result.","Keeping only the most typical pupils so the groups are cleaner."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"power","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The stem gives a low power figure and asks what to change. This is the power-levers question. The levers are: bigger n, bigger real effect, less measurement noise, and a less strict alpha. Sample size is the one you actually control.","plain":"In plain words: power is the chance of spotting a real effect. She wants that chance higher. Which of these four actually raises it?","work":"Step 1. Power is the probability of rejecting H0 when H0 is false. It rises when the test statistic gets further from zero relative to its standard error.\nStep 2. Increase n. The standard error contains n in the denominator, so a bigger n shrinks the standard error, pushes the test statistic further out, and raises power. This is the correct lever.\nStep 3. Lower alpha. A stricter cut-off makes rejection harder, so power goes DOWN. Alpha and power pull in opposite directions.\nStep 4. More outcome measures. That raises the chance of at least one false alarm, which is inflated Type I error, not power. It is also the road to selective reporting.\nStep 5. Keeping only typical pupils. That narrows the range in the sample, and a restricted sample is less representative. It does not raise power for the effect she expects and it damages generalisation.\nMemory hook: power has four dials: n, effect size, noise, alpha. Only n is properly yours to turn.","wrongs":["Lowering alpha reduces false alarms but makes a real effect harder to detect, so power falls.","","Running more tests inflates the family-wise error rate. That is more chances of a FALSE positive, which is not the same as power.","Throwing out atypical pupils shrinks the sample and restricts its range. It does not increase power and it harms generalisation."],"trap":"Reading \"stricter test\" as \"better test\". A stricter alpha buys fewer false alarms at the price of more missed effects.","tri":"Symptom: low power and \"which change would help\". -&gt; Diagnosis: power. -&gt; Treatment: run through the levers. n up means standard error down means power up. Alpha down means power down. More tests means more false alarms, not more power."},{"n":6,"q":"Wouter Claes supervises a Belgian survey of 100 employees. His assistant cannot reach 12 of them, so he enters plausible values for those 12 based on the group average and records them in the data file as if they had been collected. Nothing about this appears in the report. How should this be judged?","o":["Acceptable, because the invented values sit close to the group average and barely change the results.","A minor administrative slip, because the 12 people were genuinely sampled.","Data fabrication, which is research misconduct even though the values look plausible.","Acceptable, because replacing missing values with the mean is a recognised technique."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"ethics","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. Look for numbers that were never measured being written into a data file, or for values recorded \"as if\" collected. That is fabrication, and the tempting excuse is always that the numbers are plausible or small.","plain":"In plain words: he made up 12 people's answers and did not say so. That is inventing data, and inventing data is misconduct however reasonable the numbers look.","work":"Step 1. Were the values measured? No. They were invented.\nStep 2. Were they presented as measured? Yes. They were entered as if collected and never flagged.\nStep 3. Name it. Making up data that were never collected is fabrication, one of the three core forms of research misconduct along with falsification and plagiarism.\nStep 4. Deal with the plausibility defence. Plausible invented data are still invented, and they also shrink the apparent variability and the standard errors, so the results look more precise than they are.\nStep 5. The honest route: record the 12 as missing data, report the response rate of 88 out of 100, and if values are imputed, say so and say how.\nExplain it to a friend: a convincing forgery is still a forgery.","wrongs":["Being close to the average is no defence. It also makes the data look less variable than reality, which falsely narrows the standard errors.","This is not an administrative detail. The sample was drawn honestly; the answers were not.","","Mean imputation is a real technique, but only when it is declared and described. Here the values were recorded as if collected and nothing was reported, which is what makes it fabrication."],"trap":"Imputation and fabrication can look identical in the data file. What separates them is DISCLOSURE. Undeclared filled-in values are fabricated values.","tri":"Symptom: values entered that were never collected. -&gt; Diagnosis: fabrication (if invented) or falsification (if real values were altered). -&gt; Treatment: say it is misconduct, say why plausibility is no defence, and name the honest alternative: report the 12 as missing, or use a declared imputation method and describe it."},{"n":7,"q":"Hannah Whitlock recruits 150 first-year psychology students at her own Australian university by posting a notice in the department, and randomly assigns them to revise either by rereading or by self-testing. The self-testing group scores higher on a later test. What may she conclude?","o":["Nothing at all, because the students volunteered rather than being randomly sampled.","That self-testing works better for Australian adults in general, because the groups were formed at random.","That self-testing and test performance are associated, but not that one caused the other.","That self-testing caused the higher scores in this study, but not that the finding generalises to all adults."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"inference_type","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. A convenience sample plus random assignment. Two words, two licences. \"Volunteers\" or \"posted a notice\" kills generalisation. \"Randomly assigned\" grants causation. Award exactly what each word earns.","plain":"In plain words: she can say the revision method made the difference, because chance decided who did what. She cannot say it holds for everyone, because her people were self-selected students.","work":"Step 1. How was the sample obtained? By a notice in one department, so volunteers from one narrow group. That is a convenience sample, so no statistical inference to a wider population.\nStep 2. How were conditions allocated? Randomly. So the two groups were comparable at the start, and the later difference can be attributed to the revision method. Causal inference yes, within these participants.\nStep 3. Combine: causation yes, generalisation no.\nMemory hook: assignment buys CAUSE, sampling buys REACH. She bought cause only.","wrongs":["Too harsh. The random assignment still licenses a causal claim about these participants.","This gives her the generalisation she did not earn. Random assignment does not make a volunteer sample representative.","Too cautious. Random assignment is exactly what lets you go beyond association to causation.",""],"trap":"Students treat \"random\" as one magic word. Always read which thing was randomised: the selection of people, or the allocation to conditions.","tri":"Symptom: a non-random sample with random assignment to conditions. -&gt; Diagnosis: statistical inference against causal inference. -&gt; Treatment: random ASSIGNMENT gives causation inside the study. Random SAMPLING gives generalisation. Here only the first is present, so causation yes, generalisation no."},{"n":8,"q":"Nikos Pappas is deciding how to run a study on whether two kinds of music affect typing speed. Consider these two statements.\nI. Carry-over effects are a risk when every participant does both conditions.\nII. Differences between participants add noise when each participant does only one condition.\nWhich is true?","o":["Both statements are true.","Statement I is true, statement II is false.","Statement I is false, statement II is true.","Both statements are false."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"design_statements","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. The two-statement grid again. Each statement here describes the DOWNSIDE of one design. Translate the wording into the design name first: \"every participant does both\" is within-subject, \"only one condition\" is between-subject.","plain":"In plain words: each sentence names the main drawback of one of the two designs. Check each one separately and see whether the drawback belongs where the sentence puts it.","work":"Step 1. Statement I. \"Every participant does both conditions\" is a within-subject design. Its known weakness is carry-over: practice, fatigue or the first condition colouring the second. TRUE. This is also why counterbalancing exists.\nStep 2. Statement II. \"Each participant does only one condition\" is a between-subject design. There the differences between people land in the error term, which inflates the noise and lowers power. TRUE.\nStep 3. Both true, so the first row.\nMemory hook: within-subject trades person noise for carry-over. Between-subject trades carry-over for person noise. Each design pays for its advantage.","wrongs":["","Statement II is a correct description of the between-subject design, so it is not false.","Statement I is a correct description of the within-subject design, so it is not false.","Both statements are standard and correct."],"trap":"Assuming at least one statement must be false. In this format \"both true\" is a real answer, and it is the one students skip over.","tri":"Symptom: \"I. ... II. ... Which is true?\" -&gt; Diagnosis: a two-statement design item. -&gt; Treatment: translate each sentence into within or between, mark it T or F on its own, then read off the row. Do not assume the examiner always plants one false statement."},{"n":9,"q":"Nikos Pappas measures the wellbeing of the same 90 Greek employees twice, once in January and once in June. The details of the two measurement moments are printed below. He reports that mean wellbeing rose from 5.1 to 5.8 over the half year. Which threat does this table point to most directly?","o":["Attrition, because employees left the company between the two measurements.","Instrumentation, because the measuring instrument was not the same on the two occasions.","Regression to the mean, because the January scores were unusually low.","Maturation, because employees naturally become more satisfied over time."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"validity_threats","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. Scan the table for what CHANGED between the two moments. If the n stayed the same but the scale, the item count, the rater or the scoring changed, the answer is instrumentation.","plain":"In plain words: he used a 10-item scale in January and a 5-item version in June. The ruler changed, so you cannot tell whether wellbeing rose or just the measurement did.","work":"Step 1. Read the n column. 90 at both moments, so nobody dropped out. Attrition is ruled out by the table itself.\nStep 2. Read the instrument column. January used the full 10-item scale, June used a shortened 5-item version. The measuring instrument changed.\nStep 3. What does that do? A shorter scale can have a different mean level even if wellbeing is unchanged, because it drops some items and keeps others. The increase from 5.1 to 5.8 may be an artefact of the instrument.\nStep 4. So the threat is instrumentation.\nStep 5. Fix: administer the same 10-item scale on both occasions, or at minimum report both versions at one moment to check they agree.\nExplain it to a friend: if you weigh yourself on a different set of scales in June, the number tells you about the scales, not about you.","wrongs":["The table shows n = 90 at both moments, so nobody left. The table itself rules this out.","","Regression to the mean needs a group selected BECAUSE their scores were extreme. These are all 90 employees, not a selected low-scoring subgroup.","Maturation is possible over six months, but the table gives no evidence for it. It gives direct evidence of a changed instrument."],"trap":"Reading the means and ignoring the columns beside them. In table questions the answer is nearly always in the column everyone skips.","tri":"Symptom: a pre-post table where the instrument differs across the two moments. -&gt; Diagnosis: instrumentation. -&gt; Treatment: check what is constant and what changed. Equal n rules out attrition. A changed scale means instrumentation. Fix: use the identical instrument at both moments.","out":{"cap":"Wellbeing measured twice in the same 90 employees","h":["Moment","Instrument used","n","Mean wellbeing"],"r":[["January","10-item wellbeing scale","90","5.1"],["June","shortened 5-item version","90","5.8"]]}},{"n":10,"q":"Hannah Whitlock checks whether students who use an online study tool pass a statistics course more often. Overall, 66 per cent of tool users pass against 52 per cent of non-users. She then splits the same students by their grade in the previous year, as shown below. What may she conclude?","o":["That the tool raises the pass rate by about 14 percentage points, since the overall comparison is the relevant one.","That prior grade is a mediator, because the tool works by improving prior grades.","That prior grade is a confounder: within each prior-grade group the pass rate is the same, so the overall gap comes from who uses the tool.","That the tool helps students with high prior grades but harms students with low prior grades."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"confound","theme":1,"spot":"HINT. An overall difference that VANISHES once you split the data by a third variable. That disappearing act is the printed proof of a confounder. If the gap instead changed size and direction across the groups, you would be looking at an interaction.","plain":"In plain words: inside each prior-grade group the tool makes no difference at all. The tool users just happened to be the stronger students already.","work":"Step 1. Read the within-group pass rates. High prior grade: 96 of 120 tool users pass, which is 80 per cent, and 32 of 40 non-users pass, also 80 per cent. Equal.\nStep 2. Low prior grade: 36 of 80 tool users pass, which is 45 per cent, and 72 of 160 non-users pass, also 45 per cent. Equal again.\nStep 3. So within each level of prior grade the tool makes no difference whatsoever.\nStep 4. Why is there an overall gap then? Look at the mix. Of the 200 tool users, 120 are high-prior-grade students, which is 60 per cent. Of the 200 non-users, only 40 are, which is 20 per cent.\nStep 5. Check the overall figures: tool users 96 + 36 = 132 of 200 = 66 per cent; non-users 32 + 72 = 104 of 200 = 52 per cent. The 14 point gap is entirely produced by the different mix of prior grades.\nStep 6. So prior grade is a confounder and the overall comparison is misleading.\nMemory hook: if the difference dies inside the subgroups, it was never about the treatment.","wrongs":["This takes the overall gap at face value. The split table shows the gap is zero inside every subgroup, so there is nothing for the tool to have caused.","A mediator comes AFTER the cause, on the path from it to the outcome. Last year's grade existed before any tool use, so it cannot be a mediator.","","This would be an interaction, and it is not what the table shows. The pass rate for users and non-users is identical within each prior-grade group: 80 against 80, and 45 against 45."],"trap":"Trusting the overall percentages. Always read the split table: a difference that is equal in every subgroup is not a difference caused by the grouping variable.","tri":"Symptom: a group difference overall that disappears within levels of a third variable. -&gt; Diagnosis: confounding variable. -&gt; Treatment: compare the within-group percentages. If they are equal, the third variable explains the overall gap. Then check the mix: the groups must differ in how many fall in each level.","out":{"cap":"Passes, split by grade in the previous year","h":["Prior grade","Tool users: pass / total","Non-users: pass / total"],"r":[["High","96 / 120","32 / 40"],["Low","36 / 80","72 / 160"],["All students","132 / 200","104 / 200"]]}}]},{"id":"t2a","name":"Proportions - Set A","short":"Proportions - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on proportions: intervals for one proportion and for a difference between two, the margin of error and its half-width trap, the standard error of a difference, the probability that a sample proportion passes some value, and the two conditions you must check first. z* values: 1.645 for 90 per cent, 1.96 for 95 per cent, 2.576 for 99 per cent.","theme":2,"icon":"📊","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Ingrid Solheim surveys cyclists in two Norwegian cities and compares the proportion who wear a helmet. Her 95 per cent interval for the proportion in Bergen minus the proportion in Trondheim runs from -0.021 to 0.113. What may she conclude?","o":["Bergen has a higher helmet rate than Trondheim, because most of the interval is above zero.","The two cities have exactly the same helmet rate, because zero is inside the interval.","There is no evidence of a difference in helmet rates between the two cities, because zero is a plausible value for the difference.","Trondheim has a higher helmet rate than Bergen, because the interval reaches below zero."],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"ci2p_interpret","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. An interval for p1 minus p2 and the word \"conclude\". Do one thing first: ask whether zero lies between the two endpoints. Zero inside means no evidence of a difference. Zero outside means evidence of a difference, in the direction of the sign.","plain":"In plain words: the interval covers a range of possible differences. Zero is one of them, so \"no difference at all\" is still on the table.","work":"Step 1. Write the interval: -0.021 to 0.113, for Bergen minus Trondheim.\nStep 2. Does it contain zero? The lower endpoint is negative and the upper endpoint is positive, so yes, zero lies inside.\nStep 3. Translate. Plausible values for the difference run from Trondheim being 2.1 points higher to Bergen being 11.3 points higher. Both directions, and no difference, are all consistent with the data.\nStep 4. So the correct statement is that there is no evidence of a difference. This matches a two-sided test at the 5 per cent level failing to reject.\nMemory hook: zero inside means \"could be nothing\". It does not mean \"is nothing\".","wrongs":["\"Most of the interval\" is not a rule. The whole interval has to clear zero before you may claim a direction.","This is the \"do not reject means accept\" mistake in interval form. Zero being plausible is not the same as zero being true; the interval also allows an 11 point difference.","","Same error as the first option, in the other direction. Part of the interval lying below zero proves nothing."],"trap":"Picking a winner because more of the interval sits on one side. Direction is only claimable when BOTH endpoints have the same sign.","tri":"Symptom: a confidence interval for a DIFFERENCE between two proportions, plus \"what may she conclude\". -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting a two-proportion interval. -&gt; Treatment: check the sign of the endpoints. One negative and one positive means zero is inside means no evidence of a difference. Never upgrade that to \"the proportions are equal\"."},{"n":2,"q":"Diego Alvarez compares the proportion of customers who complete an online purchase under a new checkout page and under the old one. His 95 per cent interval for the new page minus the old page runs from 0.038 to 0.182. Which one of these statements is FALSE?","o":["The new page has a higher completion rate, because the whole interval lies above zero.","A two-sided test of no difference at the 5 per cent level would be rejected.","The best single estimate of the difference is 0.110.","The data show that 95 per cent of customers complete more purchases on the new page."],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"ci2p_interpret","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. \"Which statement is FALSE\" about a difference interval. The planted false option usually misuses the 95 per cent, attaching it to customers or to the proportion itself rather than to the long-run behaviour of the method.","plain":"In plain words: three of these are fair readings of the interval and one abuses the words \"95 per cent\". Find the abuse.","work":"Step 1. Zero and the interval. Both endpoints, 0.038 and 0.182, are positive, so zero is outside and the new page is better. True.\nStep 2. The matching test. A 95 per cent interval that excludes zero corresponds to rejecting the two-sided null of no difference at alpha = .05. True.\nStep 3. The centre. (0.038 + 0.182) / 2 = 0.220 / 2 = 0.110. That is the observed difference in proportions, the point estimate. True.\nStep 4. The last statement. The 95 per cent describes the procedure: if the study were repeated many times, 95 per cent of such intervals would capture the true difference. It says nothing about 95 per cent of customers. FALSE, so that is the answer.\nExplain it to a friend: the 95 per cent is a property of the net, not of the fish.","wrongs":["True. Both endpoints are positive, so the direction is established.","True. An interval that excludes zero and a rejected two-sided test at the same level always agree.","True. The centre of the interval is the observed difference: (0.038 + 0.182) / 2 = 0.110.",""],"trap":"Letting \"95 per cent\" migrate onto the people in the study. The confidence level describes the method across repeated samples, not a share of customers and not the probability that this particular interval is right.","tri":"Symptom: a difference interval and four statements, one false. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting a two-proportion interval. -&gt; Treatment: check three things: does the interval clear zero (direction), does the matching two-sided test reject, and is the centre the point estimate. Then reject any option that treats 95 per cent as a statement about people."},{"n":3,"q":"Amara Diallo surveys commuters in two Senegalese districts about a new bus line. In the first district 78 of 120 commuters say they would use it, and in the second district 81 of 150 say they would. Build a 95 per cent confidence interval for the difference between the two proportions, first district minus second. (3 decimals)","o":["0.012 to 0.208","-0.007 to 0.227","0.028 to 0.192","-0.044 to 0.264"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"ci2p_calc","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Two sample sizes, two counts, and a confidence level. No mention of a hypothesis. That means an interval for a difference, which uses the UNPOOLED standard error and z*.","plain":"In plain words: find each proportion, subtract them, work out how much that difference might wobble, and stretch 1.96 wobbles either side.","work":"Step 1. The two proportions. p1 = 78 / 120 = 0.650. p2 = 81 / 150 = 0.540.\nStep 2. The difference. 0.650 - 0.540 = 0.110.\nStep 3. The two variance pieces. 0.650 x 0.350 / 120 = 0.2275 / 120 = 0.00189583. 0.540 x 0.460 / 150 = 0.2484 / 150 = 0.001656.\nStep 4. Add them and take the square root. 0.00189583 + 0.001656 = 0.00355183, and the square root is 0.05960. That is the standard error.\nStep 5. The margin. z* for 95 per cent is 1.96, so 1.96 x 0.05960 = 0.11681.\nStep 6. The interval. 0.110 - 0.117 = -0.007 and 0.110 + 0.117 = 0.227, so the interval is -0.007 to 0.227.\nStep 7. Sense check. Zero is inside, so there is no evidence of a difference between the two districts.\nMemory hook: variances add, standard errors do not. Square, add, then square root.","wrongs":["This uses z* = 1.645, the 90 per cent value, instead of 1.96: 1.645 x 0.05960 = 0.098, giving 0.012 to 0.208. Check which confidence level the question asked for.","","This divides both variance pieces by the combined sample size of 270 instead of by their own n values: the square root of (0.2275 + 0.2484) / 270 = 0.04198, and 1.96 x 0.04198 = 0.082, giving 0.028 to 0.192. Each group keeps its own n.","This uses z* = 2.576, the 99 per cent value: 2.576 x 0.05960 = 0.154, giving -0.044 to 0.264."],"trap":"Using the pooled standard error here. Pooling belongs to a hypothesis TEST of equal proportions, where the null says the two proportions are the same. An INTERVAL assumes nothing, so each group keeps its own p-hat.","tri":"Symptom: two counts out of two sample sizes, a confidence level, no hypothesis. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for a difference in proportions. -&gt; Treatment: difference plus or minus z* times the unpooled standard error, where that standard error is the square root of p1(1-p1)/n1 + p2(1-p2)/n2. Add the two variances, never the two standard errors."},{"n":4,"q":"Kofi Mensah asks a random sample of 200 residents of a Ghanaian town whether they have used the new library in the past month. 46 of them say yes. Give a 90 per cent confidence interval for the proportion of all residents who have used it. (3 decimals)","o":["0.181 to 0.279","0.172 to 0.288","0.153 to 0.307","0.227 to 0.233"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"ci1p","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. One count out of one sample size, a confidence level, and the words \"proportion of all\". One proportion, one interval, z* not t*. Note the confidence level carefully: it decides which z* you use.","plain":"In plain words: 23 out of every 100 people in the sample used the library. The interval says where the true town-wide figure plausibly sits.","work":"Step 1. The sample proportion. p-hat = 46 / 200 = 0.230.\nStep 2. Check the conditions. Successes = 46 and failures = 200 - 46 = 154. Both are at least 10, so the Normal approximation is fine.\nStep 3. The standard error. 0.230 x 0.770 = 0.1771. Divide by 200 to get 0.00088550. The square root is 0.029757.\nStep 4. The z* value. 90 per cent means 5 per cent in each tail, so z* = 1.645.\nStep 5. The margin of error. 1.645 x 0.029757 = 0.048950, which rounds to 0.049.\nStep 6. The interval. 0.230 - 0.049 = 0.181 and 0.230 + 0.049 = 0.279, so 0.181 to 0.279.\nMemory hook: 90 per cent is the NARROWEST of the three, because 1.645 is the smallest z*. Less confidence buys a tighter interval.","wrongs":["","This uses z* = 1.96, the 95 per cent value: 1.96 x 0.029757 = 0.058, giving 0.172 to 0.288. The question asked for 90 per cent.","This uses z* = 2.576, the 99 per cent value: 2.576 x 0.029757 = 0.077, giving 0.153 to 0.307.","This divides by n instead of by the square root of n: the square root of 0.1771 is 0.4209, and 0.4209 / 200 = 0.002104, so the margin becomes 0.003. The n sits INSIDE the square root."],"trap":"Reaching for t*. A proportion is a count divided by n, so its standard error is built from p-hat itself and there are no degrees of freedom. t* belongs to means with an unknown s.","tri":"Symptom: a single count out of a single n, a confidence level, no second group. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for one proportion. -&gt; Treatment: p-hat plus or minus z* times the square root of p-hat(1 - p-hat)/n. Use z*, never t*, because a proportion has no sample standard deviation to estimate."},{"n":5,"q":"Mei Chen records how 320 staff at a Singapore office travel to work. Her summary is printed below. Using the cycling category, give a 99 per cent confidence interval for the proportion of all staff at this office who cycle. (3 decimals)","o":["0.343 to 0.432","0.334 to 0.441","0.317 to 0.458","0.388 to 0.458"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"ci1p","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. A frequency table and a question about ONE category. Turn the category into a proportion out of the grand total, then it is an ordinary one-proportion interval. The other rows are there to be collapsed into \"not cycling\".","plain":"In plain words: 124 of the 320 staff cycle. Everything that is not cycling counts as a failure. Then build the usual interval around 124 out of 320.","work":"Step 1. Check the total. 124 + 118 + 51 + 27 = 320, which matches, so nothing is missing.\nStep 2. The sample proportion. p-hat = 124 / 320 = 0.3875.\nStep 3. Conditions. Successes = 124, failures = 320 - 124 = 196. Both well above 10.\nStep 4. The standard error. 0.3875 x 0.6125 = 0.23734. Divide by 320 to get 0.00074170. The square root is 0.027234.\nStep 5. The z* value. 99 per cent means 0.5 per cent in each tail, so z* = 2.576.\nStep 6. The margin. 2.576 x 0.027234 = 0.070155, which rounds to 0.070.\nStep 7. The interval. 0.3875 - 0.070 = 0.317 and 0.3875 + 0.070 = 0.458, so 0.317 to 0.458.\nMemory hook: more confidence costs width. 99 per cent is always the widest of the three.","wrongs":["This uses z* = 1.645, the 90 per cent value: 1.645 x 0.027234 = 0.045, giving 0.343 to 0.432.","This uses z* = 1.96, the 95 per cent value: 1.96 x 0.027234 = 0.053, giving 0.334 to 0.441.","","This leaves the lower end at p-hat itself and only adds the margin at the top: 0.3875 rounds to 0.388, and 0.3875 + 0.070 = 0.458. The interval must be CENTRED on p-hat, so the margin is subtracted at the bottom as well as added at the top."],"trap":"Dividing by the count in some other category, or by the three other categories added together. The denominator is always the grand total, here 320.","tri":"Symptom: a printed frequency table plus \"the proportion who ...\". -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for one proportion, read from a table. -&gt; Treatment: the category count over the grand total gives p-hat, everything else is the failure count, then p-hat plus or minus z* times the square root of p-hat(1-p-hat)/n.","out":{"cap":"Mode of travel to work, 320 staff","h":["Mode","Number of staff"],"r":[["Cycling","124"],["Public transport","118"],["Walking","51"],["Car","27"]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Ravi Shankar reports that a 95 per cent confidence interval for the proportion of Indian smartphone users who have enabled two-factor authentication runs from 0.412 to 0.488. A journalist asks him for the margin of error of his estimate. What should he give? (3 decimals)","o":["0.076","0.038","0.450","0.019"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"moe","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. An interval is printed and the margin of error is asked for. The margin is HALF the total width, because the interval is the estimate plus or minus the margin. If the question instead gives the margin and asks for the interval, you go the other way.","plain":"In plain words: the interval is \"the estimate, give or take something\". That \"something\" is the margin, and it is half the distance from one end to the other.","work":"Step 1. The width of the whole interval. 0.488 - 0.412 = 0.076.\nStep 2. The interval runs the margin BELOW the estimate and the margin ABOVE it, so the width is two margins.\nStep 3. The margin. 0.076 / 2 = 0.038.\nStep 4. Check it. The centre is (0.412 + 0.488) / 2 = 0.450, and 0.450 plus or minus 0.038 does give 0.412 to 0.488. Correct.\nMemory hook: the interval is a whole pizza, the margin is half of it. Width in, halve it, margin out.","wrongs":["0.076 is the full WIDTH of the interval, which is two margins, not one. This is the single most common mistake in this question type.","","0.450 is the centre of the interval, that is the sample proportion itself, not the margin.","0.019 is the width divided by four. This happens if you halve twice, once to get the margin and once again by habit."],"trap":"Reporting the whole width as the margin. Say it out loud: the estimate plus or minus the margin. Plus AND minus, so the margin is half the span.","tri":"Symptom: an interval given, the margin of error asked for (or the reverse). -&gt; Diagnosis: margin of error. -&gt; Treatment: margin = (upper - lower) / 2. Centre = (upper + lower) / 2. Write the interval as centre plus or minus margin and read off whichever is wanted."},{"n":7,"q":"A quality manager at a Polish bakery knows from long experience that 40 per cent of loaves come out of the oven slightly under the target weight. Tomasz Nowak takes a random sample of 150 loaves. What is the probability that more than 46 per cent of the loaves in his sample are under target? (4 decimals)","o":["0.0668","0.9332","0.1336","0.4513"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"phat_dist","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. A population proportion is GIVEN, a sample size is given, and you are asked for a probability about the sample proportion. No interval, no hypothesis. That means a z-score on p-hat using the sampling distribution of p-hat.","plain":"In plain words: the long-run rate is 40 per cent. In a batch of only 150 loaves the percentage will bounce around. How often does it bounce above 46 per cent?","work":"Step 1. Centre. The mean of the sampling distribution of p-hat is p = 0.40.\nStep 2. Spread. p(1-p)/n = 0.40 x 0.60 / 150 = 0.24 / 150 = 0.0016. The square root is 0.040. Note that this uses the KNOWN p, not a p-hat.\nStep 3. Conditions. np = 150 x 0.40 = 60 and n(1-p) = 150 x 0.60 = 90. Both are at least 10, so the Normal approximation holds.\nStep 4. The z-score. z = (0.46 - 0.40) / 0.040 = 0.06 / 0.040 = 1.50.\nStep 5. The direction. The question says MORE than 46 per cent, so the upper tail. P(Z &gt; 1.50) = 1 - 0.9332 = 0.0668.\nStep 6. Sense check. 46 per cent is well above the 40 per cent average, so the answer should be small. 0.0668 is small. Good.\nMemory hook: the standard error of p-hat uses p, n and nothing else. One sample, one z, one tail.","wrongs":["","0.9332 is the LOWER tail, P(Z &lt; 1.50). That is the probability of fewer than 46 per cent. Read the direction word in the stem.","0.1336 is 2 x 0.0668, the two-sided answer. The question asks only about the upper side, so do not double.","This drops the division by n: using the square root of 0.24 = 0.4899 as the standard deviation gives z = 0.06 / 0.4899 = 0.12 and a probability of 0.4513. The n must be inside the square root."],"trap":"Forgetting that p-hat has a much smaller spread than a single loaf does. Dividing by n is what turns the spread of one item into the spread of an average of 150.","tri":"Symptom: a known p, a sample size n, and \"the probability that the sample proportion is above or below some value\". -&gt; Diagnosis: sampling distribution of p-hat. -&gt; Treatment: the mean of p-hat is p, its standard deviation is the square root of p(1-p)/n. Then z = (value - p) / that, and read the Normal table in the direction the question asks."},{"n":8,"q":"Lotte van Dijk surveys two Dutch departments about a proposed change to working hours. Her counts are printed below. She wants to build a confidence interval for the difference between the two proportions in favour. What is the standard error she should use? (4 decimals)","o":["0.0640","0.0319","0.0126","0.0453"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"se_2p","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Two groups, two counts, and the words \"standard error\" with \"for the difference\" and \"confidence interval\". That is the UNPOOLED standard error. The word interval is the signal: pooling belongs only to a test of equal proportions.","plain":"In plain words: each department has its own wobble. To get the wobble of the difference you add the two squared wobbles and take the square root. You never add the wobbles themselves.","work":"Step 1. The proportions. p1 = 96 / 250 = 0.384. p2 = 66 / 200 = 0.330.\nStep 2. First variance piece. 0.384 x 0.616 = 0.236544. Divide by 250 to get 0.00094618.\nStep 3. Second variance piece. 0.330 x 0.670 = 0.2211. Divide by 200 to get 0.00110550.\nStep 4. Add. 0.00094618 + 0.00110550 = 0.00205168.\nStep 5. Square root. The square root of 0.00205168 is 0.045295, so the standard error is 0.0453.\nStep 6. Sense check. The answer should be bigger than either single standard error (0.0308 and 0.0332) but much smaller than their sum. 0.0453 fits.\nMemory hook: VARIANCES add. Work in squares, add, then take the square root at the very end.","wrongs":["This adds the two standard errors instead of the two variances: 0.0308 + 0.0332 = 0.0640. Adding standard errors always overstates the spread.","This divides both variance pieces by the combined n of 450: the square root of (0.236544 + 0.2211) / 450 = 0.0319. Each group must be divided by its own n.","This subtracts the two variance pieces instead of adding them: the square root of (0.00110550 - 0.00094618) = 0.0126. Variances of independent quantities always add, even for a difference.",""],"trap":"Using the pooled standard error out of habit. Pooling assumes the two proportions are equal, which is a null hypothesis, not something an interval assumes.","tri":"Symptom: two proportions and the standard error of their difference, in the context of an interval. -&gt; Diagnosis: unpooled standard error for a difference in proportions. -&gt; Treatment: square root of p1(1-p1)/n1 + p2(1-p2)/n2. Each group keeps its own p-hat and its own n.","out":{"cap":"Staff in favour of the change, by department","h":["Department","In favour","Total asked"],"r":[["Claims","96","250"],["Underwriting","66","200"]]}},{"n":9,"q":"Four students each collect a small sample and want to build a confidence interval for a proportion using the Normal approximation. Their results are: Anna, 7 successes out of 50; Bram, 32 out of 80; Cato, 15 out of 30; Daan, 25 out of 120. For which sample is the Normal approximation NOT appropriate?","o":["Bram, because 32 successes out of 80 is too close to half.","Anna, because she has only 7 successes, which is fewer than 10.","Cato, because 30 is too small a sample whatever the counts are.","Daan, because his proportion of about 0.21 is too far from 0.50."],"a":1,"pct":30,"typ":"p_conditions","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Several small samples and the question of whether you are allowed to proceed. Do not look at the proportions. Count the SUCCESSES and the FAILURES in each sample and compare both to 10.","plain":"In plain words: before you may use the Normal curve for a proportion you need at least 10 people in the yes pile and at least 10 in the no pile. Count them.","work":"Step 1. Anna. Successes 7, failures 50 - 7 = 43. Successes are below 10, so she FAILS the condition.\nStep 2. Bram. Successes 32, failures 80 - 32 = 48. Both above 10, so he passes.\nStep 3. Cato. Successes 15, failures 30 - 15 = 15. Both above 10, so he passes even though n is only 30.\nStep 4. Daan. Successes 25, failures 120 - 25 = 95. Both above 10, so he passes.\nStep 5. Only Anna fails, so Anna is the answer.\nMemory hook: ten in each pile. Ten yes, ten no. That is the whole rule.","wrongs":["Being close to half is ideal, not a problem. The Normal approximation works best near 0.50.","","There is no minimum n in the rule. Cato has 15 and 15, so he comfortably passes with n = 30.","Being far from 0.50 is only a problem when it drives one of the two counts below 10. Daan has 25 and 95, so he is fine."],"trap":"Judging the condition by the sample size or by how extreme the proportion looks. The rule is about the two COUNTS, and a small n can pass while a large n fails.","tri":"Symptom: small samples and \"is the approximation appropriate\". -&gt; Diagnosis: conditions for using the Normal approximation for a proportion. -&gt; Treatment: successes at least 10 AND failures at least 10. Both must pass. Nothing about being near 0.50 and no fixed minimum n matters on its own."},{"n":10,"q":"Ravi Shankar has built a 95 per cent confidence interval for a proportion from a sample of 400 people. He would like a narrower interval. Which change would make the interval narrower?","o":["Raising the confidence level from 95 per cent to 99 per cent.","Reducing the sample from 400 to 200 people.","Keeping the confidence level at 95 per cent and increasing the sample to 1,600 people.","Reporting the margin of error instead of the interval."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"ci_width","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Any question about an interval becoming wider or narrower is a question about two things only: z* (set by the confidence level) and the standard error (driven by n). Everything else in the options is decoration.","plain":"In plain words: the interval is estimate plus or minus z* times standard error. To shrink it, shrink one of those two. More people shrinks the standard error. Less confidence shrinks z*.","work":"Step 1. Write the width. Width = 2 x z* x the square root of p-hat(1-p-hat)/n.\nStep 2. The n lever. n sits under a square root in the denominator, so a larger n gives a smaller standard error and a narrower interval. Going from 400 to 1,600 multiplies n by 4, and the square root of 4 is 2, so the width is HALVED.\nStep 3. The confidence lever. Going from 95 to 99 per cent raises z* from 1.96 to 2.576, which makes the interval WIDER, not narrower.\nStep 4. Cutting the sample to 200 halves n, which multiplies the width by the square root of 2, about 1.41. Wider.\nStep 5. So only the fourfold increase in n narrows the interval.\nMemory hook: to halve the width, quadruple the sample. Precision is expensive.","wrongs":["A higher confidence level needs a bigger z*, so the interval gets wider. More certainty always costs width.","A smaller sample gives a bigger standard error, so the interval gets wider.","","This changes how the result is written down, not how precise it is. The margin is half the interval, so reporting it does not make the estimate any sharper."],"trap":"Thinking a higher confidence level means a better, tighter interval. It is the opposite: 99 per cent is wider than 95 per cent, which is wider than 90 per cent.","tri":"Symptom: \"what makes the interval wider or narrower\". -&gt; Diagnosis: width of a confidence interval. -&gt; Treatment: width = 2 x z* x standard error, and the standard error has the square root of n underneath. So n up means narrower, confidence up means wider. Quadrupling n halves the width."}]},{"id":"t2b","name":"Proportions - Set B","short":"Proportions - Set B","blurb":"Ten more proportion questions in new contexts and new shapes: read a difference interval both ways, build a 99 per cent interval, work a margin of error forwards and backwards, find a tail probability for a sample proportion, and check the conditions from a printed table. z* values: 1.645 for 90 per cent, 1.96 for 95 per cent, 2.576 for 99 per cent.","theme":2,"icon":"📊","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Sanna Virtanen compares the proportion of patients who miss an appointment at a clinic that sends text reminders and at a clinic that does not. Her 95 per cent interval for the reminder clinic minus the no-reminder clinic runs from -0.142 to -0.008. What may she conclude?","o":["The reminder clinic has a lower missed-appointment rate, because the whole interval lies below zero.","There is no evidence of a difference, because the interval is close to zero at the upper end.","The reminder clinic has a higher missed-appointment rate, because the interval runs in the negative direction.","Nothing, because a confidence interval for a difference cannot show direction."],"a":0,"pct":75,"typ":"ci2p_interpret","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. An interval for p1 minus p2 and \"what may you conclude\". First check whether zero is inside. Then, if it is not, use the SIGN to say which group is higher: the subtraction order in the stem tells you which is which.","plain":"In plain words: every plausible value of the difference is negative, so the reminder clinic is below the other one. Reminders go with fewer missed appointments.","work":"Step 1. The interval is -0.142 to -0.008 for reminders minus no reminders.\nStep 2. Does it contain zero? No. Both endpoints are negative, so zero is outside.\nStep 3. So there IS evidence of a difference, and a two-sided test at the 5 per cent level would reject no difference.\nStep 4. Which direction? The quantity is reminders minus no reminders, and it is negative, so the reminder clinic has the SMALLER proportion of missed appointments.\nStep 5. In words: the reminder clinic misses between 0.8 and 14.2 percentage points fewer appointments.\nMemory hook: all negative means the first-named group is lower. All positive means the first-named group is higher.","wrongs":["","The upper endpoint is -0.008, which is below zero, not at it. Zero is outside the interval, so there IS evidence of a difference. \"Close to zero\" is not a rule.","This reads the sign backwards. Reminders minus no reminders being negative means the reminder clinic is LOWER.","A difference interval shows direction precisely when zero is outside it, which is the case here."],"trap":"Getting the sign the wrong way round. Before deciding who wins, write down the subtraction order the stem used, then attach the sign to it.","tri":"Symptom: a difference interval that does not contain zero. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting a two-proportion interval. -&gt; Treatment: both endpoints the same sign means there is evidence of a difference. A negative interval for p1 minus p2 means p1 is the smaller one. Read the subtraction order from the stem before you name a winner."},{"n":2,"q":"Jonas Weber reports two intervals from the same survey of German and Austrian students. The 95 per cent interval for the difference in the proportion who own a bicycle runs from 0.015 to 0.121, and the 99 per cent interval for the same difference runs from -0.002 to 0.138. Which one of these statements is FALSE?","o":["At the 5 per cent level there is evidence of a difference, because the 95 per cent interval excludes zero.","At the 1 per cent level there is no evidence of a difference, because the 99 per cent interval includes zero.","The two intervals contradict each other, so at least one of them must have been computed wrongly.","Both intervals are centred on the same estimated difference of 0.068."],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"ci2p_interpret","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Two intervals for the SAME difference at two confidence levels, and \"which is false\". The planted false option usually claims the two disagree. They never do: they are the same estimate with different z* values.","plain":"In plain words: the wider 99 per cent interval includes zero and the narrower 95 per cent one does not. That is normal, not a contradiction. The evidence is just strong enough at 5 per cent and not at 1 per cent.","work":"Step 1. Centre of the 95 per cent interval. (0.015 + 0.121) / 2 = 0.136 / 2 = 0.068.\nStep 2. Centre of the 99 per cent interval. (-0.002 + 0.138) / 2 = 0.136 / 2 = 0.068. Same centre, as it must be.\nStep 3. Widths. The 95 per cent margin is 0.121 - 0.068 = 0.053. The 99 per cent margin is 0.138 - 0.068 = 0.070. The ratio 0.070 / 0.053 is about 1.32, and 2.576 / 1.96 is about 1.31. The same standard error of 0.053 / 1.96 = 0.0270 gives both margins, so the two intervals agree.\nStep 4. So the narrower interval excluding zero while the wider one includes it is exactly what you expect when the p-value lies between .01 and .05. No contradiction.\nStep 5. The false statement is therefore the one claiming a contradiction.\nMemory hook: same centre, different stretch. The level changes the stretch only.","wrongs":["True. A 95 per cent interval excluding zero matches rejecting at alpha = .05.","True. A 99 per cent interval including zero matches not rejecting at alpha = .01.","","True. Both centres are (lower + upper) / 2 = 0.068, which is the observed difference in proportions."],"trap":"Treating \"significant at 5 per cent but not at 1 per cent\" as an error. It simply means the p-value sits between .01 and .05, which is extremely common.","tri":"Symptom: the same difference reported at two confidence levels, one interval containing zero and one not. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting a two-proportion interval and how level relates to width. -&gt; Treatment: a higher level gives a wider interval around the SAME centre. Check the centres match, then read each interval against its own alpha."},{"n":3,"q":"Leila Haddad asks employees in two Moroccan branches whether they would move to a four-day week. In the first branch 51 of 150 say yes, and in the second branch 36 of 180 say yes. Build a 99 per cent confidence interval for the difference between the two proportions, first branch minus second. (3 decimals)","o":["0.060 to 0.220","0.044 to 0.236","-0.036 to 0.316","0.014 to 0.266"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"ci2p_calc","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Two counts, two sample sizes, a confidence level and no hypothesis. An interval for a difference, unpooled standard error, z* from the stated level. Here the level is 99 per cent, so z* = 2.576.","plain":"In plain words: work out each yes rate, subtract, work out how much that difference might wobble, and stretch 2.576 wobbles either side.","work":"Step 1. The two proportions. p1 = 51 / 150 = 0.340. p2 = 36 / 180 = 0.200.\nStep 2. The difference. 0.340 - 0.200 = 0.140.\nStep 3. Conditions. Branch one: 51 successes and 99 failures. Branch two: 36 successes and 144 failures. All four counts are at least 10, so proceed.\nStep 4. The variance pieces. 0.340 x 0.660 / 150 = 0.2244 / 150 = 0.001496. 0.200 x 0.800 / 180 = 0.16 / 180 = 0.00088889.\nStep 5. Add and take the square root. 0.001496 + 0.00088889 = 0.00238489, and the square root is 0.048835. That is the standard error.\nStep 6. The margin. z* for 99 per cent is 2.576, so 2.576 x 0.048835 = 0.125800, which rounds to 0.126.\nStep 7. The interval. 0.140 - 0.126 = 0.014 and 0.140 + 0.126 = 0.266, so 0.014 to 0.266.\nStep 8. Sense check. Zero is just outside, so even at 99 per cent there is evidence that the first branch is keener.\nMemory hook: 99 per cent is the widest. If your 99 per cent interval is narrower than a 95 per cent one, you used the wrong z*.","wrongs":["This uses z* = 1.645, the 90 per cent value: 1.645 x 0.048835 = 0.080, giving 0.060 to 0.220.","This uses z* = 1.96, the 95 per cent value: 1.96 x 0.048835 = 0.096, giving 0.044 to 0.236. The question asked for 99 per cent.","This adds the two standard errors instead of the two variances: 0.038678 + 0.029814 = 0.068492, and 2.576 x 0.068492 = 0.176, giving -0.036 to 0.316. Variances add, standard errors do not.",""],"trap":"Grabbing 1.96 automatically. Read the confidence level in the stem every single time: 1.645, 1.96 and 2.576 are easy to swap and the examiner builds a distractor from each.","tri":"Symptom: two counts out of two n values, a confidence level, no hypothesis. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for a difference in proportions. -&gt; Treatment: (p1 - p2) plus or minus z* times the square root of p1(1-p1)/n1 + p2(1-p2)/n2. Add the variances, not the standard errors."},{"n":4,"q":"Pedro Oliveira asks a random sample of 260 Portuguese drivers whether they have ever used a car-sharing service. 117 say yes. Give a 95 per cent confidence interval for the proportion of all Portuguese drivers who have used one. (3 decimals)","o":["0.399 to 0.501","0.390 to 0.510","0.371 to 0.529","0.419 to 0.481"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"ci1p","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. One count, one n, one confidence level, and a question about the whole population. One-proportion interval with z*. Check which level is asked for before you pick z*.","plain":"In plain words: 117 out of 260 drivers, which is 45 per cent, have used one. The interval says where the true national figure plausibly lies.","work":"Step 1. The sample proportion. p-hat = 117 / 260 = 0.450.\nStep 2. Conditions. Successes = 117, failures = 260 - 117 = 143. Both well above 10.\nStep 3. The standard error. 0.450 x 0.550 = 0.2475. Divide by 260 to get 0.00095192. The square root is 0.030853.\nStep 4. The z* value. 95 per cent means 2.5 per cent in each tail, so z* = 1.96.\nStep 5. The margin. 1.96 x 0.030853 = 0.060472, which rounds to 0.060.\nStep 6. The interval. 0.450 - 0.060 = 0.390 and 0.450 + 0.060 = 0.510, so 0.390 to 0.510.\nStep 7. Note that zero is irrelevant here. For ONE proportion the value worth checking is usually 0.50, and 0.50 is inside this interval, so you could not claim that most drivers have used one.\nMemory hook: 1.96 for 95. The two numbers almost rhyme.","wrongs":["This uses z* = 1.645, the 90 per cent value: 1.645 x 0.030853 = 0.051, giving 0.399 to 0.501.","","This uses z* = 2.576, the 99 per cent value: 2.576 x 0.030853 = 0.079, giving 0.371 to 0.529.","This uses the standard error itself as the margin, with no z* at all: 0.450 plus or minus 0.0309 gives 0.419 to 0.481. The standard error must be multiplied by z*."],"trap":"Forgetting to multiply the standard error by z*. The standard error is the size of a typical wobble; the margin is z* wobbles.","tri":"Symptom: one count out of one n plus a confidence level. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for one proportion. -&gt; Treatment: p-hat plus or minus z* times the square root of p-hat(1 - p-hat)/n, with z* read off the confidence level. Always z*, never t*, for a proportion."},{"n":5,"q":"A Finnish broadcaster polls 400 viewers and finds that 62 per cent recognise a new programme logo. Sanna Virtanen is asked for the margin of error of that 62 per cent at the 95 per cent level. What should she report? (4 decimals)","o":["0.0399","0.0951","0.0476","0.0625"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"moe","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. A proportion, an n, a confidence level, and the margin of error asked for directly. You need only the margin, not the interval: z* times the standard error, and then stop.","plain":"In plain words: how far either side of 62 per cent does the interval reach? That one-sided distance is the margin.","work":"Step 1. The standard error. 0.62 x 0.38 = 0.2356. Divide by 400 to get 0.000589. The square root is 0.024269.\nStep 2. The z* value. 95 per cent, so z* = 1.96.\nStep 3. The margin. 1.96 x 0.024269 = 0.047568, which rounds to 0.0476.\nStep 4. Check what it means. The interval would be 0.62 plus or minus 0.0476, that is 0.572 to 0.668. The margin is ONE of those two gaps, not both.\nMemory hook: margin = z* x standard error. One multiplication, then stop.","wrongs":["This uses z* = 1.645, the 90 per cent value: 1.645 x 0.024269 = 0.0399.","This is the full WIDTH of the interval, 2 x 1.96 x 0.024269 = 0.0951. The margin is half of that.","","This uses z* = 2.576, the 99 per cent value: 2.576 x 0.024269 = 0.0625."],"trap":"Doubling the margin. The margin is the one-sided distance; the width is two margins. Decide which one the question wants before you write a number down.","tri":"Symptom: p-hat, n and a confidence level, with \"margin of error\" asked for. -&gt; Diagnosis: margin of error for one proportion. -&gt; Treatment: margin = z* times the square root of p-hat(1-p-hat)/n. Do not double it and do not add it to p-hat."},{"n":6,"q":"A colleague of Pedro Oliveira publishes a confidence interval for the proportion of Portuguese households with a heat pump, running from 0.286 to 0.374. He writes underneath that the margin of error is 0.088. By how much is he wrong, that is, what is the correct margin of error? (3 decimals)","o":["0.044","0.088","0.330","0.022"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"moe","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. An interval printed, a margin quoted, and a suspicion that the quoted figure is the whole width. Whenever a margin looks like it equals the distance from one endpoint to the other, halve it.","plain":"In plain words: he reported the whole length of the interval and called it the margin. The margin is the distance from the middle to one end, so it is half that.","work":"Step 1. The width. 0.374 - 0.286 = 0.088. That is exactly the figure he published, which is the clue.\nStep 2. The interval is centre plus or minus margin, so the width is two margins.\nStep 3. The correct margin. 0.088 / 2 = 0.044.\nStep 4. Verify. Centre = (0.286 + 0.374) / 2 = 0.660 / 2 = 0.330. Then 0.330 - 0.044 = 0.286 and 0.330 + 0.044 = 0.374. The interval is rebuilt exactly, so 0.044 is right.\nMemory hook: width is the whole road, margin is half the road. He reported the road.","wrongs":["","0.088 is the full width of the interval, which is what he wrongly published. It is two margins, not one.","0.330 is the centre of the interval, that is the sample proportion, not the margin.","0.022 is a quarter of the width. This happens if you halve the width twice."],"trap":"This is the half-width trap in its purest form, and it is a favourite exam item. Always rebuild the interval from your answer: centre plus or minus margin must give back the printed endpoints.","tri":"Symptom: an interval given and a margin to recover or to correct. -&gt; Diagnosis: margin of error from an interval. -&gt; Treatment: margin = (upper - lower) / 2, and centre = (upper + lower) / 2. Check by rebuilding the interval as centre plus or minus margin."},{"n":7,"q":"Records from a large Chilean university show that 25 per cent of first-year students change their study programme. Camila Rojas takes a random sample of 300 first-year students from this year. What is the probability that fewer than 21 per cent of her sample will change programme? (4 decimals)","o":["0.4632","0.9452","0.1096","0.0548"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"phat_dist","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. A known population proportion, a sample size, and a probability about the sample percentage. This is a z-score on p-hat. The word \"fewer\" tells you to take the lower tail.","plain":"In plain words: the long-run rate is 25 per cent. In a sample of 300 the figure will vary. How often will it land below 21 per cent?","work":"Step 1. Centre. The sampling distribution of p-hat is centred on p = 0.25.\nStep 2. Spread. p(1-p)/n = 0.25 x 0.75 / 300 = 0.1875 / 300 = 0.000625. The square root is 0.025.\nStep 3. Conditions. np = 300 x 0.25 = 75 and n(1-p) = 300 x 0.75 = 225. Both at least 10, so the Normal approximation is fine.\nStep 4. The z-score. z = (0.21 - 0.25) / 0.025 = -0.04 / 0.025 = -1.60.\nStep 5. The tail. The question says FEWER than 21 per cent, so the lower tail. P(Z &lt; -1.60) = 0.0548.\nStep 6. Sense check. 21 per cent is below the 25 per cent average, so the answer should be well under 0.50. It is.\nMemory hook: write the z, then circle the direction word in the stem before you read the table.","wrongs":["This drops the division by n: using the square root of 0.1875 = 0.4330 as the standard deviation gives z = -0.04 / 0.4330 = -0.09 and a probability of about 0.4632. The n belongs inside the square root.","0.9452 is the upper tail, P(Z &gt; -1.60). That is the probability of MORE than 21 per cent. Check the direction word.","0.1096 is 2 x 0.0548, a two-sided answer. The question asks about one side only.",""],"trap":"Using the spread of a single student rather than the spread of the sample proportion. Dividing by n is what makes an average much more stable than one observation.","tri":"Symptom: a given p, a given n, and \"the probability that the sample proportion is below some value\". -&gt; Diagnosis: sampling distribution of p-hat. -&gt; Treatment: mean = p, standard deviation = square root of p(1-p)/n, z = (value - p) divided by that, then the tail the wording asks for."},{"n":8,"q":"Youssef Benali compares two Belgian call centres on the proportion of calls resolved at the first attempt. In centre A, 58 per cent of 180 calls are resolved first time, and in centre B, 46 per cent of 220 calls are. He is building a confidence interval for the difference. What standard error should he use? (4 decimals)","o":["0.0704","0.0498","0.0351","0.0025"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"se_2p","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. Two proportions, two sample sizes, \"standard error\" and \"confidence interval for the difference\". That is the unpooled standard error. If the stem had said \"test whether the two proportions are equal\", you would pool instead.","plain":"In plain words: each centre has its own wobble. The wobble of the difference comes from adding the two squared wobbles and then taking the square root.","work":"Step 1. First variance piece. 0.58 x 0.42 = 0.2436. Divide by 180 to get 0.00135333.\nStep 2. Second variance piece. 0.46 x 0.54 = 0.2484. Divide by 220 to get 0.00112909.\nStep 3. Add. 0.00135333 + 0.00112909 = 0.00248242.\nStep 4. Square root. The square root of 0.00248242 is 0.049824, so the standard error is 0.0498.\nStep 5. Sense check. Each single standard error is about 0.0368 and 0.0336, so the combined figure should be a little larger than either but far below their sum of 0.0704. 0.0498 fits.\nMemory hook: squares in, add, square root out. The square root comes last and only once.","wrongs":["This adds the two standard errors instead of the two variances: 0.036788 + 0.033602 = 0.0704. That always overstates the spread of a difference.","","This divides both variance pieces by the combined n of 400: the square root of (0.2436 + 0.2484) / 400 = 0.0351. Each group must be divided by its own n.","0.0025 is the sum of the two variances with the square root forgotten. A variance is in squared units, so you must take the root."],"trap":"Pooling. The word \"interval\" means you assume nothing about the two proportions, so each keeps its own p-hat. Pooling belongs only to a TEST of equal proportions.","tri":"Symptom: standard error of a difference in proportions, for an interval. -&gt; Diagnosis: unpooled standard error. -&gt; Treatment: square root of p1(1-p1)/n1 + p2(1-p2)/n2. Each group keeps its own p-hat and its own n, and the two variances are added."},{"n":9,"q":"Four research assistants each bring back a small sample, shown below, and each wants to build a confidence interval for a proportion using the Normal approximation. For which sample is the Normal approximation NOT appropriate?","o":["Sample 2, because 95 is an odd sample size to work with.","Sample 3, because the sample of 28 is far too small for any interval.","Sample 1, because it has only 8 successes, which is fewer than 10.","Sample 4, because its proportion of 0.08 is too far from 0.50."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"p_conditions","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. A table of successes and failures and a question about whether you may proceed. Do not look at n and do not look at the proportion. Read the two count columns and compare each number with 10.","plain":"In plain words: you need at least 10 in the success column AND at least 10 in the failure column. Scan both columns for anything under 10.","work":"Step 1. Sample 1. Successes 8, failures 37. The 8 is below 10, so this sample FAILS.\nStep 2. Sample 2. Successes 40, failures 55. Both above 10, so it passes.\nStep 3. Sample 3. Successes 14, failures 14. Both above 10, so it passes even though n is only 28.\nStep 4. Sample 4. Successes 12, failures 138. Both above 10, so it passes even though the proportion is only 0.08.\nStep 5. Only Sample 1 fails, so Sample 1 is the answer.\nMemory hook: ten in each pile. Sample 4 has an extreme proportion and still passes because its n is large enough to put 12 in the small pile.","wrongs":["There is nothing wrong with an odd sample size, and 40 and 55 both clear 10 comfortably.","There is no minimum n in the rule. With 14 successes and 14 failures, Sample 3 passes.","","An extreme proportion is only a problem when it pushes one count below 10. Sample 4 has 12 successes and 138 failures, so it passes."],"trap":"Judging by n or by how extreme the proportion looks. A small sample can pass (14 and 14) while a bigger one fails (8 and 37). Count the two piles.","tri":"Symptom: counts of successes and failures and \"is the approximation appropriate\". -&gt; Diagnosis: conditions for the Normal approximation for a proportion. -&gt; Treatment: successes at least 10 AND failures at least 10, in that sample. Sample size on its own and distance from 0.50 on their own prove nothing.","out":{"cap":"Successes and failures in four samples","h":["Sample","Successes","Failures","n"],"r":[["Sample 1","8","37","45"],["Sample 2","40","55","95"],["Sample 3","14","14","28"],["Sample 4","12","138","150"]]}},{"n":10,"q":"Camila Rojas has built a 90 per cent confidence interval for the difference between two proportions and finds it too narrow to be convincing to her supervisor, who wants more certainty. If she recomputes the same data at the 99 per cent level, what happens to the interval, and what does that mean?","o":["It gets wider, because z* rises from 1.645 to 2.576, so a difference that looked established at 90 per cent may no longer exclude zero.","It gets narrower, because a higher confidence level means a more precise estimate.","It stays the same width but shifts upwards, because the centre moves with the confidence level.","It gets wider, and because of that the evidence for a difference becomes stronger."],"a":0,"pct":30,"typ":"ci_width","theme":2,"spot":"HINT. A change of confidence level on the same data, and a question about the interval. Only z* changes. The centre and the standard error do not move, so the interval only stretches or shrinks around a fixed middle.","plain":"In plain words: asking for more certainty means asking for a wider net. A wider net is more likely to catch zero, so the conclusion can flip from \"there is a difference\" to \"no evidence of a difference\".","work":"Step 1. What changes. The sample proportions and n are the same, so the centre and the standard error are unchanged. Only z* changes.\nStep 2. The z* values. 90 per cent gives 1.645 and 99 per cent gives 2.576.\nStep 3. The effect. 2.576 / 1.645 = 1.566, so the margin and the width are about 1.57 times as large. The interval gets wider.\nStep 4. The consequence. A wider interval around the same centre is more likely to contain zero, so a difference that excluded zero at 90 per cent may include it at 99 per cent. That means weaker, not stronger, grounds for claiming a difference.\nStep 5. Note the trade-off. More confidence means less precision. The only way to get both is a larger sample.\nMemory hook: confidence and precision pull against each other. n is the only thing that buys you both.","wrongs":["","A higher confidence level makes the interval WIDER, which is less precise, not more.","The centre is the observed difference and does not depend on the confidence level at all. Only the stretch around it changes.","The first half is right and the second half is wrong. A wider interval makes the evidence for a difference WEAKER, because zero becomes easier to include."],"trap":"Equating a high confidence level with strong evidence. Raising the level widens the interval and makes it harder, not easier, to rule zero out.","tri":"Symptom: the confidence level changes on the same data. -&gt; Diagnosis: width of a confidence interval. -&gt; Treatment: width = 2 x z* x standard error. Only z* has changed, so compare z* values: 1.645 up to 2.576 means wider by a factor of about 1.57. The centre is unchanged."}]},{"id":"t3a","name":"Chi-square - Set A","short":"Chi-square - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on the test that works with counts. Computing the statistic from a table, the goodness-of-fit version against a claimed split, what an expected count really is, the hypotheses, the degrees of freedom, and the fact that a 2 x 2 chi-square is nothing more than a z test squared.","theme":3,"icon":"🎲","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Nadia El Amrani surveys 400 commuters in Rotterdam. For each one she records which of three districts they live in and whether they hold a season ticket, yes or no. She wants to know whether these two things go together among commuters in the city as a whole. Which pair of hypotheses is she testing?","o":["H0: the mean number of season tickets is equal in the three districts. H1: at least two of the three means differ.","H0: in the population of commuters, district and season ticket holding are independent. H1: in the population, district and season ticket holding are associated.","H0: in the population, district and season ticket holding are associated. H1: in the population, they are independent.","H0: in her sample of 400, every observed count equals its expected count. H1: in her sample, at least one observed count differs from its expected count."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_hyp","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Two things are recorded on the SAME people, both of them categories, and the question asks for the hypotheses. Look for the word \"independent\" in H0 and \"associated\" in H1, and check that both sentences talk about the POPULATION.","plain":"In plain words: she wants to know whether knowing someone's district tells you anything about their chance of holding a season ticket. H0 always says no, it tells you nothing.","work":"Step 1. Name the variables. District has three categories, season ticket has two. Both are categorical, so there are no means to compare.\nStep 2. Write H0 in the standard form: the two variables are independent in the population.\nStep 3. Write H1 as the plain opposite: the two variables are associated in the population. Note that H1 does not say which way round or how strongly.\nStep 4. Check the level. Hypotheses are always claims about the population. The sample is the evidence, never the claim.\nStep 5. Only one option has both features: independence in H0 and the population as the subject.\nMemory hook: H0 is the boring hypothesis. Nothing to see here, the two labels do not know about each other.","wrongs":["Counts, not means. There is no \"mean number of season tickets\" per person: each commuter either has one or does not. This is the ANOVA wording pasted into a chi-square question.","","The two hypotheses have been swapped. H0 must be the no-effect statement, because the whole test works by assuming no association and then seeing how odd the data look.","This writes the hypotheses about the SAMPLE. Observed counts never equal expected counts exactly, so this H0 would be false in almost every sample ever collected. Hypotheses are about the population."],"trap":"Students who have just finished the ANOVA chapter reach for means. Check the measurement first: if both variables are labels and the data are counts of people, it is a chi-square question.","tri":"Symptom: two category variables measured on one sample, and four pairs of hypotheses to choose between. -&gt; Diagnosis: hypotheses for a chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: H0 says the two variables are independent in the population, H1 says they are associated. Throw out anything that mentions means, and anything that talks about the sample instead of the population."},{"n":2,"q":"Tomas Ferreira, a pharmacist in Lisbon, gives 120 patients a reminder app and the other 120 patients the usual paper leaflet. A month later he records for each patient whether every dose was taken. His table is printed below. What is the value of the test statistic for an association between group and adherence? (2 decimals)","o":["1.35","2.71","5.41","5.54"],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"chi_calc","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. A table of COUNTS of people and a request for a number. That is the chi-square statistic. The giveaway is that nothing in the table is a mean or a percentage: every entry is a head count.","plain":"In plain words: work out how many patients would have landed in each box if the app made no difference at all, then measure how far the real counts are from those four numbers.","work":"Step 1. Read off the totals. Rows are 120 and 120, columns are 126 and 114, grand total 240.\nStep 2. Expected counts. App and \"took all doses\": 120 x 126 / 240 = 63. App and \"missed\": 120 x 114 / 240 = 57. The leaflet row has the same two expected counts, 63 and 57, because the two row totals are equal.\nStep 3. Cell by cell. (72 - 63) squared / 63 = 81 / 63 = 1.285714. (48 - 57) squared / 57 = 81 / 57 = 1.421053. (54 - 63) squared / 63 = 1.285714. (66 - 57) squared / 57 = 1.421053.\nStep 4. Add them: 1.285714 + 1.421053 + 1.285714 + 1.421053 = 5.413534.\nStep 5. Round to two decimals: 5.41. With df = (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1 this gives p = .020, so there is evidence of an association.\nExplain it to a friend: 63 is the share of the 126 successes that the app group would get if success were handed out purely in proportion to group size. They got 72, which is nine more than their fair share.","wrongs":["This divides each squared difference by the grand total 240 instead of by the expected count: 324 / 240 = 1.35. The denominator is always the expected count for that cell.","This adds up only the first row: 1.285714 + 1.421053 = 2.71. All four cells count, including the leaflet row.","","This divides by the OBSERVED count instead of the expected count: 81 / 72 + 81 / 48 + 81 / 54 + 81 / 66 = 5.54. Expected goes on the bottom, observed on the top."],"trap":"Dividing by the observed count. Say the formula out loud as \"O minus E, squared, over E\". The E is the last thing you say, so it is the thing you divide by.","tri":"Symptom: an r x c table of counts, \"what is the value of the statistic\". -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square from a table. -&gt; Treatment: for every cell, expected = row total x column total / grand total. Then add up (observed - expected) squared, divided by expected, over all cells. Never round the expected counts.","out":{"cap":"Doses taken in full, by group","h":["Group","Took all doses","Missed doses","Total"],"r":[["Reminder app","72","48","120"],["Paper leaflet","54","66","120"],["Total","126","114","240"]]}},{"n":3,"q":"Yusuf Kaya is explaining his chi-square analysis of 260 Turkish households to a first-year student. The student asks him what the numbers in his \"expected\" table actually are. Which answer is correct?","o":["They are the counts each cell would hold if the two variables were independent in the population, worked out as row total times column total divided by the grand total, and they are left unrounded.","They are the counts each cell would hold if every cell held the same number of households, that is the grand total divided by the number of cells.","They are the observed counts rounded to the nearest whole household, since a cell cannot contain part of a household.","They are the counts predicted by the alternative hypothesis, which is why a large gap between observed and expected points to an association."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_concept","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. No numbers to crunch, just \"what is an expected count\". Two things must appear in the right answer: the counts come from the NULL hypothesis of independence, and they are NOT rounded.","plain":"In plain words: the expected table is the make-believe table you would get if the two variables had nothing to do with each other. It is the yardstick, not the data.","work":"Step 1. Remember where expected counts come from. Under H0 the row variable tells you nothing about the column variable, so each row should split across the columns in the same proportions as the whole sample does.\nStep 2. Turn that into the formula. The column proportion is column total / grand total. Apply it to a row of size \"row total\" and you get row total x column total / grand total.\nStep 3. Note that this is almost never a whole number, and that is fine. It is an average over many imagined samples, not a head count of real households.\nStep 4. Note that rounding it would change the statistic, because every expected count sits in a denominator.\nStep 5. So the right answer must mention independence AND no rounding.\nMemory hook: E stands for Expected under Equality. Row share times column share, decimals and all.","wrongs":["","This is the equal-cells assumption. It would only be right if the row totals were all equal AND the column totals were all equal. Independence means each row splits in the same PROPORTIONS, not that all cells are the same size.","Expected counts are not rounded observed counts. They are computed from the margins and have nothing to do with the individual cell's observed value. Rounding them changes the statistic.","Expected counts come from H0, not H1. H1 does not say what the counts should be, it only says they are not what H0 predicts."],"trap":"Rounding the expected counts to whole people because people come in whole units. An expected count of 27.5 is correct and must be used as 27.5.","tri":"Symptom: a question about what expected counts mean rather than what they are numerically. -&gt; Diagnosis: the concept of an expected count. -&gt; Treatment: say it in one sentence: the count under H0 of independence, equal to row total x column total / grand total, kept with decimals."},{"n":4,"q":"Aino Virtanen checks the blood groups of 400 new donors at a Finnish blood bank against the national figures, which are printed beside her counts below. What is the value of the test statistic for the fit between her donors and the national figures? (2 decimals)","o":["0.44","1.79","2.54","7.16"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"gof_stat","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. ONE categorical variable, a claimed split in percentages, and counts from a single sample. There is no second variable, so there is no table of rows and columns: the expected counts come from the claimed percentages times n.","plain":"In plain words: if the donors were a perfect mirror of the country, how many would fall in each blood group? Compare those four numbers with what she actually got.","work":"Step 1. Check the total. 184 + 120 + 54 + 42 = 400. Good, and the shares add to 100 per cent.\nStep 2. Expected counts. O: 0.40 x 400 = 160. A: 0.35 x 400 = 140. B: 0.15 x 400 = 60. AB: 0.10 x 400 = 40.\nStep 3. Cell by cell. O: (184 - 160) squared / 160 = 576 / 160 = 3.6. A: (120 - 140) squared / 140 = 400 / 140 = 2.857143. B: (54 - 60) squared / 60 = 36 / 60 = 0.6. AB: (42 - 40) squared / 40 = 4 / 40 = 0.1.\nStep 4. Add them: 3.6 + 2.857143 + 0.6 + 0.1 = 7.157143.\nStep 5. Round to 7.16. With df = 4 - 1 = 3 this gives p = .067, so the donors do not differ significantly from the national split at the 5 per cent level.\nExplain it to a friend: percentages tell you where the donors SHOULD have landed. Turn them into head counts first, because the statistic only eats counts.","wrongs":["This uses the plain difference instead of the squared difference: 24 / 160 + 20 / 140 + 6 / 60 + 2 / 40 = 0.44. The gap must be squared, which is what stops the positive and negative gaps cancelling.","This works in percentages instead of counts. The observed shares are 46, 30, 13.5 and 10.5 per cent, and (46 - 40) squared / 40 + (30 - 35) squared / 35 + (13.5 - 15) squared / 15 + (10.5 - 10) squared / 10 = 1.79. Chi-square only works on counts: with percentages the sample size disappears.","This divides each squared difference by the sample size 400 instead of by the expected count: (576 + 400 + 36 + 4) / 400 = 2.54.",""],"trap":"Feeding percentages into the formula. Convert the claimed percentages to expected COUNTS first by multiplying by n, because the whole test is about how many people you have, not what share they are.","tri":"Symptom: one variable, a claimed set of proportions, observed counts. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square goodness of fit. -&gt; Treatment: expected count for each category = claimed proportion x n. Then sum (observed - expected) squared / expected across the categories. df = categories - 1.","out":{"cap":"Blood groups of 400 new donors against the national figures","h":["Blood group","National share","Donors observed"],"r":[["O","40 per cent","184"],["A","35 per cent","120"],["B","15 per cent","54"],["AB","10 per cent","42"]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Pieter De Smet records, for each of 500 Belgian job applicants, which one of four recruitment channels they used and which one of five outcome categories they ended up in. He wants to know whether channel and outcome are related. How many degrees of freedom does his test have?","o":["7","12","19","20"],"a":1,"pct":30,"typ":"chi_df","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Two categorical variables, the number of categories of each is stated, and the question asks for degrees of freedom. You never need the sample size for this. If the stem gives you n, it is a decoy.","plain":"In plain words: how many of the cells in the table can you fill in freely before the row and column totals force the rest? That count is the degrees of freedom.","work":"Step 1. Count the categories. Four channels means r = 4 rows. Five outcomes means c = 5 columns.\nStep 2. Subtract one from each: 4 - 1 = 3 and 5 - 1 = 4.\nStep 3. Multiply: 3 x 4 = 12.\nStep 4. Sanity check with the free-cells idea. The table has 20 cells. Once you know the margins, filling in a 3 by 4 block of cells fixes every remaining cell, so 12 are free.\nStep 5. The 500 applicants play no part. Sample size affects the size of the statistic, not the shape of the reference distribution.\nMemory hook: knock one off each side of the table, then multiply the two leftovers.","wrongs":["This adds instead of multiplying: (4 - 1) + (5 - 1) = 7. The formula is a product.","","This treats the 20 cells as one long list of categories and uses categories minus one, 20 - 1 = 19. That is the goodness-of-fit rule, which applies when there is only ONE variable.","This counts the cells, 4 x 5 = 20, and forgets to subtract. Every df formula subtracts something."],"trap":"Adding instead of multiplying, which gives 7 here. Write the brackets down before you touch the numbers: (r - 1)(c - 1) has no plus sign in it.","tri":"Symptom: an r x c table of counts and \"how many degrees of freedom\". -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: df = (number of rows - 1) x (number of columns - 1). Multiply, never add, and ignore n completely."},{"n":6,"q":"Mei-Ling Chow asks 240 Singapore residents, 80 in each of three age bands, whether they have ever used the city bike-share scheme. Her counts are printed below. What is the value of the test statistic for an association between age band and use of the scheme? (2 decimals)","o":["1.85","6.00","11.16","11.94"],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"chi_calc","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Same job as a 2 x 2, just more rows. Equal row totals are a gift: every row then has the SAME pair of expected counts, so you only compute them once.","plain":"In plain words: 111 of the 240 residents have used the scheme. If age made no difference, each age band of 80 should contain the same share of those 111 users. Compare that with what she found.","work":"Step 1. Totals. Each row is 80, the columns are 111 and 129, the grand total is 240.\nStep 2. Expected counts. \"Has used\": 80 x 111 / 240 = 37. \"Has not\": 80 x 129 / 240 = 43. Because all three row totals are 80, every row has expected counts 37 and 43.\nStep 3. Cell by cell for \"has used\": (48 - 37) squared / 37 = 121 / 37 = 3.270270. (36 - 37) squared / 37 = 1 / 37 = 0.027027. (27 - 37) squared / 37 = 100 / 37 = 2.702703.\nStep 4. Cell by cell for \"has not\": (32 - 43) squared / 43 = 121 / 43 = 2.813953. (44 - 43) squared / 43 = 0.023256. (53 - 43) squared / 43 = 100 / 43 = 2.325581.\nStep 5. Add all six: 3.270270 + 0.027027 + 2.702703 + 2.813953 + 0.023256 + 2.325581 = 11.162791, so 11.16.\nStep 6. With df = 2 this gives p = .004, so age band and use of the scheme are associated.\nExplain it to a friend: the youngest band is 11 users above its fair share and the oldest is 10 below. Those two rows do almost all the work.","wrongs":["This divides each squared difference by the grand total 240 rather than by the expected count: 444 / 240 = 1.85.","This adds up only the \"has used\" column and stops: 3.270270 + 0.027027 + 2.702703 = 6.00. Every cell in the table contributes, both columns included.","","This divides by the OBSERVED count instead of the expected count in each cell, which gives 11.94. Close to the right answer, which is exactly why the mistake survives unnoticed. Expected counts belong in the denominator."],"trap":"Stopping after the first column because the second looks like a mirror image. It is not: the expected counts there are 43, not 37, so the contributions differ.","tri":"Symptom: a 3 x 2 table of counts and \"what is the value of the statistic\". -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square from a table, more than two rows. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total for every one of the six cells, then sum (O - E) squared / E over all six. df = (3 - 1)(2 - 1) = 2.","out":{"cap":"Ever used the bike-share scheme, by age band","h":["Age band","Has used it","Has not used it","Total"],"r":[["18 to 29","48","32","80"],["30 to 49","36","44","80"],["50 and over","27","53","80"],["Total","111","129","240"]]}},{"n":7,"q":"Rosa Jimenez compares the proportion of patients who kept a follow-up appointment in two Spanish clinics. She runs a two-proportion test on her 2 x 2 table of counts and reports a test statistic of 2.47 with a two-sided p-value of .014. A reviewer asks her to analyse the same table as a test of association instead. What will she find?","o":["A statistic of 6.10 on 1 degree of freedom, with p = .014, exactly the same p-value.","A statistic of 6.10 on 1 degree of freedom, with p = .007, half the p-value of the first test.","A statistic of 2.47 on 1 degree of freedom, with p = .116.","A statistic of 1.57 on 1 degree of freedom, with p = .210."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_z","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. A 2 x 2 table and TWO tests being compared. For a 2 x 2 table only, the chi-square statistic is the z statistic squared, and the two-sided p-values are identical. If the table were bigger than 2 x 2 this shortcut would not exist.","plain":"In plain words: the two tests are the same test wearing different clothes. Square the z and you have the chi-square. The p-value does not move.","work":"Step 1. Square the z statistic. 2.47 x 2.47 = 6.1009, so 6.10.\nStep 2. Note the df. A 2 x 2 table gives df = (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1, which is exactly the one df that makes the equality work.\nStep 3. Think about the p-value. A chi-square on 1 df counts both tails of the z distribution at once, because squaring turns -2.47 and +2.47 into the same 6.10. So the chi-square p-value is the TWO-sided z p-value.\nStep 4. Check it. The two-sided p for z = 2.47 is .0135, and the upper-tail p for chi-square = 6.1009 on 1 df is also .0135. Both round to .014.\nStep 5. So the statistic changes, the conclusion does not.\nMemory hook: chi-square on 1 df is a z that has been squared, and squaring folds the two tails into one.","wrongs":["","This halves the p-value, as though chi-square were a one-sided version of the z test. It is the opposite: squaring already folds both tails in, so the chi-square p-value IS the two-sided one. Halving it is the classic one-sided reading of a two-sided test.","This leaves the statistic unsquared. 2.47 as a chi-square value on 1 df would give p = .116 and would reverse her conclusion.","This takes the square root instead of squaring. The square root of 2.47 is 1.57, which on 1 df gives p = .210. The arrow runs z to chi-square by squaring, chi-square to z by rooting."],"trap":"Halving the p-value because \"chi-square only has one tail\". The single upper tail of chi-square already contains both z tails. Nothing needs halving or doubling.","tri":"Symptom: a 2 x 2 table, a z statistic given, a chi-square asked for, or the other way round. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square equals z squared. -&gt; Treatment: chi-square = z squared, df = 1, and the two-sided p from z equals the chi-square p. To go backwards, take the square root of chi-square."},{"n":8,"q":"Hannah Brooks records which one of five arrival channels each of 260 new volunteers at a British charity came through. The charity's recruitment plan assumed the five channels would supply 30, 25, 20, 15 and 10 per cent of volunteers. She wants to know whether the real arrivals match the plan. What should she do, and what is her null hypothesis?","o":["A test of independence, with H0 that arrival channel and volunteering are independent.","A two-proportion test, with H0 that the proportions arriving through the two largest channels are equal.","A goodness-of-fit test, with H0 that the five channels are equally likely, that is 20 per cent each.","A goodness-of-fit test, with H0 that the population proportions are .30, .25, .20, .15 and .10, exactly as the plan states."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"gof_which","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Count the variables in the stem. Here there is only ONE, the arrival channel, and a set of claimed proportions to compare it with. One variable plus a claimed split means goodness of fit. Independence needs TWO variables measured on the same people.","plain":"In plain words: she has one list of categories and somebody else's prediction for how they should split. The test asks whether her counts could plausibly have come from that prediction.","work":"Step 1. Count the variables. Arrival channel, and nothing else. Only one, so there is no table of rows and columns and no question of independence.\nStep 2. Find what the counts are being compared with. The plan supplies a specific set of proportions, not an assumption that all channels are equal.\nStep 3. Write H0 using those proportions: in the population of volunteers, the channel proportions are .30, .25, .20, .15 and .10.\nStep 4. Write H1 as the plain opposite: at least one of those proportions is different.\nStep 5. Note the expected counts you would use: 78, 65, 52, 39 and 26, which add to 260. df = 5 - 1 = 4.\nExplain it to a friend: goodness of fit asks \"does my one pile of people split the way you said it would\". Independence asks \"do two labels on the same person travel together\".","wrongs":["Independence needs two categorical variables measured on the same people. Here every person in the sample is a volunteer, so \"volunteering\" has only one level and there is nothing to cross it with.","A two-proportion test compares two groups on one yes-or-no outcome. She has five categories and a full claimed split, so this would throw away most of her data and would not answer her question.","This is the right test with the wrong H0. Equal proportions, 20 per cent each, is only the default when no split is claimed. Here a specific split IS claimed, and it is not an equal one.",""],"trap":"Defaulting to \"all categories equally likely\". Read the stem for a stated split first. The expected counts come from whatever proportions the claim names, equal or not.","tri":"Symptom: one categorical variable, counts in each category, and a claimed set of proportions. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square goodness of fit. -&gt; Treatment: H0 names the claimed population proportions, one per category. Expected counts are those proportions times n, and df = categories - 1, here 4."},{"n":9,"q":"Georgios Papadakis treats 300 patients at a Greek clinic with one of three therapy programmes and classifies each patient into one of four outcome categories. His margins are printed below. What is the expected count for patients on programme A who end up in the \"full recovery\" category, and how many degrees of freedom does his test have?","o":["Expected count 36.0 and df = 6","Expected count 36.0 and df = 12","Expected count 30.0 and df = 6","Expected count 25.0 and df = 11"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_df","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. The stem asks for TWO things at once, one expected count and the df. Both come from the margins alone: the individual cell counts are not even needed. Expected uses a product over the grand total, df uses a product of the reduced dimensions.","plain":"In plain words: of the 90 full recoveries, how many would programme A get if the programme made no difference? And how many free cells does the table have?","work":"Step 1. Identify the row and column for the cell. Row = programme A, row total 120. Column = full recovery, column total 90. Grand total 300.\nStep 2. Expected count. 120 x 90 / 300 = 10800 / 300 = 36.0 exactly.\nStep 3. Sanity check it. Programme A holds 120 of 300 patients, which is 40 per cent. Forty per cent of the 90 full recoveries is 36. Same answer, which confirms that the formula is just \"this row's share of that column\".\nStep 4. Degrees of freedom. Three programmes and four outcomes, so r = 3 and c = 4, giving (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 x 3 = 6.\nStep 5. Pair them up: expected 36.0 with df = 6.\nMemory hook: expected is a row share of a column. df knocks one off each side and multiplies.","wrongs":["","The expected count is right but df = 12 counts the cells, 3 x 4, instead of reducing each dimension by one.","df = 6 is right but the expected count of 30.0 comes from splitting the row equally over the four outcomes, 120 / 4 = 30. That assumes the four outcomes are equally common, which the column totals show they are not: they run from 60 to 90.","Both parts assume every cell should be equal. 300 / 12 cells = 25, and 12 cells minus one = 11. That is goodness-of-fit thinking applied to a two-way table."],"trap":"Splitting a row equally across the columns. Independence means each row copies the OVERALL column split, and here that split is uneven: 90, 84, 66, 60.","tri":"Symptom: a request for one expected count plus the df, with only the margins given. -&gt; Diagnosis: expected count and df together. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total. df = (rows - 1)(columns - 1). Do them separately and then match the pair.","out":{"cap":"Row and column totals for the 300 patients","h":["Total","Count"],"r":[["Programme A","120"],["Programme B","100"],["Programme C","80"],["Full recovery","90"],["Partial recovery","84"],["No change","66"],["Worse","60"]]}},{"n":10,"q":"Kwame Mensah wants to know whether study method and exam outcome are associated among students at a Ghanaian university. Four different data sets are offered to him. Which one would let him run a test of association?","o":["A table giving, for each study method, the percentage of its users who passed, with no counts shown.","A table of counts of students in which each of 180 different students appears in exactly one cell.","A table of counts in which each of 90 students appears twice, once under the method used before a study skills course and once under the method used after it.","A table giving the mean exam mark of the students using each study method."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_design","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. The question is about the DATA, not the arithmetic. Three things must be true: the entries are counts of people, the people are independent of one another, and nobody is counted twice. Percentages and means both fail at the first hurdle.","plain":"In plain words: this test eats head counts of separate people. Anything averaged, anything turned into a percentage, and anything where the same person appears twice is the wrong food.","work":"Step 1. Counts or not. The statistic divides squared differences by expected COUNTS, and the sample size enters only through those counts. Percentages have had n stripped out of them, so they cannot be used. Means are not counts at all.\nStep 2. One cell per person. If the same student appears twice, the cells are not independent and the statistic is inflated: the test thinks it has 180 people when it really has 90.\nStep 3. Independence of people. 180 different students, each in one cell, each contributing once. That passes.\nStep 4. So the only workable data set is the table of 180 students counted once each.\nStep 5. Note the fix for the before-and-after table: that design calls for a test built for paired data, not a test of independence.\nExplain it to a friend: one person, one tick, one box. If anyone gets two ticks, or the boxes hold averages, the test is out.","wrongs":["Percentages cannot go into a chi-square. A 60 per cent pass rate could come from 3 students out of 5 or 600 out of 1000, and the test needs to know which.","","Each student is counted twice, so the 180 entries come from only 90 independent people. That breaks the independence requirement and makes the statistic too large. Paired categorical data need a different test.","Means belong to a t test or an ANOVA, not to chi-square. Exam mark is a numeric variable here, not a category."],"trap":"Accepting a table because it looks like a table. Check what is INSIDE the cells. Only counts of independent, once-counted people will do.","tri":"Symptom: four possible data sets, one test, \"which one would work\". -&gt; Diagnosis: design requirements for a chi-square test. -&gt; Treatment: check three things. 1 Are the entries counts, not percentages or means? 2 Is each person in exactly one cell? 3 Are the people independent of one another? All three must pass."}]},{"id":"t3b","name":"Chi-square - Set B","short":"Chi-square - Set B","blurb":"A second pass through the counts. Choosing between goodness of fit and a test of independence, computing the statistic from 2 x 2 and 2 x 3 tables, reading expected counts off a printed output, the z-squared link, the design rules, and the degrees of freedom.","theme":3,"icon":"🎲","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Elif Demir has the birth month of 600 professional footballers in Turkey, grouped into the four quarters of the year. In the general population the four quarters hold 24, 26, 25 and 25 per cent of births. She wants to know whether footballers are spread over the quarters in the same way. What should she test, and what is her null hypothesis?","o":["A goodness-of-fit test, with H0 that the population proportions of footballers born in the four quarters are .24, .26, .25 and .25.","A test of independence, with H0 that birth quarter and being a footballer are independent.","A goodness-of-fit test, with H0 that the four quarters are equally likely, .25 each.","A one-proportion test, with H0 that the proportion of footballers born in the first quarter is .24."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"gof_which","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Count the variables. Birth quarter is the only one measured, and everyone in the sample is already a footballer, so there is nothing to cross it with. The population figures supply the claimed proportions for H0.","plain":"In plain words: she has one pile of 600 footballers split into four quarters, and somebody has told her what the split should look like. Does hers match?","work":"Step 1. Count the variables in the sample. Just birth quarter. \"Being a footballer\" is constant across all 600 people, so it is not a variable at all.\nStep 2. Find the comparison. The general-population percentages are the claim, so they go into H0 as proportions.\nStep 3. Write H0: the four population proportions for footballers are .24, .26, .25 and .25. H1: at least one of them is different.\nStep 4. Expected counts would be 0.24 x 600 = 144, 0.26 x 600 = 156, 0.25 x 600 = 150 and 150 again. They add to 600, which is the check.\nStep 5. df = 4 - 1 = 3.\nExplain it to a friend: there is no second column to cross against, so this cannot be an independence question. It is a \"does my split match the claimed split\" question.","wrongs":["","Independence needs two variables measured on the same people. Every person here is a footballer, so that variable has only one level and no table can be built. This would only work if she also had a comparison sample of non-footballers.","Right test, wrong H0. Equal quarters is the default only when no split is claimed. Here the general population figures are given and they are not equal: .24 and .26 differ from .25.","A one-proportion test would use only the first quarter and discard the other three categories, so it cannot answer whether the whole spread matches."],"trap":"Reaching for a test of independence because two groups seem to be mentioned. The comparison group here is not in the sample: its proportions arrive as fixed numbers, which is exactly what goodness of fit is for.","tri":"Symptom: one categorical variable, a claimed split taken from outside the sample. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square goodness of fit. -&gt; Treatment: H0 lists the claimed population proportions. Expected counts = proportion x n. df = categories - 1, here 3."},{"n":2,"q":"Lars Nielsen tests a new shelf label in a Danish supermarket. Of 200 shoppers who saw the new label, 88 bought the organic version of the product, and of 200 shoppers who saw the old label, 62 did. His table is printed below. What is the value of the test statistic for an association between label and purchase? (2 decimals)","o":["1.69","3.61","7.21","7.38"],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"chi_calc","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. A 2 x 2 table of head counts and a number wanted. Nothing in the table is an average or a percentage, so this is the chi-square statistic. Equal row totals mean both rows share the same expected counts.","plain":"In plain words: 150 of the 400 shoppers bought organic. If the label made no difference, each group of 200 should contain half of those 150, that is 75. Compare 88 and 62 with 75.","work":"Step 1. Totals. Rows 200 and 200, columns 150 and 250, grand total 400.\nStep 2. Expected counts. New label and bought: 200 x 150 / 400 = 75. New label and did not: 200 x 250 / 400 = 125. The old-label row is the same, 75 and 125, because the row totals match.\nStep 3. Cell by cell. (88 - 75) squared / 75 = 169 / 75 = 2.253333. (112 - 125) squared / 125 = 169 / 125 = 1.352. (62 - 75) squared / 75 = 2.253333. (138 - 125) squared / 125 = 1.352.\nStep 4. Add them: 2.253333 + 1.352 + 2.253333 + 1.352 = 7.210667, so 7.21.\nStep 5. With df = 1 this gives p = .007, so the label and the purchase are associated.\nExplain it to a friend: in a 2 x 2 with equal rows, all four cells carry the same gap of 13. Only the denominators differ, which is why the two columns contribute different amounts.","wrongs":["This divides each squared difference by the grand total 400 instead of by the expected count: (169 x 4) / 400 = 1.69.","This stops after the first row: 2.253333 + 1.352 = 3.61. Both rows count.","","This divides by the OBSERVED count instead of the expected count: 169 / 88 + 169 / 112 + 169 / 62 + 169 / 138 = 7.38. Only three hundredths away from the right answer, which is why it is worth getting the denominator right by habit rather than by luck."],"trap":"Using the observed count in the denominator. The two answers are close here, so the mistake does not announce itself. O on top, E underneath, every time.","tri":"Symptom: a 2 x 2 table of counts, \"what is the value of the statistic\". -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square from a table. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total for all four cells, then sum (O - E) squared / E. df = 1.","out":{"cap":"Bought the organic version, by shelf label","h":["Label seen","Bought organic","Did not","Total"],"r":[["New label","88","112","200"],["Old label","62","138","200"],["Total","150","250","400"]]}},{"n":3,"q":"Sofia Kowalska builds a table of heating type by income band from 240 Polish households and plans a test of association. A colleague raises four points about her study. Which point would actually make the test invalid?","o":["The two variables have different numbers of categories, four heating types against three income bands.","Some households own two homes and were recorded once for each home, so they appear twice in the table.","The table shows counts of households rather than percentages of households.","The 240 households were drawn at random from the national register."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_design","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. \"Which point would invalidate the test\" is a design question. Run through the requirements: counts rather than percentages, each person or unit in exactly one cell, independent observations. Only a breach of one of those is fatal.","plain":"In plain words: three of these four points are harmless or even good. One of them means the table secretly has fewer independent households than it claims.","work":"Step 1. Unequal numbers of categories. Perfectly fine. The table simply becomes 4 x 3, with df = (4 - 1)(3 - 1) = 6. Rows and columns never have to match.\nStep 2. Double counting. Fatal. If a household appears twice, its two entries are linked, so the observations are not independent. The sample size is inflated and the statistic comes out too large, which makes a false positive more likely.\nStep 3. Counts rather than percentages. This is exactly what the test requires. Percentages would be the problem, not counts.\nStep 4. Random sampling from the register. This is a strength, and it supports the independence requirement rather than breaking it.\nStep 5. So the invalidating point is the double counting.\nMemory hook: one household, one row of data, one cell. Two entries for one household is two votes for one voter.","wrongs":["Rows and columns do not need the same number of categories. A 4 x 3 table is completely ordinary and simply gives df = 6.","","Counts are what the test needs. This option inverts the rule: it is PERCENTAGES that cannot be fed into chi-square, because they hide the sample size.","Random sampling is a strength. It helps guarantee that the households are independent of one another, which is one of the requirements."],"trap":"Picking the \"different numbers of categories\" option because the table is not square. Squareness is irrelevant. Independence of the counted units is what matters.","tri":"Symptom: a list of features of a study and a question about validity. -&gt; Diagnosis: design requirements for a chi-square test. -&gt; Treatment: check 1 counts, not percentages or means; 2 each unit counted exactly once; 3 independent units; 4 expected counts large enough. A breach of 2 or 3 is fatal."},{"n":4,"q":"Ahmed Hassan records, for each of 400 students at an Egyptian university, their sex and which one of five canteen meals they chose. His margins are printed below. What is the expected count for male students choosing meal 2, and how many degrees of freedom does his test have?","o":["Expected count 30.0 and df = 4","Expected count 34.5 and df = 9","Expected count 46.0 and df = 1","Expected count 34.5 and df = 4"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_df","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Two things wanted in one question, an expected count and the df, and only the margins are given. Neither answer needs a single cell count. Expected is a product over the grand total, df is a product of the reduced dimensions.","plain":"In plain words: 92 students chose meal 2. If sex made no difference to the choice, how many of those 92 would be male? And how many cells of the table are free once the margins are fixed?","work":"Step 1. Locate the cell. Row = male, row total 150. Column = meal 2, column total 92. Grand total 400.\nStep 2. Expected count. 150 x 92 / 400 = 13800 / 400 = 34.5. Leave it as 34.5: do not round it to 34 or 35.\nStep 3. Sanity check. Males are 150 of 400, which is 37.5 per cent. 37.5 per cent of 92 is 34.5. Same number, which confirms the logic: the row takes its usual share of that column.\nStep 4. Degrees of freedom. Two sexes and five meals, so r = 2 and c = 5, giving (2 - 1)(5 - 1) = 1 x 4 = 4.\nStep 5. Pair them: 34.5 with df = 4.\nMemory hook: half a student is allowed in an expected count. It is an average, not a person.","wrongs":["df = 4 is right, but 30.0 comes from splitting the male row equally over the five meals, 150 / 5 = 30. The meals are not equally popular: the column totals run from 48 to 104.","The expected count is right, but df = 9 counts the ten cells and subtracts one, which is the goodness-of-fit rule for a single variable.","This splits the meal 2 column equally between the sexes, 92 / 2 = 46, and uses df = rows - 1 = 1. Both halves ignore that the sexes are not equally represented: 150 against 250.",""],"trap":"Rounding 34.5 to 35 because students are whole people, or splitting a row evenly across the columns. Expected counts keep their decimals and follow the real margins.","tri":"Symptom: one expected count plus the df, margins only. -&gt; Diagnosis: expected count and df together. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total, unrounded. df = (rows - 1)(columns - 1). Compute them separately, then pick the option whose BOTH halves match.","out":{"cap":"Row and column totals for the 400 students","h":["Total","Count"],"r":[["Male","150"],["Female","250"],["Meal 1","48"],["Meal 2","92"],["Meal 3","76"],["Meal 4","104"],["Meal 5","80"]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Isabelle Laurent asks 150 cyclists in Lyon and 150 cyclists in Marseille which one of three parking options they use most. Her table is printed below. What is the value of the test statistic for an association between city and parking option? (2 decimals)","o":["1.10","2.01","5.85","11.71"],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"chi_calc","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. A 2 x 3 table of counts with the totals printed for you. Same recipe as a 2 x 2, just six cells instead of four. Equal row totals mean the two rows share the same expected counts.","plain":"In plain words: across both cities, 106 cyclists use a street rack, 104 a garage and 90 park at home. If the city made no difference, each city of 150 should contain half of each of those numbers. Compare that with the table.","work":"Step 1. Totals. Rows 150 and 150, columns 106, 104 and 90, grand total 300.\nStep 2. Expected counts. Street rack: 150 x 106 / 300 = 53. Garage: 150 x 104 / 300 = 52. At home: 150 x 90 / 300 = 45. Both rows get the same three numbers, 53, 52 and 45, because the row totals are equal.\nStep 3. Lyon row. (62 - 53) squared / 53 = 81 / 53 = 1.528302. (38 - 52) squared / 52 = 196 / 52 = 3.769231. (50 - 45) squared / 45 = 25 / 45 = 0.555556.\nStep 4. Marseille row. The gaps are the same size with the opposite sign, so the three contributions repeat: 1.528302, 3.769231 and 0.555556.\nStep 5. Add all six: 2 x (1.528302 + 3.769231 + 0.555556) = 2 x 5.853089 = 11.706176, so 11.71.\nStep 6. With df = 2 this gives p = .003, so city and parking option are associated. The garage column does most of the work.\nExplain it to a friend: Marseille has 14 more garage users than its fair share and Lyon has 14 fewer. That single column contributes more than half the statistic.","wrongs":["This uses the plain gap instead of the squared gap: 9 / 53 + 14 / 52 + 5 / 45, doubled, gives 1.10. The gap must be squared before dividing.","This divides each squared gap by the grand total 300 rather than by the expected count: (81 + 196 + 25) x 2 / 300 = 2.01.","This adds up only the Lyon row and stops: 1.528302 + 3.769231 + 0.555556 = 5.85. Exactly half the right answer, because the two rows contribute equally here.",""],"trap":"Stopping at half way. When the two rows have equal totals the second row contributes the SAME amount as the first, so the answer is double what you have, not what you have.","tri":"Symptom: a 2 x 3 table of counts and a request for the statistic. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square from a table. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total for all six cells, then sum (O - E) squared / E over all six. df = (2 - 1)(3 - 1) = 2.","out":{"cap":"Preferred parking option, by city","h":["City","Street rack","Guarded garage","At home","Total"],"r":[["Lyon","62","38","50","150"],["Marseille","44","66","40","150"],["Total","106","104","90","300"]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Yuki Tanaka teaches two tutorial formats to 100 students each and records who passed. Her table is printed below. A test of association on this table gives a statistic of 4.80 on 1 degree of freedom with p = .028. She now runs a two-proportion test on the same data. What will she get?","o":["A statistic of 2.19, with a two-sided p-value of .028, exactly the same p-value.","A statistic of 2.19, with a p-value of .014, half the first p-value.","A statistic of 4.80, with a p-value of .028, because the two statistics are the same number.","A statistic of 23.04, with p &lt; .001, because the first statistic has to be squared again."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_z","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. A 2 x 2 table, one statistic given, the other asked for. For a 2 x 2 table ONLY, chi-square = z squared. Going from chi-square back to z means taking the square root, and the two-sided p-value does not move.","plain":"In plain words: the two tests are the same test. Take the square root of 4.80 and you have the z. The p-value stays where it was.","work":"Step 1. Take the square root. The square root of 4.80 is 2.190890, so 2.19.\nStep 2. Check the sign. Format A passed 45 of 100, Format B passed 30 of 100, so putting A first gives z = +2.19. Putting B first gives -2.19. Squaring removes the sign, which is why chi-square cannot tell you the direction.\nStep 3. Confirm it the other way round. 2.190890 squared = 4.80 exactly.\nStep 4. The p-value. Both tests report the same two-sided p, .028. Nothing is halved and nothing is doubled.\nStep 5. So the statistic is 2.19 and the p-value is .028.\nMemory hook: square the z to get chi-square, root the chi-square to get the z. The p-value is the one thing that never changes.","wrongs":["","This halves the p-value. The chi-square p-value already IS the two-sided z p-value, because squaring folds both tails of the z distribution into the one upper tail of chi-square. Halving it is a one-sided reading of a two-sided test.","The two statistics are not the same number: one is the square of the other. 4.80 as a z value would be far out in the tail and would give p &lt; .001.","This squares again: 4.80 squared = 23.04. The relationship runs the other way. Chi-square is the square of z, so z is the square ROOT of chi-square."],"trap":"Squaring when you should be rooting. Write down which statistic you have before you touch the calculator: chi-square is always the bigger of the two when z is above 1.","tri":"Symptom: a 2 x 2 table and both a chi-square and a z being compared. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square equals z squared. -&gt; Treatment: z = plus or minus the square root of chi-square, df = 1, and the two-sided z p-value equals the chi-square p-value. Which sign you get depends only on which group you put first.","out":{"cap":"Passed the course, by tutorial format","h":["Format","Passed","Failed","Total"],"r":[["Format A","45","55","100"],["Format B","30","70","100"],["Total","75","125","200"]]}},{"n":7,"q":"In a 2 x 3 table built from 240 respondents, Marco Bianchi calculates an expected count of 27.5 for one of the cells. A fellow student tells him this must be an error, because you cannot have half a person. What is the best reply?","o":["The student is right, and expected counts should be rounded to the nearest whole person before the statistic is computed.","Expected counts are not people. They are the counts the cell would hold on average if the two variables were independent, so fractions are normal and must be left unrounded.","The student is right, because an expected count below 30 breaks the condition for this test.","Expected counts come from the alternative hypothesis, so a fractional value is a sign that the two variables are associated."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_concept","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. A fractional expected count is being questioned. Two facts rescue it: expected counts come from the null hypothesis of independence, and they are averages over imagined samples, so they are not whole people and are never rounded.","plain":"In plain words: 27.5 is not a prediction that half a person will show up. It is the long-run average count for that cell if the two variables had nothing to do with each other.","work":"Step 1. Recall the formula. Expected = row total x column total / grand total. There is no reason that division should come out whole, and usually it does not.\nStep 2. Recall what it means. It is the average count you would see in that cell over many samples of 240 if H0 were true. Averages of whole numbers are routinely fractional, like 2.3 children per family.\nStep 3. Consider rounding it. The expected count sits in the denominator of (O - E) squared / E and in the numerator of the gap, so rounding changes the statistic and therefore the p-value. Do not do it.\nStep 4. Check the condition people half-remember. The usual rule is that expected counts should be at least about 5, not 30. 27.5 is comfortably fine.\nStep 5. So the correct reply explains the average and refuses the rounding.\nExplain it to a friend: nobody is claiming half a respondent exists. The 27.5 is a yardstick, and yardsticks are allowed to have fractions of a unit on them.","wrongs":["Rounding is wrong. The expected count enters the formula twice and rounding it shifts the statistic. It is an average, not a head count.","","The threshold is misremembered. The usual guidance is that expected counts should be at least about 5, and that all of them should be reasonably large. 27.5 passes easily. Also, a condition on the SIZE of an expected count says nothing about whether it may be fractional.","Expected counts come from H0, not H1. A fractional value says nothing at all about association: it is just the arithmetic of the margins."],"trap":"Rounding to whole people out of tidiness. The right answer to \"can a cell hold 27.5 people\" is \"no, but the yardstick can read 27.5\".","tri":"Symptom: a fractional expected count, and somebody objecting to it. -&gt; Diagnosis: the concept of an expected count. -&gt; Treatment: say what it is, the count under H0 of independence, equal to row total x column total / grand total, and say that it is an average, so decimals stay."},{"n":8,"q":"Astrid Johansen sorts 500 Norwegian survey respondents into three attitude groups. Her software prints the observed and expected counts below, where the expected counts come from a claimed split of 25, 50 and 25 per cent. What is the value of the test statistic? (2 decimals)","o":["0.20","0.59","1.57","2.16"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"gof_stat","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. The expected counts are already printed, so all the margin work is done. One categorical variable plus observed and expected counts means goodness of fit: just feed the two columns into the formula.","plain":"In plain words: for each of the three rows, see how far the real count is from the predicted count, square that gap, divide by the prediction, and add the three answers up.","work":"Step 1. Check the totals first. Observed: 118 + 243 + 139 = 500. Expected: 125 + 250 + 125 = 500. They match, so nothing is missing.\nStep 2. Against. (118 - 125) squared / 125 = 49 / 125 = 0.392.\nStep 3. Neutral. (243 - 250) squared / 250 = 49 / 250 = 0.196.\nStep 4. In favour. (139 - 125) squared / 125 = 196 / 125 = 1.568.\nStep 5. Add them: 0.392 + 0.196 + 1.568 = 2.156, so 2.16.\nStep 6. With df = 3 - 1 = 2 this gives p = .34, so the data are quite consistent with the claimed 25, 50, 25 split.\nMemory hook: same gap, different denominator, different damage. The gap of 7 costs 0.392 against a prediction of 125 but only 0.196 against 250.","wrongs":["This uses the plain gap instead of the squared gap: 7 / 125 + 7 / 250 + 14 / 125 = 0.196. Squaring is what makes a big miss count much more than a small one.","This divides each squared gap by n = 500 instead of by the expected count: (49 + 49 + 196) / 500 = 0.588.","This takes only the third row, 196 / 125 = 1.568, because it is the biggest contribution. All three rows are added, even the small ones.",""],"trap":"Only adding the rows where the gap looks big. Every category contributes, and the two small contributions here are nearly a fifth of the total.","tri":"Symptom: a printed table with an Observed column and an Expected column. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square goodness of fit, expected counts supplied. -&gt; Treatment: sum (O - E) squared / E across the rows. df = categories - 1, here 2.","out":{"cap":"Attitude groups: observed and expected counts, n = 500","h":["Attitude group","Observed","Expected"],"r":[["Against","118","125"],["Neutral","243","250"],["In favour","139","125"]]}},{"n":9,"q":"Daniela Cruz records, for each of 300 employees of a Mexican firm, their contract type, permanent or temporary, and whether they intend to leave within a year. She wants to know whether the two go together. Which statement about her null hypothesis is correct?","o":["It states that the proportion intending to leave is higher among temporary staff than among permanent staff.","It states that the two proportions intending to leave in her sample of 300 are exactly equal.","It states that in the population, contract type and intention to leave are independent, which is the same as saying the proportion intending to leave is the same for both contract types.","It states that the chi-square statistic is equal to zero."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_hyp","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. \"Which statement about H0 is correct\" on a 2 x 2 design. The right answer is about the POPULATION, says nothing about direction, and may be phrased either as independence or as equal proportions. For a 2 x 2 those two phrasings mean the same thing.","plain":"In plain words: H0 says contract type tells you nothing about whether someone plans to leave. With only two contract types that is the same as saying the two leaving rates are equal in the population.","work":"Step 1. Write the standard H0: contract type and intention to leave are independent in the population.\nStep 2. Translate it for a 2 x 2. Independence means each contract type splits on \"intends to leave\" in the same proportions, so the two population proportions are equal. This is also why a 2 x 2 chi-square and a two-proportion test agree.\nStep 3. Rule out direction. H0 never says one group is higher. That would be a one-sided H1, and chi-square has no one-sided version.\nStep 4. Rule out the sample. H0 is a claim about the population. The two sample proportions will differ a little whatever is true.\nStep 5. Rule out claims about the statistic. H0 is about the world, not about the number you will calculate from it.\nExplain it to a friend: H0 is \"knowing the contract tells me nothing\". With two contracts, that is just \"both groups leave at the same rate\".","wrongs":["This is directional and belongs in a one-sided H1, not in H0. A chi-square test has no direction at all: a large statistic only says the counts do not match independence.","H0 is about the population, never about the sample. Two sample proportions are practically never exactly equal, so this H0 would be false in almost any data set.","","H0 is a claim about the population, not about the test statistic. The statistic is what you compute from the data in order to judge H0, and it is positive in essentially every real sample even when H0 is true."],"trap":"Thinking \"independence\" and \"equal proportions\" are rival answers. On a 2 x 2 they are the same statement, which is why the right option can say both.","tri":"Symptom: a 2 x 2 design and four readings of H0. -&gt; Diagnosis: hypotheses for a chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: H0 is independence in the POPULATION, with no direction. On a 2 x 2 it can equally be written as \"the two population proportions are equal\". Reject anything about the sample, anything directional, and anything about the statistic."},{"n":10,"q":"Noor Rahman records, for each of 480 Bangladeshi farmers, which one of three irrigation methods they use and which one of six crops they mainly grow. She wants to know whether method and crop are related. How many degrees of freedom does her test have?","o":["2","7","10","17"],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"chi_df","theme":3,"spot":"HINT. Two categorical variables with their category counts stated, and df asked for. Multiply the two reduced dimensions. The 480 farmers are a decoy: sample size never enters a chi-square df.","plain":"In plain words: once the row and column totals are known, how many cells could still be anything? That number is the df.","work":"Step 1. Count the categories. Three irrigation methods means r = 3. Six crops means c = 6.\nStep 2. Reduce each by one: 3 - 1 = 2 and 6 - 1 = 5.\nStep 3. Multiply: 2 x 5 = 10.\nStep 4. Check with the free-cells idea. The table has 18 cells. Fix the margins, fill in a 2 by 5 block freely, and the remaining 8 cells are forced. So 10 are free.\nStep 5. Ignore the 480 farmers entirely.\nMemory hook: knock one off each side, then multiply the leftovers. Never add, never use n.","wrongs":["This uses rows - 1 only, 3 - 1 = 2, as if crop were not a variable. That is the goodness-of-fit rule applied to half the table.","This adds instead of multiplying: (3 - 1) + (6 - 1) = 7. The formula is a product.","","This treats the 18 cells as one list of categories and uses 18 - 1 = 17. That is the goodness-of-fit rule, for a single variable with 18 categories."],"trap":"Adding the two reduced dimensions. Here that gives 7, which looks plausible beside 10. Write the brackets out as a product before you compute.","tri":"Symptom: an r x c table and \"how many degrees of freedom\". -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: df = (rows - 1)(columns - 1). Multiply, do not add, and do not use n."}]},{"id":"t4a","name":"Correlation and regression - Set A","short":"Correlation and regression - Set A","blurb":"The biggest theme on the paper, about seven of the forty items. What a correlation does and does not license you to say, the slope that comes out of r and the two standard deviations, range restriction, aggregated data, what leaves r untouched, what the least-squares line actually minimises, and r squared.","theme":4,"icon":"📈","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Bruno Alves reports that across 1,200 Portuguese office workers the number of plants on a desk correlates r = .31 with self-reported job satisfaction, p &lt; .001. A magazine wants to run the headline \"Desk plants make staff happier\". What is the problem with that headline?","o":["The data are correlational, so the link cannot show that plants raise satisfaction. Satisfied staff may simply decorate more, or something else may drive both.","Nothing is wrong. With p &lt; .001 the effect is established beyond reasonable doubt.","Only the wording is wrong. With r = .31 the headline should say that plants make staff slightly happier.","The problem is that r = .31 means plants account for 31 per cent of job satisfaction, which is too little for a headline."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"causation","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. A correlation is reported and then a causal sentence is proposed. The words \"makes\", \"raises\", \"improves\" or \"leads to\" in a headline are the signal. The answer is always that a correlation on its own cannot support that verb.","plain":"In plain words: he measured two things on the same people without changing anything. That tells you they travel together, not which one is driving.","work":"Step 1. Check the design. Nobody was assigned plants. Workers chose their own desks and their own plants, so the groups are self-selected.\nStep 2. List the readings the data allow. Plants raise satisfaction. Or satisfaction raises plants, because happy people decorate. Or a third factor, such as having a window seat or a long tenure, raises both.\nStep 3. Note what the p-value settles. p &lt; .001 says the link is unlikely to be pure chance. It says nothing about direction and nothing about confounders. With 1,200 people even r = .31 is easily significant.\nStep 4. Note what r squared would add. 0.31 squared = 0.0961, so plants go with under 10 per cent of the variation in satisfaction. Even the size is modest.\nStep 5. So the objection is about the DESIGN, not about the size or the p-value.\nExplain it to a friend: to earn the word \"make\", you have to hand out the plants yourself, at random.","wrongs":["","A small p-value rules out chance, not confounding and not reverse causation. Significance and causation are different questions, and a large sample makes small correlations significant without making them causal.","Softening the verb to \"slightly\" does not fix the problem. Any causal verb is too strong here, however hedged.","This reads r where r squared belongs. The share of variation is 0.31 squared, which is 9.6 per cent, not 31 per cent. The real objection is the design, not the size."],"trap":"Letting a very small p-value stand in for a causal design. Ask instead: who decided which workers got plants? If the answer is \"the workers themselves\", no causal verb is allowed.","tri":"Symptom: an observed correlation turned into a causal claim. -&gt; Diagnosis: correlation is not causation. -&gt; Treatment: name the two rival readings that the data cannot rule out, reverse causation and a third variable, and say that only random assignment to conditions would settle it."},{"n":2,"q":"Chiara Moretti fits a line predicting weekly study hours from a motivation score for 95 Italian students. The correlation between the two is .62. The standard deviation of study hours is 8.4 and the standard deviation of motivation scores is 3.5. What is the slope of that line? (2 decimals)","o":["0.26","0.62","1.49","2.40"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"slope_from_r","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. You are given r and two standard deviations and asked for a slope, or the reverse. The response variable, the thing being predicted, supplies the SD on top. Decide which variable is y before you touch the numbers.","plain":"In plain words: r is the relationship in standardised units. To put it back into real units you scale it by how spread out the two variables are, with the predicted variable on top.","work":"Step 1. Identify y. She predicts study hours, so study hours is y and SD of y = 8.4. Motivation is x and SD of x = 3.5.\nStep 2. Write the formula. b = r x (SD of y / SD of x).\nStep 3. The ratio of spreads. 8.4 / 3.5 = 2.4.\nStep 4. Multiply by r. 0.62 x 2.4 = 1.488.\nStep 5. Round to 1.49. In words: one extra point of motivation goes with about 1.49 extra study hours per week.\nStep 6. Sanity check the size. The slope must be smaller than the bare ratio 2.4, because r is below 1. 1.49 is below 2.4, so the size is plausible.\nMemory hook: y over x, like the slope itself, which is rise over run. The predicted variable rises, so its SD goes on top.","wrongs":["This has the ratio upside down: 0.62 x 3.5 / 8.4 = 0.26. That formula gives the slope for predicting motivation FROM study hours, which is a different line.","This reports r itself as the slope. r has no units, the slope has units of hours per motivation point. They are only equal when the two SDs happen to be the same.","","This is the ratio of spreads alone, 8.4 / 3.5 = 2.4, with r forgotten. That would be the slope only if the correlation were a perfect 1."],"trap":"Getting the ratio the wrong way up. Write \"SD of the predicted variable on top\" in the margin before you start, because the two answers 1.49 and 0.26 are both on the option list.","tri":"Symptom: r plus SD of x plus SD of y, and a slope wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: slope from r and the standard deviations. -&gt; Treatment: b = r x (SD of y / SD of x), where y is the variable being PREDICTED. To go backwards, r = b x (SD of x / SD of y)."},{"n":3,"q":"Anneke Visser finds a correlation of .54 between a selection test and later job performance across 180 applicants for a warehouse post. When she looks only at the 45 applicants who were actually hired, all of whom scored in the top band of the test, the correlation falls to .12 with p = .43. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["The selection test does not really predict performance, and the .54 was a fluke in the larger group.","The hired group covers only a narrow slice of the test scores, and squeezing the range of the predictor pushes the correlation towards zero.","The sample of 45 is smaller, and a smaller sample always produces a smaller correlation.","Performance must have caused the test scores in the full group but not in the hired group."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"restriction","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. The same two variables, two correlations, and the second group is a narrow slice of the first: top scorers, selected staff, a single ability band, one age group. That narrowing is the explanation. The correlation always drops.","plain":"In plain words: in the top band everybody scored about the same on the test, so the test has almost nothing left to separate them with. With no spread in x there is no pattern left to detect.","work":"Step 1. Compare the two groups. The 180 cover the whole range of test scores. The 45 cover only the top band.\nStep 2. Think about what r measures. It measures how much of the up-and-down in y travels with the up-and-down in x. If x barely moves, there is almost no up-and-down in x to travel with.\nStep 3. Picture the scatterplot. Take a long cigar-shaped cloud sloping upwards and cut off everything but the right-hand end. What is left looks like a round blob, and a round blob has r near zero.\nStep 4. Read the consequence. The .12 understates the test's value. The honest figure for the whole applicant pool is the .54.\nStep 5. Note the p-value. r = .12 with n = 45 gives p = .43, not significant. That is a loss of information, not evidence that the test is useless.\nExplain it to a friend: you cannot judge how well a ruler measures height if everyone you measure is exactly the same height.","wrongs":["The .54 came from 180 people across the full range and is the better estimate for the applicant pool. The restricted figure is the misleading one, not the other way round.","","Sample size affects the p-value and the precision of r, not its expected size. A small random sample of the same population would give about .54 too. What matters here is that the 45 are not a random slice: they are the top of the test distribution.","Nothing in the stem suggests the direction of causation changed between the two groups, and a direction cannot change just because you looked at a subgroup."],"trap":"Concluding the predictor is worthless because r collapsed in the selected group. Range restriction is the single most common reason a good selection test looks useless once people have been selected with it.","tri":"Symptom: r computed twice, once on the full range and once on a narrow slice of it, and the second r is smaller. -&gt; Diagnosis: restriction of range. -&gt; Treatment: say that cutting the spread of x cuts r, so the restricted r understates the relationship in the full population. It is an artefact of who was kept, not a real change in the relationship."},{"n":4,"q":"Oskar Lindqvist plots the same two variables for four different Swedish samples. His description of the four clouds of points is printed below. In which plot is the linear relationship strongest?","o":["Plot 1","Plot 2","Plot 3","Plot 4"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"scatter_strength","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. Four scatterplots and the word \"strongest\". Strength is about how TIGHTLY the points hug a line, not about how steep the line is. Steepness is the slope, and the slope has nothing to do with the size of r.","plain":"In plain words: imagine drawing the line, then measuring how far the points sit from it. The plot where the points cling closest to the line has the strongest correlation, however gentle that line is.","work":"Step 1. Separate the two features of a scatterplot. Steepness is the slope, and it depends on the units. Tightness is the strength, and that is what r measures.\nStep 2. Plot 1. Points almost exactly on a line, so very tight. Strength near 1, even though the line is gentle.\nStep 3. Plot 2. Steep but fuzzy. The steepness is tempting, but a wide cloud means poor prediction, so r is moderate at best.\nStep 4. Plot 3. Gentle and fuzzy. Weakest of the sloping three.\nStep 5. Plot 4. Points on a flat line means y hardly changes as x changes, so knowing x tells you nothing about y and the correlation is about zero, despite the points being tightly packed.\nStep 6. So Plot 1 wins.\nMemory hook: fat or thin, not flat or steep. Thin wins.","wrongs":["","Steep is not strong. The slope depends on the units, so changing study hours to minutes would make any line steeper without changing r by a hair. A wide cloud means weak prediction.","Gentle and fuzzy is the weakest of the three sloping plots. Fuzziness is exactly what lowers r.","A flat horizontal line means y stays the same whatever x does, so x carries no information about y. The points are tight, but tight around no relationship at all: r is about zero."],"trap":"Picking the steepest plot. Steepness is the slope and depends on the units of measurement. Strength is the scatter around the line and does not.","tri":"Symptom: several scatterplots and a question about which relationship is strongest, closest or most predictable. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading strength from a scatterplot. -&gt; Treatment: ignore the steepness completely. Judge the scatter around the line. Tight band means r near 1 or -1, fat cloud means r near 0. A flat line means y does not change with x at all, so there is no linear relationship.","out":{"cap":"Four scatterplots of the same two variables","h":["Plot","What the cloud of points looks like"],"r":[["Plot 1","The points sit almost exactly on a gently rising straight line."],["Plot 2","The points form a wide fuzzy cloud around a steeply rising straight line."],["Plot 3","The points form a wide fuzzy cloud around a gently rising straight line."],["Plot 4","The points sit almost exactly on a flat horizontal line."]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Fatima Zahra measures hours of volunteering and a loneliness score in 72 Moroccan students and runs a correlation. Her output is printed below. What was the null hypothesis that produced the printed significance value?","o":["That the correlation in her sample of 72 students is zero.","That the correlation in the population is .29.","That volunteering does not cause loneliness.","That the correlation in the population of all such students is zero."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"corr_hyp","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. A printed r with a significance value, and a question about the hypothesis. Every significance test of a correlation starts from the same H0: the POPULATION correlation is zero. The sample value is the evidence, never the hypothesis.","plain":"In plain words: the .013 answers one question only. If there were really no linear link at all among students in general, how often would a sample of 72 throw up a correlation as far from zero as .29?","work":"Step 1. Write the standard hypotheses. H0: rho = 0 in the population. H1: rho is not 0.\nStep 2. Notice what cannot be in H0. The sample correlation is computed from the data, so it is known to be .29. You never hypothesise about a number you have already measured.\nStep 3. Notice what the test does not touch. It is a test about a linear association, not about causation. No design question is being answered here.\nStep 4. Read the result in those terms. p = .013 is below .05, so she rejects H0 and concludes there is a non-zero linear association in the population.\nStep 5. So H0 is \"the population correlation is zero\".\nMemory hook: the hypothesis is always about the Greek letter, rho, not about the Roman one, r. You test the population value using the sample value.","wrongs":["The sample correlation is not hypothesised, it is calculated. It is .29, and everyone can see that. Hypotheses are about the unknown population value.","This puts the OBSERVED value into H0. H0 is always the no-relationship value, zero. Testing H0: rho = .29 would be a different and unusual exercise.","The test says nothing about causation. It tests whether a linear association exists in the population, and an association can exist whichever way the causal arrow points, or if it points neither way.",""],"trap":"Putting the sample value into H0. Remember the two alphabets: r is what you have, rho is what you are guessing about, and H0 always guesses zero.","tri":"Symptom: a printed correlation with a Sig. value, and a question about H0. -&gt; Diagnosis: null hypothesis for a Pearson correlation. -&gt; Treatment: H0 says the population correlation, rho, equals zero. H1 says it does not. Reject any option that talks about the sample, any option that puts the observed value in H0, and any option about causation.","out":{"cap":"Correlations: volunteering hours and loneliness score","h":["Statistic","Value"],"r":[["Pearson correlation","0.29"],["Sig. (2-tailed)","0.013"],["N","72"]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Rafael Dominguez has data from 2,400 individual Chilean pupils. Across the pupils, hours of sleep correlate .18 with a maths score. He then averages both variables within each of the 40 schools and correlates the 40 school averages, which gives .71. Which conclusion is justified?","o":["Sleep matters far more at school level than at pupil level, so policy should target whole schools.","Averaging strips out the pupil-to-pupil variation, so a correlation between group means is usually much higher. The .18 is the figure that describes pupils.","The .71 is the better estimate, because averages are measured more precisely than single pupils.","One of the two figures must be a calculation error, because the same data cannot give two different correlations."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"aggregation","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. The same two variables correlated twice, once over individuals and once over group averages, with the group figure much higher. The words \"averaged within each school\", \"by country\" or \"by region\" are the signal. Aggregation inflates r.","plain":"In plain words: when you replace 60 pupils by one school average, all the messiness between pupils disappears and only the smooth differences between schools are left. Smooth data give high correlations.","work":"Step 1. Count the data points. The first correlation has 2,400 points, one per pupil. The second has 40 points, one per school.\nStep 2. Think about what averaging does. Within a school, pupils vary a lot in both sleep and maths, and much of that variation has nothing to do with sleep. Averaging cancels it out.\nStep 3. See what is left. Only the differences between whole schools remain, and those are driven by the things schools differ in systematically, so the two averages track each other closely.\nStep 4. Decide which figure answers which question. For \"does a pupil who sleeps more score higher\", the answer is .18. For \"do schools where pupils sleep more score higher on average\", the answer is .71.\nStep 5. Note the size of the pupil-level effect. 0.18 squared = 0.0324, about 3 per cent of the variation in maths scores.\nExplain it to a friend: a photo of a crowd is blurry, a photo of 40 team averages is sharp. The crowd did not get tidier, you just stopped looking at individuals.","wrongs":["This is the ecological fallacy: reading a group-level correlation as a statement about individuals. It also smuggles in causation from correlational data.","","Precision is not the issue. The two correlations answer different questions about different units. The .71 is a perfectly accurate description of schools and a misleading one about pupils.","Both figures are correct. One describes 2,400 pupils, the other describes 40 schools. A data set can produce as many correlations as it has levels of aggregation."],"trap":"Treating the bigger correlation as the truer one. The level of aggregation decides what the number is about, and the question you were asked decides which level you need.","tri":"Symptom: two correlations from one data set, individuals against group averages, and the group figure is much larger. -&gt; Diagnosis: aggregation inflates r. -&gt; Treatment: say that the group-level r describes groups, not people, and that averaging removes within-group noise, which raises r. Reading the group figure as a statement about individuals is the ecological fallacy."},{"n":7,"q":"Priya Nair draws a straight line by hand through a scatterplot of 60 points, then compares it with the line her software produces. The software line is the least-squares line. What exactly does that line do better than any other straight line?","o":["It passes through more of the data points than any other line.","It makes the sum of the vertical gaps between the points and the line as small as possible.","It makes the sum of the SQUARED vertical gaps between the points and the line as small as possible.","It makes the sum of the shortest, perpendicular distances from the points to the line as small as possible."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"least_squares","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. A question about what makes the regression line the \"best\" line. Three words must all be present in the right answer: squared, vertical, smallest. Drop any one of them and the option is wrong.","plain":"In plain words: for every point, measure straight up or down to the line. That gap is the residual. Square all 60 residuals, add them up, and the regression line is the line that makes that total as small as it can be.","work":"Step 1. Define the residual. For point i, residual = observed y minus predicted y. That is a vertical distance, measured parallel to the y axis.\nStep 2. Why vertical and not perpendicular. The whole point of the line is to predict y from x. Error in prediction is error in y, so the gap is measured in the y direction.\nStep 3. Why squared. Residuals above the line are positive and below are negative. Unsquared they cancel, and in fact for the least-squares line they sum to exactly zero, so their total could never be a criterion. Squaring makes every miss count and punishes big misses hardest.\nStep 4. Put it together. The line is the one that minimises the sum of the squared vertical residuals, which is why it is called the least-squares line.\nStep 5. Note a consequence. Because the gaps are vertical, swapping x and y gives a different line, even though r is the same either way.\nMemory hook: least SQUARES, measured STRAIGHT UP AND DOWN. The name tells you the squares, the purpose tells you the direction.","wrongs":["The line is not chosen for passing through points, and with real data it usually passes through none of them. A wiggly curve could pass through all 60 and still be a terrible description.","The plain vertical gaps sum to exactly zero for the least-squares line, and for any line through the point of averages, so making that total small is no criterion at all. The gaps must be squared first.","","Perpendicular distances belong to a different technique. Regression measures error in the prediction of y, so the gap is vertical. This is also why predicting x from y gives a different line."],"trap":"Forgetting the word \"squared\", or measuring the gap perpendicular to the line because that looks like the honest shortest distance. Vertical and squared, both.","tri":"Symptom: a question about what the regression line minimises or why it is called \"best fitting\". -&gt; Diagnosis: the least-squares criterion. -&gt; Treatment: it minimises the SUM OF SQUARED VERTICAL residuals, where a residual is observed y minus predicted y. Check all three words in every option."},{"n":8,"q":"Katerina Pavlou reports that among 500 Cypriot teenagers, time spent on social media correlates .34 with a reported anxiety score. A newspaper writes that social media is making teenagers anxious. Which alternative reading of exactly the same data is just as consistent with what she found?","o":["Anxiety and social media use are unrelated, and the .34 arose by chance.","Social media use raises anxiety, but only among girls.","The correlation would have been stronger if she had measured anxiety more accurately.","Anxious teenagers turn to social media more, so anxiety drives the use rather than the other way round."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"reverse_caus","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. A causal story is told in one direction and the question asks for an equally good alternative reading. The quickest alternative is always the same arrow pointing backwards. Look for the option that swaps cause and effect.","plain":"In plain words: a correlation is symmetric. It cannot tell you which of the two variables moved first, so the story reads just as well backwards.","work":"Step 1. Write the newspaper claim as an arrow. Social media use causes anxiety.\nStep 2. Turn the arrow round. Anxiety causes social media use. Anxious teenagers may scroll for distraction, reassurance or company.\nStep 3. Ask whether the data can tell the two apart. Both variables were measured at the same time on the same people, so there is no information about which one moved first. Both arrows predict exactly the same correlation of .34.\nStep 4. Rule out the options that are not alternative READINGS of the same finding. An option saying the result is chance contradicts the finding rather than reinterpreting it, and with n = 500 an r of .34 is far too large to be plausible chance.\nStep 5. So the reverse arrow is the answer.\nExplain it to a friend: you see umbrellas and rain together. Umbrellas do not cause rain. Same picture, opposite arrow.","wrongs":["With 500 teenagers an r of .34 is very unlikely to be chance, and in any case \"it is not real\" is not an alternative reading of a real finding.","This keeps the newspaper's direction and just adds a subgroup. It is not an alternative to the causal claim, it is a narrower version of it, and nothing in the stem is about sex differences.","Measurement error usually makes a correlation smaller, so this might be true, but it is about the SIZE of r rather than about what causes what.",""],"trap":"Looking for a complicated alternative when the simplest one is the same arrow reversed. Always try the reverse arrow and a third variable first.","tri":"Symptom: a correlation plus a causal claim in one direction, and a question about alternative explanations. -&gt; Diagnosis: reverse causation. -&gt; Treatment: write the claim as X causes Y, then write Y causes X and check whether the data rule it out. In a cross-sectional study they never do, because nothing establishes which came first."},{"n":9,"q":"Diego Salas finds a correlation of .47 between the birth weight of 80 Peruvian newborns, recorded in kilograms, and the mother's haemoglobin level. A reviewer asks him to report birth weight in grams instead, and to add 2 to every haemoglobin reading so that the scale starts above zero. What is the correlation after those two changes?","o":[".47, because multiplying one variable by a positive constant and adding a constant to the other leaves the correlation untouched.","470, because weight is now a thousand times larger.",".22, because r has to be squared when the units change.","Lower than .47, because adding 2 to haemoglobin shifts every point away from the line."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"r_invariance","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. A correlation is given and then the variables are rescaled, shifted, converted to other units, or the axes are swapped. r does not care about any of that. The answer is almost always \"it stays the same\".","plain":"In plain words: r is computed from z scores, and a z score already divides out the units. Changing kilograms to grams changes every number but not a single z score.","work":"Step 1. Recall the formula in words. r is the average product of the z scores of x and y.\nStep 2. Trace the unit change. Each weight is multiplied by 1000. The mean is multiplied by 1000 and the standard deviation is multiplied by 1000, so every (value - mean) / SD is unchanged. The z scores do not move.\nStep 3. Trace the added constant. Adding 2 to every haemoglobin reading moves the mean up by 2 and leaves the standard deviation alone, so again every z score is unchanged.\nStep 4. Conclude. Both z score lists are identical, so r is identical: .47.\nStep 5. Note what DOES change. The regression slope changes, because the slope carries units: grams per unit of haemoglobin instead of kilograms per unit. The intercept changes too.\nMemory hook: r is unitless, so it ignores units. Rescaling stretches the picture but does not change how tightly the points hug the line.","wrongs":["","A correlation can never leave the range -1 to +1, so 470 is impossible on its face. Multiplying r by the conversion factor confuses r with the slope, which does carry units.","This squares r. 0.47 squared = 0.22, which is r squared, the share of variation explained. Squaring is something you do to answer a different question, not something that happens when units change.","Adding a constant slides every point sideways by the same amount, so the shape of the cloud and its tightness around the line are untouched. Nothing moves relative to anything else."],"trap":"Confusing r with the slope. The slope changes whenever units change, r never does. Ask yourself whether the quantity has units attached: r does not.","tri":"Symptom: an r, then a change of units, a constant added, or the axes swapped. -&gt; Diagnosis: what leaves r unchanged. -&gt; Treatment: r is unchanged by adding or subtracting a constant, by multiplying by a POSITIVE constant, and by swapping which variable is x. Multiplying by a NEGATIVE constant flips the sign only. The slope changes in all these cases, but r does not."},{"n":10,"q":"Jonas Weber reads that in a study of 150 German apprentices, a conscientiousness score accounts for 36 per cent of the variation in supervisor ratings, and that the relationship is positive. What was the correlation between the two variables? (2 decimals)","o":["0.13","0.18","0.36","0.60"],"a":3,"pct":30,"typ":"r_squared","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. The words \"accounts for\", \"explains\" or \"share of the variation\" plus a percentage mean you are being handed r SQUARED, not r. If the percentage is given and r is wanted, take the square root. If r is given and a percentage is wanted, square it.","plain":"In plain words: the 36 per cent is already the squared figure. To get back to the correlation you undo the squaring by taking the square root.","work":"Step 1. Turn the percentage into a proportion. 36 per cent = 0.36, and this is r squared.\nStep 2. Undo the squaring. The square root of 0.36 is 0.60.\nStep 3. Fix the sign. The stem says the relationship is positive, so r = +0.60. Without that information the square root alone could not tell you the sign, since both +0.60 and -0.60 square to 0.36.\nStep 4. Check it the other way round. 0.60 squared = 0.36, which is 36 per cent. Correct.\nStep 5. Answer 0.60.\nMemory hook: r squared is always the smaller number for an r between 0 and 1. If you are moving towards r, the number must get BIGGER.","wrongs":["This squares again instead of taking the root: 0.36 squared = 0.1296, so 0.13. It goes the wrong way round the relationship.","This halves instead of taking the square root: 0.36 / 2 = 0.18. Halving is not the inverse of squaring.","This reads the percentage straight off as the correlation. The 36 per cent is r squared. Reporting it as r is the classic confusion between the two.",""],"trap":"Reporting 0.36 because the number was in the question. Ask which quantity the percentage belongs to: \"variation explained\" is always r squared, so the correlation must be larger than 0.36, not equal to it.","tri":"Symptom: a percentage of variation explained, and r wanted, or r given and a percentage wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: r squared. -&gt; Treatment: r squared = share of variation in y explained, as a proportion. So r = square root of that proportion, with the sign taken from the stated direction of the relationship."}]},{"id":"t4b","name":"Correlation and regression - Set B","short":"Correlation and regression - Set B","blurb":"A second pass through the biggest theme. Reading a correlation output properly, getting r back out of a slope, lurking third variables, the share of variation explained, group averages, the sign that r and the slope must share, range restriction, what the line minimises, outliers, and what leaves r alone.","theme":4,"icon":"📈","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Hugo Martin measures hours of sleep and a burnout score in 64 French nurses. His output is printed below. Which statement may he make?","o":["Sleeping less causes burnout in nurses.","About 58 per cent of the variation in burnout is explained by hours of sleep.","Nurses who sleep less tend to score LOWER on burnout, and the link is statistically significant.","Nurses who sleep less tend to score HIGHER on burnout, a moderate to strong negative linear link that would be very unlikely if there were no link in the population."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"corr_interpret","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. A printed r with a significance value and four readings of it. Check three things in each option: the DIRECTION of the sign, whether r has been confused with r squared, and whether a causal verb has crept in.","plain":"In plain words: the minus sign means the two move in opposite directions. More sleep goes with less burnout, less sleep goes with more burnout. The tiny p-value says that pattern is very unlikely to be an accident of this sample.","work":"Step 1. Read the sign. r = -0.58 is negative, so as sleep goes up burnout goes down, and as sleep goes down burnout goes up.\nStep 2. Read the size. 0.58 is a moderate to strong correlation.\nStep 3. Read the p-value. Less than .001 means a sample correlation this far from zero would almost never occur if the population correlation were zero. So he rejects H0: rho = 0.\nStep 4. Check the share of variation. 0.58 squared = 0.3364, so about 34 per cent of the variation in burnout goes with sleep, not 58 per cent.\nStep 5. Check the design. Both variables were simply measured, so no causal verb is allowed. Exhausted nurses may sleep badly as a result of burnout, or shift patterns may drive both.\nStep 6. So the correct statement gives the right direction, calls the link moderate to strong and negative, and stops short of causation.\nExplain it to a friend: minus means a see-saw. One end goes up, the other goes down.","wrongs":["This is a causal claim from correlational data. Burnout could just as easily wreck sleep, or shift work could damage both.","This reads r where r squared belongs. The share of variation is 0.58 squared, which is 33.6 per cent, not 58 per cent.","The direction is back to front. A NEGATIVE correlation means less sleep goes with HIGHER burnout, not lower. Everything else in this option is fine, which is what makes it tempting.",""],"trap":"Misreading the minus sign under time pressure. Say the sentence out loud both ways: \"more sleep, less burnout\" and \"less sleep, more burnout\". Both must be true of a negative correlation.","tri":"Symptom: a correlation and a p-value, four interpretations. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting a correlation. -&gt; Treatment: read the sign for direction, the size for strength, the p-value for whether zero is plausible, and square r if a share of variation is being claimed. Never allow a causal verb.","out":{"cap":"Correlations: hours of sleep and burnout score","h":["Statistic","Value"],"r":[["Pearson correlation","-0.58"],["Sig. (2-tailed)","less than .001"],["N","64"]]}},{"n":2,"q":"Beatrix Nagy fits a line predicting monthly spending from monthly income for 150 Hungarian households. The slope of the line is 2.80. The standard deviation of income is 6.0 and the standard deviation of spending is 21.0. What is the correlation between income and spending? (2 decimals)","o":["0.13","0.64","0.80","9.80"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"slope_from_r","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. This is the slope formula run backwards: a slope and two standard deviations are given and r is wanted. Rearranging b = r x (SD of y / SD of x) gives r = b x (SD of x / SD of y). The ratio flips when you go this way.","plain":"In plain words: the slope is in real units, euros of spending per euro of income. Stripping the units off it turns it back into a correlation.","work":"Step 1. Identify y. She predicts spending, so spending is y with SD 21.0, and income is x with SD 6.0.\nStep 2. Start from the forward formula: b = r x (SD of y / SD of x).\nStep 3. Rearrange for r: r = b x (SD of x / SD of y).\nStep 4. Substitute: r = 2.80 x (6.0 / 21.0) = 2.80 x 0.285714 = 0.80.\nStep 5. Check the range. 0.80 is between -1 and +1, so it is a possible correlation. Check forwards too: 0.80 x (21.0 / 6.0) = 0.80 x 3.5 = 2.80, which is the given slope. Correct.\nStep 6. Answer 0.80.\nMemory hook: going from r to b you multiply by SD y over SD x. Coming back you flip the ratio. If the answer leaves the range -1 to +1, you flipped it the wrong way.","wrongs":["This divides the slope by the SD of spending and forgets the SD of income: 2.80 / 21.0 = 0.13. Both standard deviations appear in the formula.","This is 0.80 squared, that is r squared rather than r: 0.64. It answers the question \"what share of the variation is explained\", which was not asked.","","This uses the ratio upside down: 2.80 x (21.0 / 6.0) = 9.80. A correlation can never exceed 1, so this answer disqualifies itself, which is exactly the check to run."],"trap":"Flipping the ratio the wrong way and producing a value above 1. Any \"correlation\" outside -1 to +1 is a signal that the two standard deviations have swapped places.","tri":"Symptom: a slope plus both standard deviations, and r wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: slope and r, worked backwards. -&gt; Treatment: r = b x (SD of x / SD of y), where y is the PREDICTED variable. Then check that the answer lies between -1 and +1, which is the free safety net this question type gives you."},{"n":3,"q":"Omar Haddad finds that across 90 Tunisian neighbourhoods the number of pharmacies correlates .66 with the number of recorded burglaries. He wonders whether pharmacies attract burglars. What is the most likely reason for the correlation?","o":["Pharmacies hold medicines, so they really do attract burglars, and a correlation of .66 across 90 neighbourhoods proves it.","Neighbourhood population size drives both counts: bigger neighbourhoods have more of almost everything, pharmacies and burglaries included.","Burglaries cause pharmacies to open, because residents buy more medicine after a break-in.","The correlation must be a coincidence, since .66 arises easily by chance in a sample of only 90."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"confound","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. Two COUNTS correlated across places, areas, schools or countries. Whenever both variables are counts of things in an area, size of the area is the lurking variable, and the fix is to work with rates per head instead of raw counts.","plain":"In plain words: a big neighbourhood has more pharmacies and more burglaries simply because it has more people. The link between the two counts comes almost entirely from that.","work":"Step 1. Notice what kind of variables these are. Both are counts within a neighbourhood, not rates.\nStep 2. Find the variable that inflates both. Population. A neighbourhood of 40,000 will have more pharmacies AND more burglaries than one of 4,000, whatever the relationship between the two.\nStep 3. Check that this alone explains the correlation. Yes. Even with no link at all between pharmacies and crime, correlating two counts across places of very different sizes produces a large positive r.\nStep 4. Name the fix. Convert both to rates per 1,000 residents, or hold population constant statistically, and see whether anything is left.\nStep 5. So population size is the answer.\nExplain it to a friend: big towns have more dentists and more dog owners. Dentists do not cause dogs. Both just count people.","wrongs":["A correlation never proves a causal mechanism, and this one has an obvious alternative explanation sitting in plain sight.","","This is reverse causation, which is worth considering in general, but it cannot plausibly produce an r of .66 across whole neighbourhoods. The size of the area is the far more likely driver.","With 90 neighbourhoods an r of .66 is far too large to be chance: the p-value is below .001. The correlation is real, it is the causal reading that is wrong."],"trap":"Jumping to reverse causation when the real culprit is a size variable. With counts across places, always ask about population first, and remember the fix is to use rates.","tri":"Symptom: two variables correlate and a direct causal story is offered, but a third variable plausibly affects both. -&gt; Diagnosis: a confounding or lurking variable. -&gt; Treatment: name the third variable and show it would produce the correlation on its own. With counts across areas, that third variable is nearly always population size. Then say how to remove it: use rates per 1,000 residents."},{"n":4,"q":"Ingrid Olsen reports that in a sample of 88 Norwegian cyclists, weekly training hours correlate .43 with a fitness score, with p below .001. A coach asks her what share of the differences in fitness her training measure accounts for. What should she answer? (1 decimal)","o":["18.5 per cent","43.0 per cent","65.6 per cent","81.5 per cent"],"a":0,"pct":30,"typ":"r_squared","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. The words \"share of the differences\", \"variation accounted for\" or \"variance explained\" mean r SQUARED. The correlation is given, so square it first and only then turn it into a percentage.","plain":"In plain words: r tells you how tightly the points hug the line. Squaring it tells you what fraction of the ups and downs in fitness travel with training hours.","work":"Step 1. Square the correlation. 0.43 x 0.43 = 0.1849.\nStep 2. Turn it into a percentage. 0.1849 x 100 = 18.49, so 18.5 per cent to one decimal.\nStep 3. Say what it means. About 18.5 per cent of the variation in fitness scores goes with differences in training hours. The other 81.5 per cent goes with everything else, including genetics, age, diet and measurement error.\nStep 4. Keep the causal language out. This is a share of variation that is shared with training hours, not proof that training produced it.\nStep 5. Note the role of the p-value. p below .001 says the correlation is very unlikely to be zero in the population. It does not make the 18.5 per cent larger.\nMemory hook: square before you report a share. r is the relationship, r squared is the share.","wrongs":["","This reports r itself as a percentage. The share of variation is r squared, so squaring is not optional. Reporting 43 per cent more than doubles the real figure.","This takes the square ROOT of 0.43, which is 0.6557, instead of the square. The operation goes the other way: rooting is for turning a share back into a correlation.","This is 1 minus r squared, that is 81.5 per cent, the share of variation NOT accounted for. It is the leftover, not the answer."],"trap":"Reporting the correlation as the percentage. A significant correlation can still account for a small share of the variation, and 0.43 looks much more impressive than the 18.5 per cent it really buys.","tri":"Symptom: r given, a share or percentage of variation wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: r squared. -&gt; Treatment: square r, then multiply by 100. Going the other way, from a percentage to r, take the square root. Never report r itself as a percentage."},{"n":5,"q":"Carlos Rivera has survey data from 9,000 individual Colombian adults, where income correlates .22 with a happiness score. A colleague instead takes the average income and average happiness of each of the 32 departments and reports a correlation of .83. The colleague concludes that income matters much more for people than the individual figure suggested. What is wrong with that conclusion?","o":["Nothing is wrong. With 32 departments the estimate is cleaner than with 9,000 noisy individuals.","The two correlations are estimates of the same quantity, so the colleague should report the average of .22 and .83.","The department figure is unreliable because 32 is too small a sample for a correlation.","The two correlations describe different units. Averaging smooths away the differences between people, which inflates r, so .83 cannot be read as a statement about individuals."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"aggregation","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. One data set, two correlations, and the second one computed on averages per country, region, school or department. The group figure is almost always much larger. Reading it as a fact about individuals is the error.","plain":"In plain words: 32 department averages are 32 smooth summary numbers. All the disagreement between individual people has been averaged away, and what is left lines up neatly. Neat data give big correlations.","work":"Step 1. Count the units. The first correlation has 9,000 points, one per adult. The second has 32 points, one per department.\nStep 2. Think about what averaging removed. Within one department, adults differ enormously in income and in happiness, and much of that has nothing to do with income. Averaging cancels it.\nStep 3. See what is left. Only systematic differences between whole departments, which line up far more tidily, giving .83.\nStep 4. Match the figure to the question. The colleague asked about people, so the answer is .22. The .83 answers a question about departments.\nStep 5. Put a number on the individual effect. 0.22 squared = 0.0484, so under 5 per cent of the variation in individual happiness goes with income.\nStep 6. Name the error. Reading a group-level correlation as a statement about individuals is the ecological fallacy.\nExplain it to a friend: average out 300 people into one number 32 times over, and you have thrown away the noise. The noise was the people.","wrongs":["The group figure is not cleaner, it is about something else. It is an accurate description of departments and a misleading one about adults.","They are not two estimates of one quantity, so averaging them is meaningless. One describes adults, the other describes departments.","Thirty-two is small, but that affects the precision and the p-value, not the inflation. Even with 500 departments the aggregated correlation would still be much larger than the individual one.",""],"trap":"Assuming the larger correlation is the better one. Ask what one dot on the scatterplot stands for. If a dot is a department, the correlation is a fact about departments.","tri":"Symptom: an individual-level r and a much bigger group-average r from the same data, with a conclusion drawn about people. -&gt; Diagnosis: aggregation inflates r, and the ecological fallacy. -&gt; Treatment: say which unit each correlation describes, explain that averaging removes within-group variation, and insist that the individual-level question is answered by the individual-level r."},{"n":6,"q":"Mina Park is checking a classmate's regression homework. The classmate reports a correlation of -0.35 between daily screen time and hours of sleep, and a slope of +1.42 for the line predicting sleep from screen time. What should Mina say?","o":["Both can be right, because r and the slope measure different things and need not agree in sign.","Both can be right, because the slope depends on the units of measurement while r does not.","At least one of the two must be wrong, because r and the slope always carry the same sign.","The slope must be wrong, because with r = -0.35 the slope has to be -0.35 as well."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"r_and_slope","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. Both r and the slope are quoted and something looks off. The only thing these two must share is the SIGN. Their sizes can be anything, because the slope carries units and r does not.","plain":"In plain words: the slope is r stretched by the ratio of the two standard deviations, and standard deviations are always positive. Stretching by a positive number cannot change a minus into a plus.","work":"Step 1. Write the link. b = r x (SD of y / SD of x).\nStep 2. Note the sign of each piece. Standard deviations are never negative, so the ratio SD of y / SD of x is strictly positive.\nStep 3. Draw the conclusion. Multiplying a negative r by a positive ratio gives a negative b. So r = -0.35 forces a negative slope, and +1.42 is impossible.\nStep 4. Note what is NOT forced. The size. With SD of sleep = 1.2 and SD of screen time = 0.1, a correlation of -0.35 would give a slope of -4.2. Large slopes with small correlations are perfectly normal, so the 1.42 is not the problem, the plus sign is.\nStep 5. Picture it. A downward-sloping cloud cannot be summarised by an upward line. One of the two figures has to be wrong.\nMemory hook: same sign always, same size almost never.","wrongs":["They do measure different things, but the sign is the one thing they must agree on, because the stretching factor between them is always positive.","The first half is true and the second half does not follow. Units change the SIZE of the slope, never its sign.","","This demands that the two numbers be equal, which they almost never are. They coincide only if the two standard deviations happen to be identical. The error is in the sign, not in the magnitude."],"trap":"Thinking that a slope much bigger than r is itself suspicious. Only the sign is constrained. Check signs first, and never expect the two numbers to match.","tri":"Symptom: an r and a slope quoted together, with a question about whether they are consistent. -&gt; Diagnosis: how r and the slope relate. -&gt; Treatment: b = r x (SD of y / SD of x). Both SDs are positive, so b and r must share a sign. Their magnitudes need not match at all."},{"n":7,"q":"Tariq Aziz measures reading speed and reading comprehension in 200 adults and finds a correlation of .61. He then repeats the analysis on just the 38 adults in his sample who work as professional proof-readers, whose reading speeds are all very similar, and finds .09. What does the drop tell him?","o":["Very little about the real relationship. The proof-readers barely differ in reading speed, and a narrow range of the predictor pushes the correlation towards zero.","That speed and comprehension are unrelated among skilled readers, so the .61 must come entirely from unskilled readers.","That the relationship between speed and comprehension is curved rather than straight.","That 38 is too small a sample, and small samples always shrink a correlation towards zero."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"restriction","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. The same two variables, two correlations, and the second group is a narrow band of one variable: experts, top scorers, one age group, one ability band. The words \"all very similar\" or \"all in the top band\" give it away. r drops because the spread was cut.","plain":"In plain words: among the proof-readers, speed hardly varies at all. If x barely moves, there is no pattern between x and y left to find, so r collapses even though the underlying relationship has not changed.","work":"Step 1. Compare the groups. The 200 adults span the whole range of reading speeds. The 38 proof-readers are tightly bunched.\nStep 2. Recall what r needs. It needs variation in BOTH variables. The formula divides by the standard deviation of x, so if x hardly varies there is almost nothing for the correlation to pick up.\nStep 3. Picture the plot. Take the upward cigar-shaped cloud from the 200 adults and cut out a thin vertical strip. Inside that strip the points look like a round blob, and a blob gives r near zero.\nStep 4. Decide what the .09 tells him. Almost nothing about the relationship in general. It is a fact about a deliberately narrow group.\nStep 5. Note the p-value. With n = 38, r = .09 gives p = .59, nowhere near significant. That is a loss of information, not evidence of no relationship.\nExplain it to a friend: you cannot see how much taller people get with age if everyone you measure is 25.","wrongs":["","This treats the restricted figure as the honest one. In fact the .61, from the full range of speeds, is the better description of adults in general. The subgroup figure is the distorted one.","Nothing in the stem points to a curve. Range restriction flattens r whether the relationship is straight or not, and a curve would usually show up as a pattern in a residual plot rather than as a drop in a subgroup.","Sample size changes the precision of r and the p-value, not its expected size. A random sample of 38 adults from the full range would still average about .61. The problem is that these 38 were chosen for being alike on x."],"trap":"Reading the collapsed correlation as proof that the two variables are unrelated in the subgroup. Check the spread of the predictor in that subgroup first: if it is tiny, the correlation was never going to show anything.","tri":"Symptom: a full-range r and a much smaller r in a subgroup with little spread in x. -&gt; Diagnosis: restriction of range. -&gt; Treatment: say the restricted r understates the relationship in the full population, and that the narrowing of x is the cause, not a real change in the relationship."},{"n":8,"q":"Nkosi Dlamini fits a regression line predicting monthly electricity use from household size for 120 South African homes. Which statement about that line is FALSE?","o":["It is the line for which the sum of the squared vertical gaps between the points and the line is as small as possible.","It is the line for which the sum of the squared shortest distances from the points to the line, measured at right angles to the line, is as small as possible.","The gaps it works with are the residuals, that is observed electricity use minus predicted electricity use.","Swapping the variables round and predicting household size from electricity use would generally give a different line."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"least_squares","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. \"Which statement is FALSE\" about a regression line. Three of the four will be textbook facts. The usual false one swaps VERTICAL for perpendicular, or drops the word squared.","plain":"In plain words: the line is chosen to make the up-and-down misses as small as possible once they are squared. Distances measured at right angles to the line belong to a different method altogether.","work":"Step 1. Check the first statement. Squared, vertical, smallest. All three words present. True, and it is the definition.\nStep 2. Check the second. It says perpendicular. Regression measures error in PREDICTING y, so the gap runs parallel to the y axis, straight up and down. Perpendicular distances would mix error in x with error in y. False, so this is the answer.\nStep 3. Check the third. A residual is indeed observed minus predicted, measured in the units of y. True.\nStep 4. Check the fourth. Because the squared gaps are vertical, which variable sits on the y axis matters. Predicting x from y minimises a different set of gaps and generally gives a different line. True, and worth remembering alongside the fact that r itself is the same either way.\nStep 5. So the false statement is the perpendicular one.\nMemory hook: regression looks straight down, not sideways. Vertical gaps, squared.","wrongs":["True. This is the least-squares criterion, word for word.","","True. A residual is observed y minus predicted y, and it is exactly the vertical gap that gets squared.","True, and a useful fact. Vertical gaps depend on which variable is y, so the two regression lines differ. Only r stays the same when you swap them."],"trap":"Choosing the perpendicular distance because it sounds like the fairest measure of how far a point is from a line. Regression is about predicting y, so it only cares about how wrong the prediction of y is.","tri":"Symptom: four statements about the regression line, one of them false. -&gt; Diagnosis: the least-squares criterion. -&gt; Treatment: the line minimises the sum of SQUARED VERTICAL residuals, where a residual is observed y minus predicted y. Anything about perpendicular distances is false, and so is anything that drops the squaring."},{"n":9,"q":"Lena Fischer has 25 points with a correlation of .74. She then discovers that one of the 25 was entered wrongly: the point sits at a high value of x but a very low value of y, far below the rest of the cloud. After she corrects the entry, that point joins the main cloud. What is the most likely effect on the correlation?","o":["It will fall, because correcting an error always reduces a correlation.","It will stay at .74, because one point out of 25 has almost no influence.","It will change sign, because the point was on the wrong side of the line.","It will rise, because the faulty point ran against the overall upward trend and was dragging the correlation down."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"outliers","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. One unusual point and a question about what it does to r. There is no fixed rule: it depends on WHERE the point sits. A point far out along the trend inflates r, a point far out against the trend deflates it. Decide which by comparing the point with the direction of the cloud.","plain":"In plain words: the rest of the points rise together. This one sat high on x but low on y, which is the opposite pattern, so it was arguing against the trend and holding r down. Remove that argument and r goes up.","work":"Step 1. Find the trend. r = .74 with the bad point still in, so the cloud rises: high x goes with high y.\nStep 2. Place the bad point. High x, very low y. That is the opposite corner from where the trend would put it.\nStep 3. Work out what it was doing. It pulled the fitted line flatter and added a large squared residual, which weakens the correlation. So the .74 was achieved in spite of it.\nStep 4. Work out the effect of the fix. Once corrected, the point agrees with the rest, so the cloud becomes tighter and r rises above .74.\nStep 5. Note the role of n. With only 25 points, one point carries real weight, so the change can be substantial.\nExplain it to a friend: one person shouting the opposite was dragging the average opinion towards the middle. Correct their vote and the group looks much more agreed.","wrongs":["There is no such rule. Correcting an error can raise or lower r depending on where the faulty point was and where the corrected one lands.","With n = 25 a single extreme point has considerable influence, and this one sat far from the cloud in the direction that fights the trend. Influence depends on position as well as on n.","A sign change would need the whole pattern to reverse. Fixing one point cannot turn a rising cloud of 25 points into a falling one.",""],"trap":"Believing that outliers always weaken a correlation. A point far out ALONG the trend line inflates r, sometimes dramatically. Always locate the point relative to the trend before deciding.","tri":"Symptom: a single extreme or mistyped point and a question about its effect on r. -&gt; Diagnosis: the influence of an outlier. -&gt; Treatment: find the direction of the main cloud. A point that extends the trend raises r. A point that contradicts the trend lowers r. With a small n, a single point can move r a long way."},{"n":10,"q":"Santiago Ortiz finds a correlation of -0.62 between daily commuting time in minutes and hours of sleep for 140 Argentinian workers. He now redraws the plot with sleep on the horizontal axis and commuting time on the vertical axis, and converts commuting time from minutes to hours. What is the new correlation? (2 decimals where needed)","o":["-0.62, because r does not depend on which variable goes on which axis, nor on the units either variable is measured in.","+0.62, because swapping the axes reverses the sign of the relationship.","-0.0103, because commuting time has been divided by 60.","0.38, because r has to be squared once the units change."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"r_invariance","theme":4,"spot":"HINT. An r is given, then the axes are swapped, the units changed, or a constant added. None of that touches r. Note the contrast with the slope, which changes under every one of those operations.","plain":"In plain words: r is built from z scores, and swapping the axes just swaps the two lists of z scores round before multiplying them. Multiplication does not care about order, and changing units does not change a z score at all.","work":"Step 1. Recall the formula in words. r is the average of the product of the z score of x and the z score of y.\nStep 2. Swap the axes. The product z of x times z of y is the same as z of y times z of x, so r is identical. It is symmetric in the two variables by construction.\nStep 3. Change the units. Dividing every commuting time by 60 divides the mean by 60 and the standard deviation by 60, so each (value - mean) / SD is unchanged. The z scores do not move.\nStep 4. Combine both changes. Neither one touches a single z score, so r stays at -0.62, minus sign included.\nStep 5. Note what DOES change. The regression slope changes under both operations, and the two regression lines for the two ways round are different lines. r is the one number that stays put.\nMemory hook: r is symmetric and unitless. Swap it, stretch it, shift it: r shrugs.","wrongs":["","Swapping the axes does not change the sign. Workers who commute longer still sleep less however you draw the plot. Only multiplying a variable by a negative number flips the sign.","This divides r by 60 as though r carried the units of commuting time. It does not. The SLOPE would change by a factor of 60, but r is unitless.","This squares r: 0.62 squared = 0.3844. Squaring r gives the share of variation explained, which is a different quantity, and it is not something a change of units triggers."],"trap":"Expecting the sign to flip when the axes are swapped. Direction is a fact about the two variables, not about the drawing. Only the slope, not r, is affected by which variable you chose to predict.","tri":"Symptom: an r, then swapped axes or changed units. -&gt; Diagnosis: what leaves r unchanged. -&gt; Treatment: r survives swapping x and y, adding a constant, and multiplying by a positive constant. Only multiplying one variable by a NEGATIVE constant flips the sign. The slope changes in all these cases."}]},{"id":"t5a","name":"Inference for regression - Set A","short":"Inference for regression - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on what a regression output lets you say: working n back out of an ANOVA table, reading a residual plot, telling the two kinds of interval apart, building F out of mean squares rather than sums of squares, and what a significant overall F does and does not prove.","theme":5,"icon":"📉","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Nadia Haddad predicts first-year study success from three background variables. Part of her output was lost in printing and only the shaded cells below survived. How many students were in her sample?","o":["136","100","99","97"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"backout_n","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. The table has holes in it and the question asks for n, not for a test result. Whenever you are asked to work BACKWARDS to a sample size, your route always runs through df error, because df error is the only cell that contains n.","plain":"In plain words: you cannot see n anywhere, but df error hides it, because df error = n - p - 1. So the job is to rebuild df error from the pieces you can see, then add p and 1 back on.","work":"Step 1. Get MSE from F. F = MS model / MSE, so MSE = MS model / F = 240.0 / 12.00 = 20.0.\nStep 2. Get SS model from MS model. MS model = SS model / df model, and df model = p = 3, so SS model = 3 x 240.0 = 720.0.\nStep 3. Get SS error by subtraction. SS error = SST - SS model = 2640.0 - 720.0 = 1920.0.\nStep 4. Get df error. df error = SS error / MSE = 1920.0 / 20.0 = 96.\nStep 5. Unpack n. df error = n - p - 1, so 96 = n - 3 - 1, so n = 100.\nStep 6. Sense check. df total should be n - 1 = 99, and 3 + 96 = 99. It balances.\nMemory hook: F is the ladder from MS model down to MSE, and df error is the ladder from SS error down to n.","wrongs":["This forgot step 3. Dividing the TOTAL sum of squares by MSE gives 2640.0 / 20.0 = 132, and 132 + 3 + 1 = 136. SS model has to come off first, because df error belongs to the residual row only.","","This used df error = n - p and got n = 96 + 3 = 99. The extra 1 is for the intercept, which also costs a degree of freedom. With 3 predictors a regression estimates 4 numbers, not 3.","This used df error = n - 1 and got n = 96 + 1 = 97, the rule for a one sample t test. In regression every predictor costs its own degree of freedom."],"trap":"Dividing SST instead of SS error by MSE. The residual row and the total row are different rows. Always subtract SS model first.","tri":"Symptom: a regression ANOVA table with missing cells and a question about the sample size. -&gt; Diagnosis: work n back out of df error. -&gt; Treatment: 1 MSE = MS model / F, 2 SS model = p x MS model, 3 SS error = SST - SS model, 4 df error = SS error / MSE, 5 n = df error + p + 1.","out":{"cap":"Surviving cells of the regression ANOVA table (3 predictors)","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Regression","?","3","240.0","12.0"],["Residual","?","?","?",""],["Total","2640.0","?","",""]]}},{"n":2,"q":"Henrik Dahl regresses household electricity use on floor area for 240 Danish homes. When he plots the residuals against the predicted values, the points sit in a narrow band on the left of the plot and spread out into a wide fan on the right, with no bend in the middle. What does this tell him?","o":["The spread of the residuals is not constant, so the standard errors, the p-values and the intervals from this model cannot be trusted.","The relationship between floor area and electricity use is curved, so a straight line is the wrong model.","The residuals are not normally distributed, which makes the slope estimate biased.","Nothing is wrong, because a fan shape is what you expect when the sample is large."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"resid_plot","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Residuals plotted against predicted values, and the stem describes a SHAPE. Learn two shapes only. A fan or funnel that widens means unequal spread. A bend, arch or U means the straight line is the wrong form. The stem here says \"no bend in the middle\", which rules the second one out for you.","plain":"In plain words: the model misses by a little for small houses and by a lot for big houses. The line itself is fine, but the wobble around it changes size, and every standard error in the output assumed that wobble was one fixed size.","work":"Step 1. Name what the plot shows. The width of the band grows as the predicted value grows. That is a funnel, not a curve.\nStep 2. Match it to a condition. Regression inference needs the residuals to have the same spread at every value of x. This plot says they do not.\nStep 3. Work out the consequence. Every standard error is built from one single MSE, which is one single estimate of that spread. If the spread is not one number, that estimate is wrong everywhere.\nStep 4. Be careful about what is NOT broken. The fitted line is still a reasonable summary of the average relationship. It is the uncertainty around it that is misreported.\nExplain it to a friend: the line is roughly in the right place, but the error bars printed next to it are the wrong size.","wrongs":["","A curve would show as a bend or an arch in the residuals. The stem says explicitly that there is no bend. This is the other classic shape, offered here to see whether you read the description.","A fan shape is about spread, not about shape of the distribution. Also, non-normal residuals do not bias the slope. They affect the p-values in small samples.","A large sample does not create a funnel. n = 240 makes the estimates precise, it does not excuse a broken condition."],"trap":"Reading every ugly residual plot as \"not linear\". Ask one question first: is the MIDDLE of the band drifting (a curve), or is the WIDTH of the band changing (unequal spread)?","tri":"Symptom: a residual plot that fans out, with no curve. -&gt; Diagnosis: non-constant variance, also called heteroscedasticity. -&gt; Treatment: say the equal spread condition fails, so the standard errors are wrong, which makes every t test, p-value and interval in the output unreliable. The slope itself stays unbiased."},{"n":3,"q":"Priya Raman predicts a toddler vocabulary score from the number of minutes a parent reads aloud each day. For a reading time of 20 minutes her software prints two 95 per cent intervals: (62.4, 68.0) and (51.3, 79.1). Which interval is the one for a single new toddler whose parent reads for 20 minutes, and why?","o":["(62.4, 68.0), because an interval about one individual uses only that individual's own value of x.","(62.4, 68.0), because an interval about one individual is always the more precise of the two.","(51.3, 79.1), because it is wide enough to hold 95 per cent of the toddler scores that were used to fit the line.","(51.3, 79.1), because a single toddler also scatters around the line, on top of the uncertainty about where the line is."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"two_intervals","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Two intervals printed at the SAME value of x, and you must say which is which. You never need to calculate. The wider one is always the one about a single individual, the narrower one is always the one about the average response. Check that they share the same midpoint, and you are done.","plain":"In plain words: guessing an average is easier than guessing one child. Both intervals sit around the same prediction, but the one for a single child has to carry an extra source of variation, so it has to be wider.","work":"Step 1. Check the centres. (62.4 + 68.0) / 2 = 65.2 and (51.3 + 79.1) / 2 = 65.2. Same predicted value, so both belong to x = 20 in this model.\nStep 2. Measure the widths. 68.0 - 62.4 = 5.6 points. 79.1 - 51.3 = 27.8 points.\nStep 3. Decide which uncertainty is bigger. An interval for the MEAN response only has to pin down where the line sits. An interval for ONE toddler has to do that AND allow for how far an individual child scatters around the line.\nStep 4. Two sources of error beat one, so the individual interval is the wider one: (51.3, 79.1).\nMemory hook: individuals are unpredictable, averages are tame. Wide means one person.","wrongs":["The first half picks the narrow interval, which is the interval for the mean. The reason given is also empty: both intervals are computed at the same x.","This has the logic exactly backwards. Predicting one individual is the harder job, so it is the less precise one.","The interval is the right one but the reason is not. A prediction interval is about where ONE future toddler will land, not about holding 95 per cent of the scores already in the sample. That second idea is a description of the data, and it is not what the model prints.",""],"trap":"Choosing the narrow interval because \"a single toddler is one number and feels more specific\". Specific is not the same as certain. The extra scatter of an individual always makes that interval wider.","tri":"Symptom: two intervals at one value of x, and the question asks which belongs to an individual. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for the mean response against prediction interval for one observation. -&gt; Treatment: same centre, two widths. Wider = prediction interval for one individual. Narrower = confidence interval for the mean response."},{"n":4,"q":"Tomasz Nowak predicts a wellbeing score from hours of sleep and hours of screen time in 83 adults. The model summary and the coefficients are printed below. Which one of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["The two predictors together explain about 34 per cent of the variance in wellbeing scores.","The model as a whole is significantly related to wellbeing, F(2, 80) = 20.28, p &lt; .001.","With screen time held constant, each extra hour of sleep goes with a rise of 2.45 points in wellbeing.","Because the overall F test is significant, each of the two predictors separately makes a significant contribution."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"read_output","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. A printed regression output plus \"which statement is FALSE\". Do not read the options first. Read the output and write three facts on your scratch paper: what R squared is, whether the overall F is significant, and which individual p-values are below .05. Then test each option against your notes.","plain":"In plain words: three of these four sentences are things the output really says, and one is a sentence students add by themselves. Find the one the output does not support.","work":"Step 1. R squared = .336, so the model accounts for 33.6 per cent of the variance, which rounds to about 34 per cent. Statement a is true.\nStep 2. F(2, 80) = 20.28 with p &lt; .001 is far below .05, so the set of two predictors is significantly related to wellbeing. Statement b is true.\nStep 3. b for sleep is 2.45. In multiple regression every slope is read with the other predictors held constant, so statement c is exactly the right wording. True.\nStep 4. Now the individual rows. Sleep has t = 2.45 / 0.52 = 4.71, p &lt; .001, so it is significant. Screen time has t = -0.31 / 0.28 = -1.11, p = .272, which is well above .05, so it is NOT significant.\nStep 5. So statement d is false. A significant overall F says at least one predictor is pulling its weight, never that all of them are.\nExplain it to a friend: the team won the match, but that does not mean every player scored.","wrongs":["True. R squared of .336 is 33.6 per cent, which is about 34 per cent of the variance.","True, and read off the output directly. p &lt; .001 is below any usual alpha.","True, and the \"holding the other predictor constant\" clause is what makes it the correct reading of a multiple regression slope.",""],"trap":"Letting a significant overall F settle the individual predictors. Screen time has p = .272 printed right there. Always look down the t column before you say anything about single predictors.","tri":"Symptom: regression output and a hunt for the false claim. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading output, with the overall test and the individual tests kept apart. -&gt; Treatment: check R squared as a percentage, check the F row for the whole model, then check each t row on its own. The overall F and the individual t tests answer different questions and can disagree.","out":{"cap":"Regression output: R = .580, R squared = .336, F(2, 80) = 20.28, p &lt; .001","h":["Predictor","b","SE(b)","t","p"],"r":[["(Constant)","31.6","4.18","7.56","&lt; .001"],["Hours of sleep","2.45","0.52","4.71","&lt; .001"],["Hours of screen time","-0.31","0.28","-1.11",".272"]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Elena Vasquez predicts monthly supermarket spending from four household characteristics in a Mexican consumer panel. Her software printed the sums of squares and the degrees of freedom but not the test statistic. What is the value of the statistic for the overall test of her model? (2 decimals)","o":["0.28","8.31","33.23","0.12"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"f_from_output","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. An ANOVA table with the SS and df columns filled in and the F cell empty. The whole point of this question type is that you must go through the MS column first. If you can divide two numbers straight out of the SS column, you are being tested on exactly that temptation.","plain":"In plain words: F compares two AVERAGES of squared variation, not two totals. So turn each sum of squares into a mean square by dividing by its own df, and only then divide one by the other.","work":"Step 1. MS model. 1296.0 / 4 = 324.0.\nStep 2. MS error. 4680.0 / 120 = 39.0.\nStep 3. F. 324.0 / 39.0 = 8.3077, so F = 8.31.\nStep 4. Sense check. F is a ratio of two variances, so under a true null it should sit near 1. A value of 8.31 is comfortably above 1, which fits a model that explains something. Anything near 0 would be impossible for a model with this much explained variation.\nMemory hook: F stands for Fair comparison. Each side has to be divided by its own df first, or you are comparing 4 pieces of information with 120 pieces of information.","wrongs":["This is SS model / SS error = 1296.0 / 4680.0 = 0.28, the sums of squares divided without ever forming the mean squares. It is the single most common error in this question type.","","This is SS model / MS error = 1296.0 / 39.0 = 33.23. The denominator was turned into a mean square but the numerator was left as a sum of squares. Both sides must be mean squares.","This is the ratio upside down: MS error / MS model = 39.0 / 324.0 = 0.12. F always has the model term on top."],"trap":"Dividing SS by SS. It gives a number that looks like a proportion, and students accept it because 0.28 resembles an R squared. Write the MS column out in full before you divide.","tri":"Symptom: SS and df given, F wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: F from an ANOVA table. -&gt; Treatment: MS model = SS model / df model, MS error = SS error / df error, then F = MS model / MS error. Never SS model / SS error.","out":{"cap":"Regression ANOVA table, test statistic missing","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Regression","1296.0","4","?","?"],["Residual","4680.0","120","?",""],["Total","5976.0","124","",""]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Kwame Mensah first predicts graduate starting salary from degree grade alone and reports a correlation of .42 between the two. He then adds internship months as a second predictor, and the two-predictor model reports R squared = .31. How much extra variance in salary does internship months account for? (1 decimal, as a percentage)","o":["11.0 per cent","17.6 per cent","13.4 per cent","31.0 per cent"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"r2_change","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Two models, and the first one is described with a CORRELATION while the second is described with an R SQUARED. That mismatch is deliberate. Before you subtract anything, put both models on the same scale by squaring the correlation.","plain":"In plain words: variance explained is always the squared quantity. Model one gave you r, which is not yet a share of variance. Square it, then take it away from the bigger model to see what the new predictor added.","work":"Step 1. Put model one on the variance scale. r = .42, so R squared for model one = .42 x .42 = .1764.\nStep 2. Read off model two. R squared = .31.\nStep 3. Subtract. .31 - .1764 = .1336.\nStep 4. As a percentage, .1336 x 100 = 13.36, which rounds to 13.4 per cent.\nStep 5. Sense check. The second model must explain at least as much as the first, so the difference has to be positive and smaller than .31. It is.\nMemory hook: r is a direction and a strength, r squared is a share of a cake. You can only subtract cake from cake.","wrongs":["This subtracted r from R squared: .31 - .42 = -.11, read as 11 per cent. It mixes two different scales, which is why it even comes out negative. That negative sign is your warning bell.","This is R squared for model one on its own: .42 x .42 = .1764, or 17.6 per cent. That is what degree grade already explained, not what internship months added.","","This is R squared for model two on its own, 31.0 per cent. That is what both predictors explain together, so the first model has not been subtracted out."],"trap":"Treating .42 as if it were already a proportion of variance. Any time a stem says \"correlation of\", square it before it goes anywhere near a subtraction.","tri":"Symptom: r for one model, R squared for a bigger model, and a question about extra or additional variance. -&gt; Diagnosis: change in R squared. -&gt; Treatment: 1 square r to get R squared for model one, 2 subtract it from R squared for model two, 3 turn the difference into a percentage."},{"n":7,"q":"Sofia Lindqvist predicts sleep quality from screen time, caffeine intake and exercise minutes in 100 Swedish adults, and reports F(3, 96) = 5.12 with p = .002. What may she conclude from this result alone?","o":["At least one of the three predictors has a slope that is not zero in the population.","All three predictors have slopes that are not zero in the population.","Screen time, being the predictor she cares about most, has a slope that is not zero.","The three predictors together explain a large share of the variance in sleep quality."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"overall_f","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. An F with two df values and a p-value, then \"what may she conclude\". This is the overall or omnibus test. Its answer is always the same shape: AT LEAST ONE slope is non-zero. Any option with \"all\" or with a named single predictor is wrong by construction.","plain":"In plain words: the overall F asks one yes or no question, namely \"does this set of predictors, taken as a bundle, predict the outcome better than nothing at all?\". A yes does not say who in the bundle did the work.","work":"Step 1. Write down the null the F tests. H0: beta1 = beta2 = beta3 = 0, so the model predicts nothing at all.\nStep 2. Write down its opposite. Ha: at least one beta is not 0. Note that the opposite of \"all are zero\" is \"not all are zero\", never \"none is zero\".\nStep 3. Compare p with alpha. p = .003 is below .05, so reject H0.\nStep 4. State the conclusion in the words of Ha. At least one of the three slopes differs from zero in the population.\nStep 5. Note the limit. The F test cannot tell her which one, and it says nothing about how big the effect is.\nExplain it to a friend: the smoke alarm went off, so at least one room has smoke in it. The alarm will not tell you which room.","wrongs":["","This is the \"all\" version of the conclusion, the same slip as reading \"not all means are equal\" as \"all means differ\". A significant F is satisfied by one single non-zero slope.","Caring about a predictor is not evidence about it. To say something about screen time on its own she must look at its own t test and p-value.","A significant F says the share of variance explained is more than you would expect by chance, not that it is large. With n = 100 and 3 predictors, R squared here is only about .14."],"trap":"Upgrading \"at least one\" into \"all\". Read the null first: it is a statement about every slope at once, so breaking it needs only one exception.","tri":"Symptom: a significant overall F in a multiple regression and a conclusion to choose. -&gt; Diagnosis: what the omnibus F tests. -&gt; Treatment: H0 says every slope is zero. Rejecting H0 means NOT every slope is zero, which is the same as at least one is non-zero. To name which, you need the individual t tests."},{"n":8,"q":"Rafael Costa predicts weekly running distance from resting heart rate. The mean resting heart rate in his sample is 68 beats per minute. He builds a 95 per cent interval for the mean running distance of all runners with a heart rate of 68, and another at a heart rate of 50. Which statement is true?","o":["The two intervals have the same width, because both use the same model and the same confidence level.","The interval at 50 is narrower, because fewer runners have a heart rate that low so there is less variation to allow for.","The interval at 50 is wider, because 50 lies far from the mean of x and the line is least well pinned down out there.","The interval at 50 cannot be built at all, because 50 is not the mean of x."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"two_intervals","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. The same kind of interval built at TWO different values of x, with the mean of x mentioned in the stem. That mention is the giveaway. The rule is: both kinds of interval are narrowest at the mean of x and get wider as you move away from it in either direction.","plain":"In plain words: the regression line is anchored at the middle of your data, like a see-saw resting on its pivot. Near the pivot a small tilt moves the line hardly at all. Out at the ends the same tilt moves it a lot, so you are less sure where the line is out there.","work":"Step 1. Locate the two values of x. The first is 68, which is exactly the mean of x. The second is 50, which is 18 beats below the mean.\nStep 2. Recall what drives the width. The standard error of a fitted value contains a term that grows with (x - x-bar) squared. At x = x-bar that term is zero, which is as small as it can get.\nStep 3. Apply it. At x = 68 the term is zero, so that interval is the narrowest the model can give. At x = 50 the term is positive, so that interval is wider.\nStep 4. Choose the option that says the interval at 50 is wider for that reason.\nMemory hook: picture a bow tie around the regression line. It is pinched in the middle and flares at both ends.","wrongs":["Same model and same confidence level, but the width still changes with x. This option treats the interval as if it had one fixed width along the whole line, which would make the bow tie a straight tube.","This swaps the direction. Having fewer observations near x = 50 is exactly what makes the line LESS well pinned down there, so the interval gets wider, not narrower.","","Intervals can be built at any value of x inside the observed range. The mean of x is just where they happen to be narrowest."],"trap":"Assuming an interval has one width for the whole line. Both kinds of interval flare out at the edges of the data, and the further you go the less you should trust them.","tri":"Symptom: one interval at the mean of x and one far from it. -&gt; Diagnosis: how interval width depends on the distance from x-bar. -&gt; Treatment: width is smallest at x = x-bar and grows with the distance from x-bar. This holds for the confidence interval for the mean response and for the prediction interval alike."},{"n":9,"q":"Mei Chen fits a model that predicts job satisfaction from five workplace measures in a sample of 64 employees. Which pair of degrees of freedom belongs to the test of her model as a whole?","o":["(5, 63)","(5, 58)","(6, 57)","(5, 59)"],"a":1,"pct":30,"typ":"df_regression","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. A number of predictors, a sample size, and a request for degrees of freedom. Count two things only: p, how many predictors, and n, how many cases. Then the pair is (p, n - p - 1) and nothing else.","plain":"In plain words: the first number is how many predictors you used. The second is how much information is left over once the model has spent some. The model spends one degree of freedom per predictor plus one for the intercept.","work":"Step 1. Identify p. Five workplace measures, so p = 5. That is the first df.\nStep 2. Identify n. Sixty-four employees, so n = 64.\nStep 3. Apply the rule. df error = n - p - 1 = 64 - 5 - 1 = 58.\nStep 4. Write the pair in order, model first: (5, 58).\nStep 5. Sense check. The two df values should add up to n - 1 = 63, and 5 + 58 = 63. It balances.\nMemory hook: the model estimates 6 numbers, five slopes and one intercept, so 6 degrees of freedom are spent and 64 - 6 = 58 survive.","wrongs":["This used n - 1 = 63 for the error df, the rule from a one sample t test. In regression every predictor costs a degree of freedom as well.","","This counted 6 predictors by adding the intercept into the first number, then took 64 - 6 - 1 = 57. The intercept belongs in the minus 1 of the error df, not in p.","This used n - p = 64 - 5 = 59 and forgot the intercept. Check with the balance rule: 5 + 59 = 64, which is n, not n - 1, so it cannot be right."],"trap":"Dropping the minus 1. Use the balance check every time: df model plus df error must come to n - 1.","tri":"Symptom: p and n given, df pair wanted for a multiple regression. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for the overall F. -&gt; Treatment: df model = p, df error = n - p - 1, written in that order. The minus 1 is the intercept and it is never optional."},{"n":10,"q":"Ayla Demir predicts house price from floor area in square metres and from the number of rooms. The two predictors correlate at .93. Her overall test is clearly significant, yet neither predictor reaches significance in its own test. What explains this pattern?","o":["She has made an arithmetic error, because a significant overall test cannot occur when no predictor is significant.","The sample is too small, so the overall test has too little power to be trusted.","The two predictors cancel each other out, so together they explain nothing about house price.","The two predictors carry almost the same information, which inflates their standard errors and so shrinks their individual t values."],"a":3,"pct":30,"typ":"multicollinearity","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Two predictors with a very high correlation, a significant overall test and non-significant individual tests. That exact combination has one name. Watch for phrases like \"correlate at .9\", \"measure much the same thing\", or a tolerance close to zero.","plain":"In plain words: floor area and number of rooms are almost the same variable wearing different clothes. The model can see that size predicts price, but it cannot tell which of the two twins deserves the credit, so it says \"not sure\" about each one separately.","work":"Step 1. Spot the clue. A correlation of .93 between two predictors is very high and means they overlap almost completely.\nStep 2. See what that does to a slope. Each slope is the effect of one predictor with the other held constant. When the two move together, there is hardly any data in which one changes while the other stands still, so there is almost nothing left to estimate that unique effect from.\nStep 3. See what that does to the standard error. Thin information means a large SE(b). Since t = b / SE(b), a large SE drags t down towards zero and pushes p up.\nStep 4. See why F survives. The overall F asks only whether the bundle predicts price. The bundle does, very clearly. It never has to split the credit.\nStep 5. So the pattern is normal and expected, not a mistake.\nExplain it to a friend: two witnesses who always tell the identical story do prove the event happened, but you cannot say which of them you learned it from.","wrongs":["This pattern is a well known and perfectly possible outcome. It is the standard fingerprint of multicollinearity, not an arithmetic slip.","A small sample would weaken the overall test too, but her overall test is clearly significant. The problem is the overlap between the predictors, not the number of houses.","They explain a great deal about house price together, which is exactly why the overall test is significant. What is unclear is only how to divide the credit between them.",""],"trap":"Concluding that neither floor area nor room count matters for price. The slopes are uncertain, not zero. Both predictors are strongly related to price on their own.","tri":"Symptom: predictors that correlate strongly, overall F significant, individual t tests not. -&gt; Diagnosis: multicollinearity. -&gt; Treatment: say that the shared information inflates the standard errors of the slopes. Since t = b / SE(b), a bigger SE gives a smaller t and a bigger p. The overall F is untouched because it only asks about the predictors as a set."}]},{"id":"t5b","name":"Inference for regression - Set B","short":"Inference for regression - Set B","blurb":"Ten more questions on regression inference, with the shapes mixed up: count the degrees of freedom, rebuild a lost sample size, read a curved residual plot, decide what a significant and a non-significant overall F each allow, spot the false claim in an output, and separate r from R squared.","theme":5,"icon":"📉","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Jonas Holm predicts the annual maintenance cost of a rental flat from six features of the building, using records from 150 flats. How many degrees of freedom belong to the residual row of his output? (whole number)","o":["143","144","142","149"],"a":0,"pct":30,"typ":"df_regression","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. A sample size, a count of predictors, and the word residual, error or within. Only one formula applies: df error = n - p - 1. Count the predictors carefully and do not let the outcome variable sneak into p.","plain":"In plain words: you start with 150 pieces of information. The model uses one up for each of the six features it estimates a slope for, and one more for the intercept. What is left over is the residual df.","work":"Step 1. Identify n. Records from 150 flats, so n = 150.\nStep 2. Identify p. Six features of the building, so p = 6. The maintenance cost is the outcome and is never counted in p.\nStep 3. Apply the rule. df error = n - p - 1 = 150 - 6 - 1 = 143.\nStep 4. Sense check. df model is 6 and df total is n - 1 = 149, and 6 + 143 = 149. It balances.\nMemory hook: seven numbers are estimated, six slopes and one intercept, so 150 - 7 = 143.","wrongs":["","This is n - p = 150 - 6 = 144 and forgets the intercept. The balance check catches it: 6 + 144 = 150, which is n and not n - 1.","This counted seven variables in p by including the maintenance cost itself: 150 - 7 - 1 = 142. The outcome is what the model predicts, not a predictor.","This is n - 1 = 149, which is the TOTAL df, not the residual df. That row still has the model df inside it."],"trap":"Counting the outcome variable as one of the predictors. Read the stem again and ask: how many things go into the right-hand side of the equation?","tri":"Symptom: n and p given, residual or error df wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: df error in multiple regression. -&gt; Treatment: df error = n - p - 1, where p counts PREDICTORS only and the 1 is the intercept."},{"n":2,"q":"Amara Nwosu predicts a patient recovery score from four clinical measures. The journal that published her study printed only the cells shown below. How many patients did she study?","o":["121","84","85","81"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"backout_n","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Missing cells and a question about the number of participants. Never try to guess n directly. Build the chain MSE, then SS model, then SS error, then df error, and only at the end turn df error into n.","plain":"In plain words: n is hidden inside the residual degrees of freedom. So rebuild that one cell, then undo the formula df error = n - p - 1 to get n back.","work":"Step 1. MSE from F. MSE = MS model / F = 315.0 / 9.00 = 35.0.\nStep 2. SS model from MS model. df model = p = 4, so SS model = 4 x 315.0 = 1260.0.\nStep 3. SS error by subtraction. 4060.0 - 1260.0 = 2800.0.\nStep 4. df error. 2800.0 / 35.0 = 80.\nStep 5. Unpack n. 80 = n - 4 - 1, so n = 85.\nStep 6. Sense check. df total = n - 1 = 84, and 4 + 80 = 84. It balances.\nMemory hook: four steps down, one step back up. The last step is always \"add p, add 1\".","wrongs":["This divided the TOTAL sum of squares by MSE: 4060.0 / 35.0 = 116, then 116 + 4 + 1 = 121. SS model has to be taken off first, because MSE belongs to the residual row.","This used df error = n - p and got 80 + 4 = 84. The intercept costs a degree of freedom too, so the rule has a minus 1 as well.","","This used df error = n - 1 and got 80 + 1 = 81, which is the rule for a one sample t test. Four predictors cannot cost nothing."],"trap":"Using SST where SS error belongs. The F and the MS in the regression row tell you about the model; MSE and df error live in the residual row.","tri":"Symptom: a regression ANOVA table with holes and a question about sample size. -&gt; Diagnosis: recover n from df error. -&gt; Treatment: MSE = MS model / F, then SS model = p x MS model, then SS error = SST - SS model, then df error = SS error / MSE, then n = df error + p + 1.","out":{"cap":"Published cells of the regression ANOVA table (4 predictors)","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Regression","?","4","315.0","9.0"],["Residual","?","?","?",""],["Total","4060.0","?","",""]]}},{"n":3,"q":"Felipe Duarte regresses plant height on the amount of fertiliser given, for 180 pots. His plot of the residuals against the predicted values is an upside-down U: the residuals are negative at both ends and positive in the middle, and the band keeps roughly the same thickness throughout. What is the problem?","o":["The residuals have unequal spread, so the standard errors in the output are too small.","The relationship bends, so a straight line is the wrong model for these data.","There are influential outliers at both ends of the fertiliser range.","Nothing is wrong. Residuals are expected to be negative at the extremes of x."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"resid_plot","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Residual plot plus a described shape. Two shapes, two diagnoses. A fan or funnel that widens or narrows means unequal spread. An arch, a U or any bend in the MIDDLE LINE of the band means the straight line is wrong. Here the stem tells you the thickness is constant, so spread is not the issue.","plain":"In plain words: the residuals should be a shapeless cloud with no pattern. Here they form a clear arch, which means the real relationship curves and the straight line cuts across it, overshooting in some places and undershooting in others.","work":"Step 1. Describe the shape precisely. The centre of the band moves: down, up, down. The width of the band does not move.\nStep 2. Match that to a condition. A moving centre is a failure of linearity. A changing width would have been a failure of equal spread.\nStep 3. Read what the arch means for the data. Negative residuals at both ends and positive in the middle means the real curve rises and then levels off or falls, while the straight line keeps going.\nStep 4. Work out the consequence. No amount of fixing the standard errors helps here, because the equation itself is the wrong shape.\nExplain it to a friend: fertiliser helps up to a point and then stops helping. A straight line cannot say \"up to a point\".","wrongs":["Unequal spread shows as a change in the THICKNESS of the band. The stem says the thickness stays roughly the same, so this is the other shape.","","Outliers are a few isolated points far from the rest. This is a smooth pattern followed by all 180 points, which is a systematic problem, not a few odd pots.","There is nothing normal about this. A good residual plot has no pattern at all: no bend, no fan, just a flat formless band around zero."],"trap":"Calling every bad residual plot heteroscedastic because that is the word you remember. Ask whether the MIDDLE of the band drifts or the WIDTH of the band changes, and let that choose the diagnosis.","tri":"Symptom: residuals form an arch or U with constant thickness. -&gt; Diagnosis: the linearity condition fails, the model form is wrong. -&gt; Treatment: say that a straight line does not fit the shape of the relationship. The fix is to change the model, for example by adding a squared term or transforming a variable, not to change the standard errors."},{"n":4,"q":"Ingrid Olsen predicts customer satisfaction from four service features and reports that the test of her whole model is significant at the .01 level. A colleague asks what this means for the individual features. Which reply is correct?","o":["Every one of the four features is significantly related to satisfaction.","At least one of the four features is significantly related to satisfaction, but the test does not say which.","Exactly one of the four features is significantly related to satisfaction.","Nothing can be said about the features, because the test of the whole model is about the intercept."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"overall_f","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. A significant test of the WHOLE model and a question about the individual predictors. The correct option is always \"at least one, but not which one\". Reject anything with every, all, exactly one, or a named predictor.","plain":"In plain words: the overall test is a single yes or no about the whole bundle of predictors. It answered yes, so something in the bundle works. It is not able to point at a culprit.","work":"Step 1. Write the null for the overall test. H0: all four slopes equal zero.\nStep 2. Write the alternative. Ha: at least one slope is not zero. Logic says the opposite of \"all are zero\" is \"not all are zero\", which is the same as \"at least one is not zero\".\nStep 3. The result is significant, so reject H0 and accept the wording of Ha.\nStep 4. Decide what is still unknown. The F test never names a predictor. With four features there are four separate t tests, and any number of them between one and four could turn out significant.\nExplain it to a friend: a positive result on a test for \"any allergy at all\" tells you something is a trigger, not which food to avoid.","wrongs":["This is the \"all\" version. A single non-zero slope is enough to make the overall F significant, so \"all\" claims far more than the test supports.","","\"Exactly one\" is just as overconfident as \"all\". The F test puts no number on how many slopes are non-zero.","The overall F tests the slopes, not the intercept. H0 for the overall F sets every slope to zero and leaves the intercept free."],"trap":"Treating the overall F as a shortcut that saves you reading the coefficients table. It never is. Significant F means \"look at the t tests next\", not \"they are all significant\".","tri":"Symptom: a significant overall F and a question about single predictors. -&gt; Diagnosis: what the omnibus F does and does not test. -&gt; Treatment: H0 is that all slopes are zero at once. Rejecting it means at least one is not zero. The individual t tests, and only those, identify which."},{"n":5,"q":"Hassan Karim predicts marathon finishing time from weekly training hours, years of running experience and age, in 120 runners. His output is printed below. Which one of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["The model as a whole is significant, F(3, 116) = 12.00, p &lt; .001.","Two of the three predictors are significant at the .05 level and one is not.","With the other two predictors held constant, each extra weekly training hour goes with a change of 1.86 in finishing time.","R squared of .237 means the multiple correlation between the predictors and finishing time is .237."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"read_output","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. A printed output and \"which statement is FALSE\". Make your own notes from the output before you look at the options: R squared as a percentage, the square root of R squared if you need R, the overall p, and each individual p against .05. One of the four options will contradict your notes.","plain":"In plain words: three of these sentences are true readings of the output and one quietly mixes up R with R squared. Find the mix-up.","work":"Step 1. Check the overall test. F(3, 116) = 12.00 with p &lt; .001 is significant, so statement a is true. The df pair also fits: p = 3 predictors and n - p - 1 = 120 - 3 - 1 = 116.\nStep 2. Check the individual tests. Training hours p &lt; .001 is significant. Experience p = .005 is significant. Age p = .674 is not. So two out of three, and statement b is true.\nStep 3. Check the slope reading. b = 1.86 for training hours, and in multiple regression every slope is read with the other predictors held constant, so statement c is true.\nStep 4. Check the last one. R squared = .237, so R = the square root of .237 = .487, not .237. Statement d is false.\nStep 5. Note how far apart they are. R squared .237 means 23.7 per cent of the variance explained, while R = .487 is a moderate correlation. Calling R \"the same as .237\" understates the association badly.\nMemory hook: R squared is the area, R is the side of the square. Take the root to go from area back to side.","wrongs":["True. p &lt; .001 is below .05 and below .01, and the df pair matches 3 predictors in a sample of 120.","True. Training hours and experience both have p below .05, age has p = .674.","True. The slope of 1.86 is the change in finishing time per extra training hour, with experience and age held constant.",""],"trap":"Reading R squared as though it were R, or quoting R squared as a correlation. Whenever an option puts a single number in both roles, suspect it.","tri":"Symptom: regression output, hunt the false claim. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading output, with R and R squared kept apart. -&gt; Treatment: R squared is the share of variance explained. R is its square root and is the correlation between the observed and predicted values. They are different numbers and never interchangeable.","out":{"cap":"Regression output: R squared = .237, adjusted R squared = .217, F(3, 116) = 12.00, p &lt; .001","h":["Predictor","b","SE(b)","t","p"],"r":[["(Constant)","212.4","18.65","11.39","&lt; .001"],["Weekly training hours","1.86","0.47","3.96","&lt; .001"],["Years of experience","0.94","0.33","2.85",".005"],["Age in years","-0.08","0.19","-0.42",".674"]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Chiara Bellini predicts a child height in centimetres from the mid-parent height. For a mid-parent height of 172 cm she needs two answers: the average height of all children of such parents, and the height of one particular child of such parents. Which statement about the two 95 per cent intervals is correct?","o":["The interval for the average child is wider, because an average pools many children and so collects more uncertainty.","The two intervals are identical, because both are built at a mid-parent height of 172 cm.","The interval for one particular child is wider, because it must allow for how far individual children scatter around the line as well as for uncertainty about the line.","The interval for one particular child is wider, but only when the sample size is small. With a large sample the two become equal."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"two_intervals","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. The same value of x, but two different targets: \"the average of all\" against \"one particular\". Those two phrases are the whole question. Average of all = confidence interval for the mean response = narrower. One particular = prediction interval = wider. Always.","plain":"In plain words: averages are steadier than individuals. Predicting what a typical child of 172 cm parents does is easier than predicting what one named child does, so the interval for the named child has to be wider.","work":"Step 1. Name the two targets. Target one is a population MEAN at x = 172. Target two is a single future OBSERVATION at x = 172.\nStep 2. List the sources of error for target one. Only one: we do not know exactly where the true line sits.\nStep 3. List them for target two. Two: we do not know where the line sits, AND an individual child sits above or below the line by some amount of its own.\nStep 4. More sources of error means a wider interval, so the interval for one particular child is wider.\nStep 5. Check what happens as n grows. Uncertainty about the line shrinks towards zero, but individual scatter does not, because it is a property of children and not of the sample. So the prediction interval stays wider forever.\nExplain it to a friend: you can learn the average rainfall in May very precisely, and still not know whether next Tuesday is wet.","wrongs":["Pooling many children makes an average MORE stable, not less. This option has the direction of the effect reversed.","Same x gives the same centre, not the same width. The two intervals answer different questions, so they cannot have the same width.","","The first half is right and the second half is wrong. Individual scatter does not disappear with a large sample, so the prediction interval never collapses onto the interval for the mean."],"trap":"Thinking a huge sample makes individual predictions precise. A big sample pins down the LINE, never the next individual.","tri":"Symptom: one value of x, two questions, one about a mean and one about an individual. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for the mean response against prediction interval. -&gt; Treatment: both share the same centre. The prediction interval carries an extra variance term for individual scatter, so it is always the wider one, and it stays wider no matter how big n gets."},{"n":7,"q":"Lars Petersen predicts weekly screen time from two personality measures in a sample of Danish teenagers. The sums of squares and the degrees of freedom are printed below but the test statistic is not. What is the value of the statistic for the overall test? (2 decimals)","o":["11.67","0.27","23.33","0.14"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"f_from_output","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. SS and df are given, F is missing. The examiner wants to see whether you go through the MS column. If a tempting answer can be got by dividing two SS values directly, that answer is the trap, not the solution.","plain":"In plain words: a sum of squares is a total, and totals built from different amounts of information cannot be compared. Divide each by its own df to turn it into an average, and only then take the ratio.","work":"Step 1. MS model. 980.0 / 2 = 490.0.\nStep 2. MS error. 3570.0 / 85 = 42.0.\nStep 3. F. 490.0 / 42.0 = 11.6667, so F = 11.67.\nStep 4. Sense check. Under a true null F sits near 1, so a value near 12 points to a model that does explain something. A value below 1 would have been the warning sign that you divided the wrong way round.\nMemory hook: SS over df gives MS, MS over MS gives F. Two divisions, then one more.","wrongs":["","This is SS model / SS error = 980.0 / 3570.0 = 0.27, the sums of squares divided without forming the mean squares. The most common error in this question type.","This left the numerator as a sum of squares while dividing the denominator properly: 980.0 / 42.0 = 23.33. Both sides must be mean squares.","This divided MS model by SS error: 490.0 / 3570.0 = 0.14. The residual side still needs dividing by its 85 degrees of freedom."],"trap":"Dividing SS by SS, because the answer looks like a proportion and feels familiar. Write the MS column out before you touch the F cell.","tri":"Symptom: an ANOVA table with SS and df but no F. -&gt; Diagnosis: F from mean squares. -&gt; Treatment: MS model = SS model / df model, MS error = SS error / df error, F = MS model / MS error. The model term is always on top.","out":{"cap":"Regression ANOVA table, test statistic missing","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Regression","980.0","2","?","?"],["Residual","3570.0","85","?",""],["Total","4550.0","87","",""]]}},{"n":8,"q":"Yuki Tanaka predicts job performance from two selection test scores in 60 new hires and reports F(2, 57) = 1.84 with p = .168. What is the right thing to say about her model?","o":["Both selection tests are unrelated to job performance in the population.","The model is significant at the .20 level, so she may report a relationship with that caveat.","Since the overall test failed, she should now run the two individual t tests to find the predictor that works.","There is not enough evidence that the two tests together predict job performance, which is not the same as showing that neither of them does."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"overall_f","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. A NON-significant overall F. The correct option always speaks of a lack of evidence, never of a proven absence. Any option that reads a large p as proof that the predictors do nothing is the trap.","plain":"In plain words: the model did not clear the bar. That means she failed to show a relationship, not that she showed there is none. A quiet result and an empty result look the same from the outside.","work":"Step 1. Compare p with alpha. p = .168 is above .05, so do not reject H0.\nStep 2. Say what that does and does not mean. Not rejecting H0 is a failure to find evidence. It is never proof that H0 is true, for exactly the same reason that a non-significant t test does not prove two means are equal.\nStep 3. Think about why the result may be quiet. With n = 60 and two predictors, a real but modest relationship could easily be missed. Power is limited.\nStep 4. Choose the option that is honest about both halves: no evidence of a relationship, and no proof of no relationship.\nMemory hook: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.","wrongs":["This accepts the null as proven. A non-significant result cannot establish that slopes are exactly zero in the population.","This moves alpha after seeing the data so that the result passes. Alpha is fixed before the analysis, conventionally at .05.","Hunting among individual tests after a non-significant overall test inflates the chance of a false positive. The overall test is the gate, and it stayed shut.",""],"trap":"Writing \"the selection tests do not predict performance\". The honest sentence is \"this study could not show that they do\", especially with only 60 hires.","tri":"Symptom: overall F with p above alpha. -&gt; Diagnosis: failing to reject the omnibus null. -&gt; Treatment: say \"not enough evidence that the predictors as a set are related to the outcome\". Do not accept H0, do not go hunting among the individual t tests, and do not move alpha after the fact."},{"n":9,"q":"Daniela Munoz first predicts a teenager sleep duration from daily screen time alone, and reports a correlation of .36 between the two. She then adds caffeine intake to the model, and the two-predictor model reports R squared = .29. How much extra variance in sleep duration does caffeine intake account for? (1 decimal, as a percentage)","o":["16.0 per cent","13.0 per cent","7.0 per cent","29.0 per cent"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"r2_change","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. Model one is reported as a CORRELATION and model two as an R SQUARED. That difference in wording is the trap. Square the correlation first so both models are on the variance scale, then subtract.","plain":"In plain words: you can only subtract shares of variance from shares of variance. A correlation is not yet a share, so square it before it goes into the subtraction.","work":"Step 1. Convert model one. r = .36, so R squared for model one = .36 x .36 = .1296.\nStep 2. Read model two. R squared = .29.\nStep 3. Subtract. .29 - .1296 = .1604.\nStep 4. As a percentage, .1604 x 100 = 16.04, which rounds to 16.0 per cent.\nStep 5. Sense check. The difference must be positive, since adding a predictor can never lower R squared, and it must be less than .29. It is.\nMemory hook: square first, subtract second. If your difference comes out negative, you forgot to square.","wrongs":["","This is R squared for model one on its own: .36 x .36 = .1296, or 13.0 per cent. That is what screen time already explained, not what caffeine added.","This subtracted r from R squared: .29 - .36 = -.07, read as 7 per cent. Two different scales were mixed, which is why the sign comes out negative.","This is R squared for model two on its own, 29.0 per cent. That is what both predictors explain together, with nothing subtracted."],"trap":"Subtracting .36 from .29 straight away. A correlation and a proportion of variance are different units, like comparing a length with an area.","tri":"Symptom: r for the small model, R squared for the big model, question about extra or additional variance explained. -&gt; Diagnosis: change in R squared. -&gt; Treatment: 1 square r, 2 subtract from the larger R squared, 3 multiply by 100."},{"n":10,"q":"Oskar Zielinski predicts exam performance from hours of revision and from number of practice papers completed. The two predictors correlate at .89. Compared with a model that uses hours of revision alone, what happens when he puts both predictors in together?","o":["R squared falls, because the two predictors compete for the same variance.","The standard errors of the slopes fall, because the model now has more information in it.","The standard errors of the slopes rise, so each slope becomes harder to distinguish from zero even though the model still predicts well.","The overall test becomes non-significant, because the two predictors cancel each other out."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"multicollinearity","theme":5,"spot":"HINT. The stem volunteers a high correlation BETWEEN two predictors, usually something like .85 or above, or mentions a low tolerance or a high VIF. That is multicollinearity, and the question is always about what happens to the standard errors.","plain":"In plain words: revision hours and practice papers are nearly the same thing. The model can still predict exam performance well, but it cannot work out how much credit each of the two near-twins deserves, so it reports each slope with a lot of uncertainty.","work":"Step 1. Recall what a slope means here. The slope for revision hours is the effect of revision hours with practice papers held constant.\nStep 2. Ask whether the data can show that. At r = .89 the two almost always move together, so there are very few students who revised a lot but did few papers, or the other way round. That unique information is thin.\nStep 3. Thin information means a large standard error for that slope. Since t = b / SE(b), a bigger SE gives a smaller t and a bigger p.\nStep 4. Check what is unaffected. R squared cannot go down when a predictor is added, and the overall F is about the set, so it generally stays significant.\nStep 5. So the right description is: standard errors up, individual slopes uncertain, prediction still good.\nExplain it to a friend: the two predictors are a pair of identical twins who go everywhere together. You know the pair was at the scene, but not which twin did the deed.","wrongs":["R squared never falls when a predictor is added to a model. At worst it stays where it was.","This has the effect backwards. Overlapping predictors give LESS unique information about each slope, so standard errors rise.","","The overall test usually stays significant, which is exactly what makes this pattern recognisable: a healthy overall F sitting next to weak individual t tests."],"trap":"Concluding that neither predictor matters. The slopes are imprecise, not zero. Each predictor on its own is strongly related to exam performance.","tri":"Symptom: two predictors that correlate strongly with each other. -&gt; Diagnosis: multicollinearity. -&gt; Treatment: the standard errors of the individual slopes become inflated, so t values shrink and p values grow. R squared and the overall F are not harmed, because they only concern the predictors as a set."}]},{"id":"t6a","name":"One-way ANOVA - Set A","short":"One-way ANOVA - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on comparing three or more group means: writing the hypotheses, counting the df pair, completing an ANOVA table, turning MSE into a standard deviation, the price of many pairwise tests, and above all the exact sentence a significant F does and does not allow.","theme":6,"icon":"🧪","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Beatriz Alves compares the mean number of hours slept per night by nurses working early, late and night shifts. She expects night-shift nurses to sleep least. Which pair of hypotheses belongs to the test she needs?","o":["H0: mu(early) = mu(late) = mu(night). Ha: mu(night) &lt; mu(early) and mu(night) &lt; mu(late).","H0: at least two of the three means are equal. Ha: all three means differ.","H0: mu(early) = mu(late) = mu(night). Ha: not all three means are equal.","H0: mu(early) = mu(late) = mu(night) = 0. Ha: at least one mean is not 0."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"anova_hyp","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. Three or more group means and a request for the hypotheses. The pair is fixed and it never changes: H0 says all the population means are equal, Ha says they are not all equal. The expectation mentioned in the stem is a distraction, because this test has no direction.","plain":"In plain words: the test asks one blunt question, \"are these population means all the same?\". It cannot be pointed in a direction, and it has nothing to say about zero.","work":"Step 1. Count the groups. Three shifts, so k = 3 population means.\nStep 2. Write H0. All of them equal: mu(early) = mu(late) = mu(night). Note that this says nothing about what the common value is.\nStep 3. Write Ha as the strict logical opposite. The opposite of \"all equal\" is \"not all equal\". That covers every other possibility: one odd group, two odd groups, or all three different.\nStep 4. Deal with her expectation. She may expect night shift to be lowest, but the F statistic is built from squared deviations, so it cannot be one-sided. If she wants to test a direction she needs a planned comparison, not the F test.\nMemory hook: F has no arrows. H0 is all equals signs, Ha is one short sentence in words.","wrongs":["The H0 is right but the Ha is directional. An F statistic squares everything, so it cannot detect direction and this Ha cannot be tested with it.","Both halves are wrong. H0 must say ALL the means are equal, not at least two, and Ha must say NOT ALL are equal, not that all differ.","","This is the null for a set of regression slopes, not for a set of means. ANOVA never claims the means are zero, only that they are equal to one another."],"trap":"Letting the researcher expectation turn Ha into a one-sided statement. The direction she expects affects what she does AFTER a significant F, not the hypotheses she starts with.","tri":"Symptom: three or more groups, hypotheses wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: hypotheses for a one-way ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: H0: mu1 = mu2 = ... = muk. Ha: not all the means are equal. Reject any Ha with an inequality sign in it and any H0 that sets the means to zero."},{"n":2,"q":"Niels Bakker compares concentration scores across four classroom lighting conditions, with 15 pupils in each condition. Which pair of degrees of freedom belongs to his test? (model df first)","o":["(3, 59)","(3, 56)","(56, 3)","(4, 56)"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"df_oneway","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. A number of groups and a number of people, and a request for degrees of freedom. Count k, the number of groups, and N, the TOTAL number of people across all groups. Then the pair is (k - 1, N - k), in that order, every time.","plain":"In plain words: the first df counts how many groups you have, minus one. The second counts how many people you have in total, minus one for each group mean you had to estimate.","work":"Step 1. Count the groups. Four lighting conditions, so k = 4.\nStep 2. Get the total N. Fifteen pupils in each of four conditions, so N = 4 x 15 = 60. The 15 is per group, not the total.\nStep 3. df between = k - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3.\nStep 4. df within = N - k = 60 - 4 = 56.\nStep 5. Write the pair in the right order: (3, 56).\nStep 6. Sense check. The two should add to N - 1 = 59, and 3 + 56 = 59. It balances.\nMemory hook: the small number is always first. Groups are few, people are many.","wrongs":["This used N - 1 = 59 for the second df, which is the TOTAL df, not the within df. The within row has already given up one df per group mean.","","This is the right pair written backwards. The order matters, because the between df belongs to the numerator of F and the within df to the denominator. F(56, 3) is a different distribution from F(3, 56).","This used k instead of k - 1 for the first df. You lose one df because the group means are compared with the overall mean, which is itself estimated from the data."],"trap":"Reading 15 as the sample size. When a stem says \"in each\", multiply by k before you do anything else.","tri":"Symptom: k groups, n per group or a total N, df pair wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a one-way F. -&gt; Treatment: N = k x n when the groups are equal. Then df between = k - 1 and df within = N - k. Write them between first, within second."},{"n":3,"q":"Fatima Zahra compares mean customer waiting times at three branches of a bank, with 30 customers timed at each branch, and reports F(2, 87) = 7.94 with p &lt; .001. What may she conclude?","o":["All three branches have different mean waiting times.","The branch with the longest observed mean is significantly slower than the other two.","Waiting time depends on the branch in 99.9 per cent of customers.","Somewhere among the three branches the population mean waiting times are not all equal."],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"anova_conclude","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. An F value, a p-value and \"what may she conclude\" with three or more groups. This is the most likely question type on the whole theme. The correct option is always the modest one: NOT ALL the means are equal. Cross out any option that says all differ, that names a particular group, or that turns the p-value into a percentage of people.","plain":"In plain words: the test says the group means are not all the same. It does not say how many differ, and it does not say which ones. Those are separate questions.","work":"Step 1. Write H0 and Ha. H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3. Ha: not all three means are equal.\nStep 2. Compare p with alpha. p &lt; .001 is below .05, so reject H0.\nStep 3. Say Ha out loud, in its own words. Not all of the three population means are equal. Equivalently, at least two of them differ.\nStep 4. Check what is still unknown. The F test has not compared any specific pair. It is entirely possible that two branches are identical and the third is slow.\nStep 5. Pick the only option that stops where the evidence stops.\nExplain it to a friend: someone in the room is talking, but you have not turned round yet to see who.","wrongs":["This is the classic overstatement. \"Not all equal\" is satisfied by two identical branches plus one different one, so \"all three differ\" claims much more than the F test shows.","Naming the slowest branch needs a post-hoc comparison. Having the largest observed mean is not the same as being significantly larger.","A p-value is not a percentage of customers. It is the probability of data this extreme if all the means were in fact equal.",""],"trap":"Jumping from \"the means are not all equal\" to \"every group differs from every other\". Train yourself to write the modest sentence first, then stop.","tri":"Symptom: a significant one-way F and a conclusion to choose. -&gt; Diagnosis: what a significant F licenses. -&gt; Treatment: reject H0, then state Ha in its exact words: not all the population means are equal, so at least two of them differ. To name a pair you must run a post-hoc test."},{"n":4,"q":"Viktor Petrov compares the mean grip strength of four groups of athletes, with 12 athletes in each group. His software printed the sums of squares and the degrees of freedom but the last column is missing. What is the value of his test statistic? (2 decimals)","o":["4.50","0.31","13.50","0.10"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"f_from_table","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. An ANOVA table with the F cell blank. The examiner is watching for whether you build the MS column first. If one of the options can be got by dividing the two SS values straight away, that option is the trap.","plain":"In plain words: the two sums of squares are totals built from different amounts of information, 3 df against 44 df. Turn each into an average by dividing by its own df, then compare the two averages.","work":"Step 1. MS between. 486.0 / 3 = 162.0.\nStep 2. MS within. 1584.0 / 44 = 36.0.\nStep 3. F. 162.0 / 36.0 = 4.50.\nStep 4. Sense check. If all four population means were equal, both mean squares would estimate the same variance and F would sit near 1. A value of 4.5 says the group means vary four and a half times more than the noise within groups would lead you to expect.\nStep 5. The df check. k = 4 so df between = 3, and N = 4 x 12 = 48 so df within = 44. Both match the printed table.\nMemory hook: F is a ratio of two VARIANCES, and a variance is always a sum of squares divided by its df. So never feed raw SS into F.","wrongs":["","This is SS between / SS within = 486.0 / 1584.0 = 0.31, the sums of squares divided without forming the mean squares at all.","This divided MS between by nothing useful: 486.0 / 36.0 = 13.50 leaves the numerator as a raw sum of squares while the denominator is a mean square. Both sides must be mean squares.","This is MS between / SS within = 162.0 / 1584.0 = 0.10. The within sum of squares still needs dividing by its 44 degrees of freedom."],"trap":"Dividing SS between by SS within. It gives a number that looks like a proportion, and students accept it because it resembles an effect size. Fill in the whole MS column before you touch the F cell.","tri":"Symptom: SS and df given in a one-way table, F wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: F from an ANOVA table. -&gt; Treatment: MS between = SS between / df between, MS within = SS within / df within, F = MS between / MS within. Between on top, always.","out":{"cap":"One-way ANOVA table, test statistic missing","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Between groups","486.0","3","?","?"],["Within groups","1584.0","44","?",""],["Total","2070.0","47","",""]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Aiko Nakamura compares mean reaction times across three task difficulty levels. Her output gives a within-groups mean square of 54.76. What is her best single estimate of the standard deviation of reaction times inside a difficulty level? (2 decimals)","o":["54.76","27.38","7.40","2.72"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"sqrt_mse","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The stem gives you a within-groups mean square, or MSE, or MS within, and then asks for a standard deviation, a spread or an SD. Mean square means variance. The answer is always its SQUARE ROOT.","plain":"In plain words: MSE is a variance, and a variance lives in squared units. A standard deviation lives in the original units, so you have to take the square root to get back there.","work":"Step 1. Name what MSE is. MS within is the pooled estimate of the common population VARIANCE inside the groups. Here that is 54.76.\nStep 2. Name what is wanted. A standard deviation, which is the square root of a variance.\nStep 3. Take the root. The square root of 54.76 is 7.40, because 7.4 x 7.4 = 54.76.\nStep 4. Sense check the units. If reaction times are measured in hundredths of a second, a typical distance from the group mean of about 7.4 of those units is believable, while 54.76 would be enormous.\nMemory hook: MS stands for Mean Square. Square is in the name, so undo it with a root.","wrongs":["This hands back MSE itself, which is a variance, not a standard deviation. The units are squared.","This halved MSE: 54.76 / 2 = 27.38. Halving has nothing to do with the relationship between a variance and a standard deviation.","","This took the root twice: the square root of 7.40 is 2.72. One root is enough to go from variance to standard deviation."],"trap":"Reporting MSE where s was asked for. Read the question for the WORD: variance means leave it alone, standard deviation or spread means take the root.","tri":"Symptom: MSE or MS within given, a standard deviation wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: pooled estimate of the common within-group SD. -&gt; Treatment: s = sqrt(MSE). Never MSE itself, never MSE halved."},{"n":6,"q":"Samuel Oyelaran compares mean job satisfaction across five departments and obtains p = .41 for his overall test. He wants to go on and compare every pair of departments to see which are different. What should he do?","o":["Run all ten pairwise comparisons, but only report the ones that come out significant.","Run no pairwise comparisons, because the overall test gave him no evidence that any of the means differ.","Run all ten pairwise comparisons at .05 each, because the overall test and the pairwise tests are separate questions.","Run the single comparison between the highest and the lowest observed mean, because that is the only pair that could be significant."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"posthoc_when","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The stem shows an overall result and then asks about following it up with pairwise comparisons. The rule has two conditions and both must hold: you must have REJECTED the overall null, and you must have MORE THAN TWO groups. Check the p-value first.","plain":"In plain words: the overall test is the gate. It stayed shut here, so there is nothing to walk through. Post-hoc tests exist to find out WHICH means differ, and he has no evidence that any of them do.","work":"Step 1. Read the overall result. p = .41, far above .05, so do not reject H0.\nStep 2. State what that leaves him with. No evidence that the five department means differ at all.\nStep 3. Ask what a post-hoc test is for. It answers \"which pairs differ\", a question that only arises once you know SOME pair does.\nStep 4. Think about the cost of ignoring this. Five departments give 5 x 4 / 2 = 10 pairs. Testing ten pairs at .05 each gives a family-wise error rate of 1 - .95^10 = .40, so a false positive is close to a coin toss.\nStep 5. So the right move is to stop and report the non-significant overall test.\nExplain it to a friend: the metal detector did not beep, so there is no reason to start digging ten holes.","wrongs":["Reporting only the comparisons that came out significant is selective reporting. It guarantees a misleading paper, because with ten tests at .05 about one false positive is expected.","","They are not separate questions. The overall test is the protection for the pairwise ones, which is precisely why it comes first.","Picking the most extreme pair AFTER looking at the data is the worst version of this error, because the biggest observed gap is the most likely to be there by chance."],"trap":"Treating a non-significant F as a licence to go hunting among the pairs. Two conditions, both needed: reject the overall null, and have more than two groups.","tri":"Symptom: an overall F result plus a question about pairwise follow-up tests. -&gt; Diagnosis: when post-hoc tests are appropriate. -&gt; Treatment: post-hoc tests are for a SIGNIFICANT F with k greater than 2. With a non-significant F you stop. Searching the pairs anyway is fishing and inflates the Type I error rate."},{"n":7,"q":"Clara Jensen plans to compare mean recovery scores for five different therapies by testing every possible pair of therapies separately, each at the 5 per cent level. What is the probability that she makes at least one Type I error across all those tests? (2 decimals)","o":["0.05","0.50","0.60","0.40"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"familywise","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The stem says every possible pair, each at 5 per cent, and asks about at least one error. Two steps, never one: first count the pairs with m = k(k-1)/2, then apply 1 - .95 to the power m.","plain":"In plain words: each separate test has a 95 per cent chance of behaving itself. For all of them to behave, all of those 95 per cents must happen together, so you multiply. What is left over is the chance that at least one test cries wolf.","work":"Step 1. Count the comparisons. k = 5 therapies, so m = 5 x 4 / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10 pairs.\nStep 2. Find the chance that one single test behaves. 1 - .05 = .95.\nStep 3. Find the chance that all ten behave. The tests are treated as independent, so multiply: .95^10 = .5987.\nStep 4. Turn that into \"at least one misbehaves\". 1 - .5987 = .4013, which rounds to 0.40.\nStep 5. Sense check. It must be bigger than .05 and smaller than 1, and it should feel alarming. A 40 per cent chance of a false alarm is exactly why post-hoc corrections exist.\nMemory hook: at least one is the opposite of none. Work out \"none\", then take it away from 1.","wrongs":["This is alpha for a single test. The whole point of the question is that ten tests are far riskier than one.","This multiplied alpha by the number of tests: .05 x 10 = .50. That approximation overshoots, and it would go above 1 for 21 or more tests, which is impossible for a probability.","This is .95^10 = .60, the probability that NO test gives a false positive. The question asks for at least one, so this needs taking away from 1.",""],"trap":"Counting 20 comparisons instead of 10 by forgetting to divide by 2. Pair A with B is the same comparison as B with A, so k(k-1) must be halved. Using 20 would give 1 - .95^20 = .64.","tri":"Symptom: k groups, all pairwise tests, each at .05, \"at least one\" error wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: family-wise error rate. -&gt; Treatment: 1 count the comparisons, m = k(k-1)/2, 2 compute 1 - .95^m. Do not multiply .05 by m, and do not stop at .95^m."},{"n":8,"q":"Ravi Sharma compares mean weekly exercise minutes across four age bands, with 30 adults per band, and reports F(3, 116) = 1.52 with p = .213. What may he conclude?","o":["He has no evidence that the mean exercise minutes differ between the age bands, which is not the same as having shown that they are equal.","The four population means are equal.","At least two of the four age bands differ in mean exercise minutes.","The result is inconclusive, so he should lower his significance level to .10 and test again."],"a":0,"pct":75,"typ":"anova_conclude","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. A one-way F with p ABOVE .05 and \"what may he conclude\". The correct option always speaks of a lack of evidence and never of equality proved. Watch for the two traps: \"the means are equal\" and \"move alpha and try again\".","plain":"In plain words: he did not find a difference. That is not the same as finding that there is no difference. The study simply came back quiet.","work":"Step 1. Compare p with alpha. p = .213 is well above .05, so do not reject H0.\nStep 2. Translate the decision honestly. Not rejecting means the data are consistent with all four means being equal, but they are also consistent with modest real differences that this study was too small to detect.\nStep 3. Rule out the opposite error. Saying \"the means are equal\" accepts H0, which no significance test allows you to do.\nStep 4. Rule out moving the goalposts. Alpha is chosen before the analysis. Raising it to .10 after seeing p = .213 would not even help, since .213 is above .10 as well.\nStep 5. Choose the option that reports a lack of evidence and says so plainly.\nMemory hook: not guilty is not the same as innocent.","wrongs":["","This accepts the null. A non-significant F cannot establish that four population means are exactly equal, especially with only 30 adults per band.","This is the conclusion for a SIGNIFICANT F. Here p = .213, so the null was not rejected and nothing may be said about any pair.","Alpha is fixed before the data are seen. Adjusting it afterwards to chase a result is a serious error, and .213 would still not pass at .10."],"trap":"Writing \"there is no difference between the age bands\". The honest sentence is \"this study found no evidence of a difference\", which leaves room for an effect too small for n = 120 to pick up.","tri":"Symptom: one-way F with p above alpha. -&gt; Diagnosis: failing to reject the ANOVA null. -&gt; Treatment: say \"not enough evidence that the population means differ\". Never accept H0, never shift alpha after the fact, and never run post-hoc tests."},{"n":9,"q":"Marta Kowalska wants to know whether people who commute by bike, by car or by train differ on average, and she will compare three group means. Which of the variables she has collected can serve as the outcome in that comparison?","o":["Which of the three modes of transport the person uses.","Self-reported stress on the morning of the survey, scored from 0 to 100.","Whether the person arrived on time, recorded as yes or no.","Which mode of transport the person would prefer if money were no object."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"anova_design","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The stem describes groups and asks which variable can be the OUTCOME. Sort her variables into two piles first: labels and numbers. The thing that splits people into groups must be a label, and the outcome must be a number you can average.","plain":"In plain words: you cannot take the mean of a word. The outcome has to be something measured on a scale, so that each group has a mean and the means can be compared.","work":"Step 1. Identify the grouping variable. Mode of transport used, with three levels: bike, car, train. That is the factor, so it cannot also be the outcome.\nStep 2. Test each remaining variable for the one thing the outcome needs: can you compute a mean of it?\nStep 3. Stress scored from 0 to 100 is a number on a scale, so yes. A group mean such as 48.2 is meaningful.\nStep 4. Arrived on time, yes or no, is a category. Comparing groups on that needs a test for proportions, not a comparison of means.\nStep 5. Preferred mode is another category, so it has no mean either.\nStep 6. Only the stress score works as the outcome.\nExplain it to a friend: you can average a score out of 100, you cannot average \"train\".","wrongs":["This is the variable that FORMS the groups. It is the factor, not the outcome, and the comparison would be circular.","","A yes or no answer is categorical. You would compare three proportions here, which is a different analysis altogether.","Preferred mode is a label, not a number. There is no such thing as a mean preferred mode."],"trap":"Choosing a yes or no outcome because percentages feel like numbers. A proportion is a summary of a category, not a quantitative measurement of each person.","tri":"Symptom: a study with three or more groups and a question about which variable goes where. -&gt; Diagnosis: choosing a quantitative response variable. -&gt; Treatment: the grouping variable is categorical with k levels. The response must be quantitative, so that group means exist. A yes or no outcome and a second category question are both unusable here."},{"n":10,"q":"Pierre Girard compares mean blood pressure across three diet groups. His output reports a within-groups mean square of 20.25. What does this number estimate?","o":["The variance of the three group means around the overall mean.","The total variance of all the blood pressure readings in the study.","The common standard deviation of blood pressure inside a diet group.","The common variance of blood pressure inside a diet group, assumed to be the same in all three populations."],"a":3,"pct":30,"typ":"mse_meaning","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The stem names MS within, MSE or the error mean square and asks what it MEANS rather than asking you to compute something. Two words have to be right in your answer: WITHIN, so not between and not total, and VARIANCE, so not standard deviation.","plain":"In plain words: MSE measures how much people scatter around their own group mean. It is the noise in the study, pooled across the groups, and it is the yardstick against which the differences between the group means are judged.","work":"Step 1. Place the number in the table. It comes from the WITHIN groups row, so it is built from how far each person sits from their own group mean.\nStep 2. Recall the condition behind it. One-way ANOVA assumes the three populations share one common variance, so the three groups can be pooled into one estimate of it.\nStep 3. Fix the units. A mean square is a sum of SQUARED deviations divided by df, so it is a variance, not a standard deviation. The standard deviation here would be the square root of 20.25, which is 4.5.\nStep 4. Rule out the other rows. The spread of the three group means around the overall mean is MS between. The spread of all the readings together is the total mean square.\nStep 5. So MSE estimates the common within-group variance.\nMemory hook: within means inside the groups, and mean SQUARE means variance. Put the two words together and you have the answer.","wrongs":["That is MS between, the other row of the table. It is the numerator of F, not the denominator.","The total variance would use the distance of every reading from the OVERALL mean. MS within uses the distance from each group own mean instead.","Right place, wrong units. The common standard deviation is the square root of this number, which is 4.5. MSE itself is a variance.",""],"trap":"Mixing up variance and standard deviation. MSE is the variance, sqrt(MSE) is the standard deviation, and exam options almost always offer both.","tri":"Symptom: a question about what MS within or MSE represents. -&gt; Diagnosis: meaning of the error mean square. -&gt; Treatment: MSE is the pooled estimate of the common within-population VARIANCE. Its square root is the pooled standard deviation. MS between, not MSE, is about how the group means spread out."}]},{"id":"t6b","name":"One-way ANOVA - Set B","short":"One-way ANOVA - Set B","blurb":"Ten more questions on the one-way F, with the shapes mixed up: pick a workable outcome variable, read a significant and a non-significant result correctly, go from MSE to a standard deviation, count the df pair from unequal groups, price up all the pairwise tests, and finish an ANOVA table.","theme":6,"icon":"🧪","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Linnea Berg wants to compare three hospital wards on how well their patients feel they were informed, and plans to compare three group means. She has four variables in her file. Which one can serve as the outcome?","o":["The ward the patient stayed on, coded 1, 2 or 3.","Whether the patient would recommend the hospital, recorded as yes or no.","The name of the nurse who admitted the patient.","The score the patient gave on an information questionnaire, from 10 to 50."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"anova_design","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. Groups are described and you must pick the OUTCOME. Ignore how a variable is coded and ask the only question that matters: can you take a meaningful average of it? A ward coded 1, 2, 3 is still a label even though it looks numeric.","plain":"In plain words: the outcome has to be a real measurement, something where a group mean makes sense. Numbers used as name tags do not count.","work":"Step 1. Find the factor. Ward is what splits the patients into three groups, so it is the factor and cannot be the outcome as well.\nStep 2. Apply the average test to each candidate. Ask: would a mean of this number say anything?\nStep 3. Ward coded 1, 2, 3 fails. A mean ward of 2.1 is meaningless, because the codes are labels and the gaps between them mean nothing.\nStep 4. Would recommend, yes or no, fails too. That is a category, so it needs a comparison of proportions.\nStep 5. Nurse name fails. It is text, and nothing can be averaged.\nStep 6. The questionnaire score from 10 to 50 passes. A ward mean such as 38.6 is meaningful and the three ward means can be compared.\nMemory hook: if the average of it would sound silly out loud, it cannot be the outcome.","wrongs":["This is the factor, the variable that makes the groups. Being coded with numbers does not make it quantitative.","A yes or no answer is categorical, so the three wards would be compared on proportions, not on means.","A name cannot be averaged. It is a label with no order and no spacing.",""],"trap":"Being fooled by a category that has been given number codes. Ask what the number measures: a quantity, or just an identity?","tri":"Symptom: several variables offered, three or more groups, choose the response. -&gt; Diagnosis: choosing a quantitative response variable. -&gt; Treatment: the grouping variable is the factor and cannot be the outcome. The outcome must be quantitative, so group means exist. Yes or no answers and names are categorical."},{"n":2,"q":"Omar Farouk compares mean memory test scores across three revision methods and reports F(2, 72) = 6.31 with p = .003. A fellow student writes in the report that \"all three revision methods produce different mean scores\". Why is that sentence wrong, and what should it say?","o":["Because a significant result only shows that the means are not all equal, so the sentence should say that at least two of the three methods differ.","Because a significant result only applies to the sample, so the sentence should say that the three sample means differ.","Because the test was two-sided, so the sentence should say that the methods may differ in either direction.","Nothing is wrong. With p = .003 all three methods have been shown to differ."],"a":0,"pct":75,"typ":"anova_conclude","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. A significant one-way F and a proposed sentence to judge. The giveaway word is ALL. \"All means differ\" is always too strong. The correct wording is \"not all the means are equal\", which is the same as \"at least two differ\".","plain":"In plain words: the test only rules out the idea that the three means are all identical. Ruling that out needs just one odd method, so you cannot claim that every method differs from every other.","work":"Step 1. Write the hypotheses. H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3. Ha: not all three means are equal.\nStep 2. Reach the decision. p = .003 is below .05, so reject H0 and adopt the wording of Ha.\nStep 3. Think about what breaks H0. H0 fails as soon as ONE mean is different. So two of the methods could be identical while the third stands apart, and the F would still be significant.\nStep 4. Compare the two sentences. \"All three differ\" claims three separate differences. \"At least two differ\" claims one. Only the second is supported.\nStep 5. Note the next step. To say which pair or pairs differ, he must run a post-hoc test such as Bonferroni or Tukey.\nExplain it to a friend: finding out that three siblings are not all the same height does not tell you that no two of them match.","wrongs":["","A significant test is precisely how you generalise beyond the sample. The sample means obviously differ, which is never the point of a test. The problem with the sentence is the word all, not the word produce.","The F test is not one-sided or two-sided in the usual sense, because F squares everything and only has an upper tail. Direction is not the issue here.","This endorses the overstatement. p = .003 licenses \"not all equal\", never \"all different\"."],"trap":"Reading \"not all equal\" as \"all different\". They sound alike and mean very different things. Write the modest version down before you write anything else.","tri":"Symptom: a significant F and a conclusion worded with \"all\". -&gt; Diagnosis: overstating a significant F. -&gt; Treatment: write Ha exactly: not all the population means are equal, which means at least two differ. Which two, and whether the third joins in, requires post-hoc tests."},{"n":3,"q":"Isabel Ferreira compares mean daily step counts across four neighbourhoods. Her output table gives a within-groups mean square of 38.44, in units of hundreds of steps. Which number should she report as the pooled estimate of the standard deviation inside a neighbourhood? (2 decimals)","o":["38.44","19.22","6.20","2.49"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"sqrt_mse","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. A mean square is handed to you and a STANDARD DEVIATION is asked for. Mean square always means variance, so one square root is needed. If the stem had asked for the variance, the answer would have been the printed number itself.","plain":"In plain words: the table gives you the noise in squared units. A standard deviation lives in the original units, so you square root it to come back down.","work":"Step 1. Recognise what 38.44 is. MS within is the pooled estimate of the common VARIANCE within the neighbourhoods.\nStep 2. Recognise what is wanted. The pooled standard deviation, which is the square root of that variance.\nStep 3. Compute it. The square root of 38.44 is 6.20, because 6.2 x 6.2 = 38.44.\nStep 4. Sense check. In hundreds of steps, a typical distance of about 6.2 from the neighbourhood mean, which is 620 steps, is believable. A standard deviation of 38.44 hundreds of steps would be far too large.\nMemory hook: the S in MS is Square. Undo it once.","wrongs":["This is MSE itself, which is a variance. Its units are squared, so it cannot be a standard deviation.","This halved MSE: 38.44 / 2 = 19.22. Halving is not the same as taking a square root.","","This took the root twice: the square root of 6.20 is 2.49. Only one root separates a variance from a standard deviation."],"trap":"Handing back the mean square when the standard deviation was asked for. Circle the word in the question: variance means keep it, standard deviation means root it.","tri":"Symptom: MS within or MSE given, standard deviation or pooled SD wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: s = sqrt(MSE). -&gt; Treatment: take one square root of the mean square and nothing else. Never halve it, never root it twice."},{"n":4,"q":"Jan Dvorak compares mean anxiety scores across three therapy groups, with 12, 15 and 18 clients in them. Which pair of degrees of freedom belongs to his test? (model df first)","o":["(2, 42)","(2, 44)","(42, 2)","(3, 41)"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"df_oneway","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. Group sizes that are not equal, and a df pair wanted. Nothing changes: add the group sizes to get N, then use (k - 1, N - k). Unequal groups only means you have to do one extra addition.","plain":"In plain words: the first number is the number of groups minus one. The second is everybody in the study, minus one for each group mean the model had to estimate.","work":"Step 1. Count the groups. Three therapy groups, so k = 3.\nStep 2. Add up the people. N = 12 + 15 + 18 = 45.\nStep 3. df between = k - 1 = 3 - 1 = 2.\nStep 4. df within = N - k = 45 - 3 = 42.\nStep 5. Write the pair with the model df first: (2, 42).\nStep 6. Sense check. They should add to N - 1 = 44, and 2 + 42 = 44. It balances.\nMemory hook: one df goes for the overall mean, and one more for each extra group mean. Three groups cost three in total, which is why it is N - k.","wrongs":["","This used N - 1 = 44, which is the TOTAL df, not the within df. The within row has already paid for the three group means.","This is the correct pair written backwards. The order matters, because F(42, 2) is a different distribution with different critical values from F(2, 42).","This used k = 3 for the first df instead of k - 1 = 2, and then took 45 - 3 - 1 = 41 by also subtracting an extra 1. The one-way rule has no minus 1 in the second df, that belongs to regression."],"trap":"Forgetting to add the three group sizes, or reaching for a per-group number as though the groups were equal. Unequal groups are the normal case in practice.","tri":"Symptom: unequal group sizes, df pair wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a one-way F. -&gt; Treatment: N = sum of the group sizes, then df between = k - 1 and df within = N - k, written between first."},{"n":5,"q":"Nneka Obi plans to compare mean reading scores for four teaching styles by testing each possible pair separately at the 5 per cent level. What is the probability that at least one of those tests produces a false positive? (2 decimals)","o":["0.30","0.26","0.74","0.46"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"familywise","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The words every pair, each at 5 per cent, and at least one. Always two steps: count the pairs with m = k(k-1)/2, then 1 - .95 to the power m. Two traps wait for you: multiplying .05 by m, and stopping at .95^m.","plain":"In plain words: each test on its own behaves 95 per cent of the time. For all of them to behave at once you multiply those chances together, and the leftover is the risk that at least one test shouts when it should not.","work":"Step 1. Count the pairs. k = 4 teaching styles, so m = 4 x 3 / 2 = 12 / 2 = 6 comparisons.\nStep 2. Chance one test behaves. 1 - .05 = .95.\nStep 3. Chance all six behave. .95^6 = .7351.\nStep 4. Chance at least one misbehaves. 1 - .7351 = .2649, which rounds to 0.26.\nStep 5. Sense check. It must be above .05 and below 1, and it should be uncomfortable. A 26 per cent risk of a false claim is why post-hoc corrections exist.\nMemory hook: at least one is the opposite of none, so compute none and subtract from 1.","wrongs":["This multiplied alpha by the number of tests: .05 x 6 = .30. That approximation always overshoots, and it would pass 1 for enough tests, which no probability can do.","","This is .95^6 = .74, the chance that NO test gives a false positive. The question asks for at least one, so subtract it from 1.","This counted k(k-1) = 12 comparisons instead of 6, giving 1 - .95^12 = .46. Pair A with B is the same test as B with A, so the count must be halved."],"trap":"Forgetting the divide by 2 in m = k(k-1)/2. With four groups there are 6 pairs, not 12. Write them out if you need to: AB, AC, AD, BC, BD, CD.","tri":"Symptom: all pairwise comparisons at .05, probability of at least one Type I error wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: family-wise error rate. -&gt; Treatment: m = k(k-1)/2, then family-wise error = 1 - .95^m."},{"n":6,"q":"Carlos Reyes compares the mean number of errors made by three groups of air traffic trainees. His printout gives the sums of squares and the degrees of freedom, but the final column was cut off. What is the value of his test statistic? (2 decimals)","o":["0.33","21.00","0.17","10.50"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"f_from_table","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. SS and df are printed and the F cell is empty. The MS column is the step you are being tested on. Any option you can reach by dividing the two SS values directly is there to catch you.","plain":"In plain words: 672 was collected from 2 degrees of freedom and 2016 from 63. Those totals are not comparable until each is turned into an average per degree of freedom.","work":"Step 1. MS between. 672.0 / 2 = 336.0.\nStep 2. MS within. 2016.0 / 63 = 32.0.\nStep 3. F. 336.0 / 32.0 = 10.50.\nStep 4. Sense check. If all three population means were equal, both mean squares would estimate the same thing and F would sit near 1. A value of 10.5 is a long way above 1, so the group means differ by much more than the within group noise alone would explain.\nStep 5. The df check. k = 3 so df between = 2, and N = 66 so df within = 66 - 3 = 63. Both match the table.\nMemory hook: two small divisions first, one big division second.","wrongs":["This is SS between / SS within = 672.0 / 2016.0 = 0.33, dividing the two sums of squares without forming the mean squares.","This left the numerator as a raw sum of squares: 672.0 / 32.0 = 21.00. The between row still needs dividing by its 2 degrees of freedom.","This is MS between / SS within = 336.0 / 2016.0 = 0.17. Here the within row was never divided by its 63 degrees of freedom.",""],"trap":"Taking a ratio of sums of squares. It produces a number under 1 that looks like a proportion of variance, which feels plausible but is not F. Fill in the MS column first, every time.","tri":"Symptom: a one-way table with SS and df, F missing. -&gt; Diagnosis: F from an ANOVA table. -&gt; Treatment: MS between = SS between / df between, MS within = SS within / df within, F = MS between / MS within.","out":{"cap":"One-way ANOVA table, test statistic missing","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Between groups","672.0","2","?","?"],["Within groups","2016.0","63","?",""],["Total","2688.0","65","",""]]}},{"n":7,"q":"Hanna Virtanen compares mean satisfaction scores for four canteen menus and reports F(3, 76) = 4.61 with p = .005. The menu with the highest observed mean is menu C. Which conclusion is justified by this result?","o":["Menu C has a significantly higher population mean satisfaction than each of the other three menus.","The four population mean satisfaction scores are not all equal, and a post-hoc test is now needed to say which menus differ.","Each of the four menus produces a different population mean satisfaction score.","There is a 0.5 per cent chance that the four population means are in fact equal."],"a":1,"pct":75,"typ":"anova_conclude","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. A significant F and an option that names the group with the biggest observed mean. That option is bait. The F test compares no pair, so any conclusion that names a group needs a post-hoc test behind it.","plain":"In plain words: the test says the four menus are not all alike. It does not say that menu C is the winner, even though C scored highest in the sample. Finding the winner is a separate step.","work":"Step 1. Write the hypotheses. H0: all four population means are equal. Ha: not all four are equal.\nStep 2. Decide. p = .005 is below .05, so reject H0.\nStep 3. State the conclusion in the words of Ha. The four population means are not all equal.\nStep 4. Notice the condition for the next step. Rejecting H0 with k = 4, which is more than two groups, is exactly when post-hoc comparisons are appropriate.\nStep 5. Resist the named group. Menu C has the highest SAMPLE mean, which is a description of this sample. Whether its population mean beats the others is untested until a post-hoc comparison is run.\nExplain it to a friend: the race was not a tie, so now you look at the photo finish to see who beat whom.","wrongs":["This names a single menu and claims three separate significant differences. The F test made no pairwise comparison at all.","","This is the \"all means differ\" overstatement. Not all equal is satisfied by one odd menu out of four.","This reads the p-value as a probability about the hypothesis. A p-value is the chance of data this extreme IF the means were equal, never the chance that the means are equal."],"trap":"Promoting the biggest sample mean into a proven winner. A significant F opens the door to post-hoc tests, and only those tests can name a menu.","tri":"Symptom: a significant one-way F plus a named best group. -&gt; Diagnosis: what a significant F licenses, and what needs a post-hoc test. -&gt; Treatment: conclude that the population means are not all equal, then say post-hoc comparisons are required to identify which pairs differ."},{"n":8,"q":"Diego Morales compares mean typing speed across three keyboard layouts. In his output the within-groups row has a mean square of 16.00. Which statement about that number is correct?","o":["It is the amount of variance in typing speed that the three layouts explain.","It is the standard deviation of typing speed within a layout, so a typical typist sits 16 words per minute from their own group mean.","It estimates the variance of typing speed within a layout, and ANOVA assumes this variance is the same in all three populations.","It is the variance of the three group means around the overall mean, so it grows when the layouts differ more."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"mse_meaning","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. The stem names the within-groups mean square, or MSE, and asks what it IS rather than asking for a calculation. Two words must both be right in your answer: WITHIN rather than between or explained, and VARIANCE rather than standard deviation.","plain":"In plain words: MSE is the leftover noise, how much typists scatter around their own layout mean once the layout has been accounted for. It is the measuring stick that the differences between the layout means are compared against.","work":"Step 1. Place the number. It sits in the WITHIN groups row, built from each typist distance from their own group mean.\nStep 2. Name the units. A mean square is a sum of squared deviations divided by df, so it is a variance. The matching standard deviation would be the square root of 16.00, which is 4.00 words per minute.\nStep 3. Recall the assumption. ANOVA pools the groups into one estimate, which is only legitimate if the three populations share a common variance. That is the equal variances or homogeneity assumption.\nStep 4. Rule out the other rows. Explained variance and the spread of the group means both belong to MS between, the numerator of F.\nStep 5. So the correct statement is the one that says within-group variance plus the equal variances assumption.\nMemory hook: within plus square. Inside the groups, and in squared units.","wrongs":["Explained variance belongs to the BETWEEN groups row. MS within is the part the layouts do not explain.","Right row, wrong units. 16.00 is a variance, so the typical distance from a group mean is its square root, 4.00 words per minute.","","That describes MS between, which does grow as the group means spread out. MS within hardly changes when the group means move apart."],"trap":"Quoting a mean square as though it were a standard deviation. Every exam set offers both numbers side by side, here 16.00 and 4.00, so decide which the question wants before you look at the options.","tri":"Symptom: what does MS within or MSE represent. -&gt; Diagnosis: meaning of the error mean square. -&gt; Treatment: MSE is the pooled estimate of the common within-population variance, and it rests on the equal variances assumption. Its square root is the pooled standard deviation. MS between is the row that grows when the group means differ."},{"n":9,"q":"Yara Haddad compares mean recovery times across four physiotherapy protocols and obtains p = .002 for her overall test. Her supervisor asks whether any follow-up analysis is needed. What is the correct advice?","o":["Yes. Because the overall null was rejected and there are more than two protocols, pairwise comparisons with a correction are needed to find which protocols differ.","No. The overall test already shows that every protocol differs from every other, so nothing more is needed.","No. Pairwise comparisons would raise the family-wise error rate, so they must never be run after an ANOVA.","Yes, but she should run the pairwise comparisons at .05 each without any correction, because the overall test has already protected her."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"posthoc_when","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. An overall result followed by a question about follow-up analysis. Two conditions decide it, and both must be met: the overall null was REJECTED, and there are MORE THAN TWO groups. Here both are met, so the answer is yes.","plain":"In plain words: the overall test told her that something differs among the four protocols. It did not tell her what. Post-hoc comparisons are the tool for that second question, and they come with a correction so the error rate stays under control.","work":"Step 1. Check condition one. p = .002 is below .05, so the overall null is rejected.\nStep 2. Check condition two. There are four protocols, so k = 4, which is more than two. With exactly two groups the F test would already have identified the difference and no follow-up would be needed.\nStep 3. Both conditions hold, so post-hoc comparisons are appropriate.\nStep 4. Decide whether a correction is needed. Four protocols give m = 4 x 3 / 2 = 6 pairs. At .05 each the family-wise error rate would be 1 - .95^6 = .26, which is far too high, so a correction is required.\nStep 5. So the advice is yes, with a correction.\nExplain it to a friend: the alarm has told you a room has smoke in it. Now you open the doors one at a time, carefully.","wrongs":["","A significant F never shows that every group differs from every other. That is the \"all means differ\" overstatement, and it is the reason post-hoc tests exist in the first place.","Pairwise comparisons after a significant F are standard practice. It is running them UNCORRECTED that inflates the family-wise error rate, and that is what the correction fixes.","A significant overall F does not protect six separate tests. Without a correction the family-wise error rate climbs to about .26."],"trap":"Thinking the significant F has already done the pairwise work. It has only opened the door. The names of the differing pairs come from the post-hoc test.","tri":"Symptom: a significant F with more than two groups and a question about next steps. -&gt; Diagnosis: when post-hoc tests are needed. -&gt; Treatment: significant F plus k greater than 2 means run pairwise comparisons, with a correction such as Bonferroni or Tukey to hold the family-wise error rate near .05."},{"n":10,"q":"Stefan Vogel compares the mean number of sick days taken by employees in five departments. He believes the call centre has the highest average. Which pair of hypotheses is appropriate for the test that compares all five means at once?","o":["H0: the call centre mean equals the average of the other four means. Ha: the call centre mean is higher.","H0: the five sample means are equal. Ha: the five sample means are not all equal.","H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4 = mu5. Ha: not all five population means are equal.","H0: not all five population means are equal. Ha: all five population means are equal."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"anova_hyp","theme":6,"spot":"HINT. Five groups and a request for the hypotheses, with a researcher hunch thrown in to tempt you. The pair never changes: H0 all population means equal, Ha not all equal. No directions, no sample means, and H0 is always the equality.","plain":"In plain words: the test asks whether the five population means could all be the same. His hunch about the call centre is a question for later, with a planned or post-hoc comparison.","work":"Step 1. Count the groups. Five departments, so k = 5 population means.\nStep 2. Write H0. mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4 = mu5, five population means set equal to one another.\nStep 3. Write Ha as the exact opposite. Not all five are equal. That one phrase covers every way the equality could break.\nStep 4. Check the three standard traps. Are the hypotheses about populations and not samples? Yes. Does H0 carry the equality? Yes. Is Ha free of any direction? Yes.\nStep 5. Park his hunch. The call centre idea is a specific comparison. It can be tested with a planned contrast or examined after a significant F, but it is not what the overall F tests.\nMemory hook: H0 is a row of equals signs. Ha is five words: not all means are equal.","wrongs":["This is a planned comparison of one group against the rest, and it is directional. The overall F compares all five means at once and has no direction.","Hypotheses are always about POPULATION means. Sample means are known quantities, so there is nothing to hypothesise about them.","","This has H0 and Ha swapped. H0 must always be the statement of equality, because that is the claim the test tries to overturn."],"trap":"Letting the researcher expectation make Ha directional, or writing the hypotheses about the sample means. Check both every time: population, and no arrows.","tri":"Symptom: several groups, hypotheses wanted, a directional expectation mentioned. -&gt; Diagnosis: hypotheses for a one-way ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: H0: mu1 = ... = muk. Ha: not all equal. Hypotheses are about POPULATION means, H0 always carries the equality, and the F test is never directional."}]},{"id":"t7a","name":"Two-factor ANOVA - Set A","short":"Two-factor ANOVA - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on designs with two factors at once: the df for an interaction and for the error, what a significant interaction allows you to say, filling in MS and F in a printed two-way table, reading an interaction plot, and why one two-factor analysis beats two separate one-way analyses. The df question is the single most likely item on the whole paper, so it appears twice.","theme":7,"icon":"🔀","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Anneke Bosma studies how quickly people spot errors in a text. She uses three kinds of feedback (none, written, spoken) and two levels of time pressure (relaxed, hurried), and puts 12 different participants in each combination. Her software asks her to state the degrees of freedom for the feedback by time pressure test and for the error term. What are they? (whole numbers)","o":["6 and 66","2 and 66","2 and 71","5 and 66"],"a":1,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. The stem gives you the number of levels of two factors and the number of people in each combination, then asks for df. That is the df question. Count three things and nothing else: a (levels of factor one), b (levels of factor two), N (total people).","plain":"In plain words: how many numbers are free to move in the interaction test, and how many are left over for the error. One is a product of two small numbers, the other is everybody minus the number of cells.","work":"Step 1. Read off a and b. Feedback has 3 levels, so a = 3. Time pressure has 2 levels, so b = 2.\nStep 2. Count the cells. ab = 3 x 2 = 6 combinations.\nStep 3. Count the people. 12 in each of the 6 cells, so N = 6 x 12 = 72.\nStep 4. Interaction df. (a - 1)(b - 1) = (3 - 1)(2 - 1) = 2 x 1 = 2.\nStep 5. Error df. N - ab = 72 - 6 = 66.\nStep 6. So the pair is 2 and 66.\nMemory hook: the interaction df is a PRODUCT, never a count. Multiply the two \"one less than\" numbers. And the error df takes away one df for every cell, so it is N minus the number of cells.","wrongs":["6 is the number of CELLS, not the interaction df. This is the trap the examiner plants every time: 3 x 2 = 6 looks like an answer, but the formula subtracts one from each factor first, so it is 2 x 1 = 2.","","71 is N - 1 = 72 - 1, which is the TOTAL df for the whole analysis, not the error df. The error df still has to give up one df for each of the 6 cells.","5 is ab - 1 = 6 - 1, the df for all six cells treated as one set of groups. That is the df you would use if you ran a one-way ANOVA on the six combinations. It is not the interaction df."],"trap":"Writing the number of cells where the product belongs. Say it out loud: \"minus one, minus one, then MULTIPLY\". 3 x 2 = 6 is the design; (3 - 1)(2 - 1) = 2 is the df.","tri":"Symptom: two factors, their level counts, a number per cell, and a request for df. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a two-factor ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: interaction df = (a - 1)(b - 1). Error df = N - ab, where ab is the number of cells and N is the total number of people."},{"n":2,"q":"Tobias Reinhardt has mislaid his notes about a study he ran last year. His printed output still shows 6 for the interaction row and 84 for the error row, and he remembers that one of his two factors had 3 levels. He wants to reconstruct how many levels the other factor had and how many people took part. What were they? (whole numbers)","o":["7 levels and 96 people","4 levels and 85 people","4 levels and 91 people","4 levels and 96 people"],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. The df are GIVEN and the design is missing. That is the same question turned around. Do not look for a new formula: use the two you already know and solve them as little equations.","plain":"In plain words: you know the answers 6 and 84 and you have to find the question. Work backwards through the two df formulas.","work":"Step 1. Use the interaction row. (a - 1)(b - 1) = 6 and a = 3, so (3 - 1)(b - 1) = 6, which is 2(b - 1) = 6.\nStep 2. Divide both sides by 2. b - 1 = 3, so b = 4. The other factor had 4 levels.\nStep 3. Count the cells. ab = 3 x 4 = 12.\nStep 4. Use the error row. N - ab = 84, so N - 12 = 84, so N = 96.\nStep 5. Sense check. 96 people over 12 cells is 8 per cell, a whole number, which is what a balanced design should give. If the division had come out ragged you would know something was wrong.\nExplain it to a friend: the 6 tells you the SHAPE of the design, the 84 tells you how many people filled it.","wrongs":["This reads the interaction df as b - 1 and forgets that the other factor contributes a factor of 2. It gives b - 1 = 6, so b = 7. But a 3 by 7 design would have an interaction df of 2 x 6 = 12, not 6.","The 4 levels are right, but this treats the error df as N - 1, giving N = 84 + 1 = 85. The error df is N - ab, so 12 df have to be added back, not 1.","The 4 levels are right, but this uses N - a - b = 84, giving N = 84 + 3 + 4 = 91. The formula subtracts the PRODUCT of the level counts (12), not their sum (7).",""],"trap":"Going for the error row first. You cannot, because N - ab needs ab, and you only learn b from the interaction row. Interaction row first, always.","tri":"Symptom: df printed, design unknown. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a two-factor ANOVA, worked backwards. -&gt; Treatment: write (a - 1)(b - 1) = interaction df and solve for the missing level count. Then write N - ab = error df and solve for N. Do the interaction row FIRST, because you need ab before you can get N."},{"n":3,"q":"Yusuf Demir gives volunteers either caffeine or a placebo after either a normal night of sleep or a shortened one, and measures reaction time. The caffeine by sleep test comes out significant. What does that significant result tell him?","o":["That the size of the caffeine effect depends on how much sleep the person had, so the single overall caffeine effect can be misleading on its own.","That both caffeine and sleep must also have significant main effects.","That caffeine intake and amount of sleep are correlated with each other in his sample.","That caffeine has no effect on reaction time, because the interaction has taken over the explanation."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"interaction_interp","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Two factors, a significant interaction, and \"what does this tell him\". Do not try to compute anything. The answer is always a sentence of the form \"the effect of ONE factor depends on the level of the OTHER\".","plain":"In plain words: caffeine does not do one single thing. It does one thing to a rested person and something else to a tired person. So quoting one average caffeine effect hides two different stories.","work":"Step 1. Write down what an interaction means. The two factors do not simply add up. What one factor does changes across the levels of the other.\nStep 2. Put it in his words. The reaction-time benefit from caffeine is one size after a normal night and a different size after a short night.\nStep 3. Work out the consequence. The main effect of caffeine is a single average over both sleep conditions. If the real effect is big in one and small in the other, that average describes neither.\nStep 4. So the honest report leads with the interaction and then gives the caffeine effect separately for the rested and the sleep-deprived group.\nMemory hook: interaction = \"it depends\". Whenever you can answer the question \"does caffeine help?\" only with \"depends on how tired you are\", you have an interaction.","wrongs":["","Main effects and the interaction are three separate tests. An interaction can be significant while one or both main effects are not, for example when caffeine helps the tired group by as much as it harms the rested group, so the two cancel in the average.","An interaction is not a correlation between the two factors. In this design the experimenter decided who got caffeine and who was sleep deprived, so the two factors are deliberately unrelated. An interaction is about the OUTCOME behaving differently in different combinations.","The interaction does not switch the caffeine effect off. It says the effect varies. In fact it usually means caffeine matters a great deal, just not equally everywhere."],"trap":"Treating the interaction as a rival to the main effects, as if only one of them can be true. They answer different questions. The interaction tells you whether you are allowed to quote a main effect as a single number.","tri":"Symptom: a significant interaction between two factors. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting an interaction. -&gt; Treatment: say \"the effect of factor A depends on the level of factor B\", then add the consequence: the main effect of A, read on its own, averages over conditions where A does different things, so it is misleading. The fix is to describe the effect of A separately at each level of B (simple effects)."},{"n":4,"q":"Priya Raghunathan compares two teaching styles across three class sizes, with 10 different pupils in each combination. Her software has printed the sums of squares and the degrees of freedom but not the rest of the table. What is the F ratio for the style by size test? (2 decimals)","o":["0.89","2.62","2.40","4.80"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"ms_from_ss","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. A printed table with an SS column and a df column but no MS or F column, and a question asking for one F. That is the fill-in-the-table question. You never need the raw data, only two divisions and one more division.","plain":"In plain words: turn each sum of squares into a mean square by dividing by its own df, then divide the effect mean square by the error mean square. That is all an F ratio is.","work":"Step 1. Get the mean square for the interaction. MS = SS / df = 48.0 / 2 = 24.00.\nStep 2. Get the mean square for the error. MS error = 540.0 / 54 = 10.00.\nStep 3. Divide. F = MS interaction / MS error = 24.00 / 10.00 = 2.40.\nStep 4. Sense check. F is a bit above 1, so the interaction variance is only about two and a half times the noise. With df 2 and 54 that is not far enough out to be convincing, and indeed p is about .10.\nStep 5. Note the shared denominator. If you wanted the other two F ratios you would reuse the same 10.00: F for style = 84.0 / 1 / 10.00 = 8.40, F for size = 126.0 / 2 / 10.00 = 6.30.\nMemory hook: SS is raw material, MS is SS per degree of freedom, F is signal MS over noise MS. Divide twice, then divide once more.","wrongs":["This divides the interaction SS by the ERROR df: 48.0 / 54 = 0.89. Every row must be divided by its own df, and the interaction row has 2.","This uses the total df to make the error mean square: 540.0 / 59 = 9.1525, then 24.00 / 9.1525 = 2.62. The error row has 54 df, not 59. The 59 belongs to the Total row and is never used as a denominator df.","","This forgets to turn the interaction SS into an MS and divides the raw SS by the error MS: 48.0 / 10.00 = 4.80. An F ratio is always MS over MS, never SS over MS. This is the classic \"SS divided instead of MS\" slip, and it doubles the answer here because the interaction df is 2."],"trap":"Mixing the df up between rows. Cover the table with your hand and write the two divisions out separately: 48 over 2, then 540 over 54. Only then make the fraction.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way table with SS and df printed and an MS or F asked for. -&gt; Diagnosis: MS and F inside a two-way table. -&gt; Treatment: MS = SS / df for the row you want, MS error = SS error / df error, then F = MS effect / MS error. Each row uses its OWN df. All three F ratios in a two-factor table share the same MS error.","out":{"cap":"Two-way ANOVA: teaching style by class size","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Teaching style","84.0","1"],["Class size","126.0","2"],["Style x Size","48.0","2"],["Error","540.0","54"],["Total","798.0","59"]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Lars Engdahl tests how well people read text on a screen at three font sizes, in two age groups. He plots the six cell means with font size along the bottom and one line for each age group. The means are printed below, and the two lines in his plot run parallel. What does the picture show?","o":["An interaction, because the two lines are far apart from each other.","A main effect of age and a main effect of font size, but no interaction, because the lines are separated and sloping yet parallel.","No main effect of age, because the two lines never cross.","An interaction, because both lines slope upwards."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"read_plot","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Cell means plotted as lines, or a table of cell means, and a question about what the picture shows. Ask three questions in a fixed order: are the lines PARALLEL (no interaction), are they SEPARATED vertically (main effect of the line factor), do they SLOPE (main effect of the bottom-axis factor).","plain":"In plain words: the younger group reads 6 words a minute more at every font size, and everybody gains 4 words for each step up in font size. Two separate stories that do not interfere with each other.","work":"Step 1. Check for parallel. Under 40 minus Over 60 is 10 - 4 = 6, then 14 - 8 = 6, then 18 - 12 = 6. The gap is the same at all three font sizes, so the lines are parallel and there is no interaction.\nStep 2. Check the vertical separation. The gap of 6 is not zero, and it is there at every font size, so the younger group reads faster throughout. That is a main effect of age.\nStep 3. Check the slope. Within each age group the means climb by 4 for each step up in font size: 10, 14, 18 and 4, 8, 12. Both lines slope upwards, so bigger font helps. That is a main effect of font size.\nStep 4. Put it together: two main effects, no interaction. The two factors simply add up.\nMemory hook: PARALLEL means no interaction, full stop. Then the GAP gives you one main effect and the SLOPE gives you the other.","wrongs":["Distance between the lines is a MAIN EFFECT of the line factor, not an interaction. An interaction is about the gap CHANGING, not about the gap being large.","","Crossing has nothing to do with main effects. The lines sit 6 words apart at every font size, which is exactly what a main effect of age looks like.","Both lines sloping the same way is a main effect of font size. It would only be an interaction if they sloped by DIFFERENT amounts."],"trap":"Reading \"the lines are far apart\" or \"the lines go up\" as an interaction. Neither is. The only question an interaction answers is whether the lines are parallel.","tri":"Symptom: an interaction plot or a grid of cell means. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading main effects and the interaction off a picture. -&gt; Treatment: parallel lines mean no interaction. A constant vertical gap means a main effect of the factor drawn as separate LINES. A slope away from flat means a main effect of the factor on the bottom AXIS.","out":{"cap":"Mean words read correctly per minute (six cell means)","h":["Age group","Small font","Medium font","Large font"],"r":[["Under 40","10.0","14.0","18.0"],["Over 60","4.0","8.0","12.0"]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Chiara Donati studies how well staff perform under three supervisor styles on day and night shifts, with 20 staff in each of the six combinations. Her printout has lost one number from the SS column, shown as a question mark below. What is the missing sum of squares? (1 decimal)","o":["165.0","260.0","580.0","1065.0"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"ss_from_table","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. A printed table with a gap in the SS column. No new formula is needed. The SS column ADDS DOWN to the Total row, so the missing number is the Total minus everything else in the column.","plain":"In plain words: the total variation is cut into four slices and three of the slices are printed. Find the fourth by subtraction.","work":"Step 1. Write the identity. SS style + SS shift + SS interaction + SS error = SS total.\nStep 2. Fill in what you have. 320.0 + 95.0 + SS interaction + 900.0 = 1480.0.\nStep 3. Add the printed pieces. 320.0 + 95.0 + 900.0 = 1315.0.\nStep 4. Subtract. SS interaction = 1480.0 - 1315.0 = 165.0.\nStep 5. Check on the df column, which adds down in exactly the same way: 2 + 1 + 2 + 114 = 119, and 119 is the Total df. The column really does add, so the method is right. (119 = N - 1 with N = 120, which matches 20 staff in each of the 6 cells.)\nMemory hook: in any ANOVA table, both the SS column and the df column add down to the Total row. Use the df column as a free check on your arithmetic.","wrongs":["","This forgets the shift row: 1480.0 - 320.0 - 900.0 = 260.0. Every source line above the Total has to come out, and there are three of them here.","This subtracts only the error: 1480.0 - 900.0 = 580.0. That is the SS for the whole model (all three effects together), not for the interaction alone.","This forgets the error row: 1480.0 - 320.0 - 95.0 = 1065.0. The error is usually the biggest slice of the total, so leaving it out gives a wildly inflated answer. Notice it is larger than the error SS itself, which should ring an alarm."],"trap":"Subtracting some but not all of the printed rows. Tick each source line off with a pencil as you subtract it, then verify on the df column.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way ANOVA table with one SS missing. -&gt; Diagnosis: recovering a sum of squares. -&gt; Treatment: SS A + SS B + SS AxB + SS error = SS total. Subtract every printed SS from the total. Check your work on the df column, which adds the same way.","out":{"cap":"Two-way ANOVA with one sum of squares missing","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Supervisor style","320.0","2"],["Shift (day or night)","95.0","1"],["Style x Shift","?","2"],["Error","900.0","114"],["Total","1480.0","119"]]}},{"n":7,"q":"Oluwaseun Adebayo has run a study with two factors, drink type at 2 levels and room temperature at 3 levels, and the output has appeared on her screen. Before reading it she wants to know what to expect. How many F ratios should the analysis produce, and what do they have in common?","o":["Two F ratios, one for each factor, and each has its own error term.","Six F ratios, one for each combination of drink type and room temperature.","One F ratio for the whole model, exactly as in a one-way ANOVA.","Three F ratios, one for each main effect and one for the interaction, and all three are divided by the same mean square error."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"two_way_concept","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. The stem describes a two-factor design and asks what the analysis DELIVERS, with no numbers to compute. That is the structure question. The answer never changes: three questions, three F ratios, one shared denominator.","plain":"In plain words: a two-factor ANOVA asks three questions at once. Does factor one matter on average? Does factor two matter on average? Do they work differently in combination? Each question gets its own F, and all three are judged against the same pile of leftover noise.","work":"Step 1. List the effects. A two-factor design splits the explained variation three ways: factor A on its own, factor B on its own, and the A by B interaction.\nStep 2. Count the F ratios. One per effect, so three.\nStep 3. Find the denominators. The table has a single Error row, so a single MS error. Every one of the three F ratios uses it: F_A = MS_A / MS error, F_B = MS_B / MS error, F_AB = MS_AB / MS error.\nStep 4. Note why the shared error matters. The two factors and the interaction are all judged against the same noise, which is what makes the three tests comparable and what keeps the analysis efficient.\nExplain it to a friend: three different questions, one shared measuring stick for noise.","wrongs":["The interaction is a third effect in its own right, so two F ratios is one too few. And the table has one Error row, so the F ratios share a denominator rather than each having their own.","The six cells are the design, not the tests. You would get six group means, not six F ratios. Confusing cells with tests is the same mistake as putting the number of cells in the interaction df.","A one-way ANOVA has one factor and so one F. The whole point of adding a second factor is to get the second main effect and the interaction as well.",""],"trap":"Forgetting that the interaction is a test, not just a side remark. Three effects means three F ratios, every time, in every balanced two-factor design.","tri":"Symptom: a two-factor design and a question about how many tests, or what the analysis tests. -&gt; Diagnosis: the structure of a two-factor ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: three effects (main effect of A, main effect of B, A by B interaction) means three F ratios. Each F = its own MS divided by the ONE MS error for the whole table."},{"n":8,"q":"Nadia Haddad has data on reading scores from pupils who were given either a quiet or a noisy room, and who worked either alone or in pairs. A colleague suggests she run one analysis comparing quiet with noisy and a second, separate analysis comparing alone with paired. Why is one analysis with both factors together better?","o":["Because two separate analyses would each need their own fresh sample of pupils, which she does not have.","Because only a design with two factors allows her to make a causal claim about either factor.","Because the two-factor analysis can test whether noise and working arrangement act differently in combination, which two separate analyses cannot see, and because each factor is taken out of the other one's error term, which makes both tests more sensitive.","Because the two-factor analysis removes the need for post-hoc comparisons."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"two_way_adv","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. The stem offers you the choice between one two-factor analysis and two one-way analyses, and asks why one is better. Two things to say, always the same two: you GAIN the interaction, and you SHRINK the error term.","plain":"In plain words: running them separately throws away the most interesting question (do the two things depend on each other?) and it also leaves each factor's variation sitting in the other analysis as noise.","work":"Step 1. What do two separate analyses give you? Two main effects, roughly the same two main effects as the factorial design. So the main effects are not the gain.\nStep 2. What is missing? Neither separate analysis can ask whether noise matters more when working alone than when working in pairs. That question only exists when both factors are in the same model. Gain number one: the interaction.\nStep 3. What happens to the error term? In the noise-only analysis, the variation caused by working alone or in pairs has nowhere to go, so it lands in the error. In the two-factor analysis it gets its own row, so SS error and therefore MS error are smaller. Gain number two: a smaller denominator, so a bigger F and more power.\nStep 4. There is a third, practical gain: one analysis on one sample instead of two, so fewer tests and a smaller family-wise error rate.\nMemory hook: you gain a question (the interaction) and you lose some noise (the other factor leaves the error term).","wrongs":["Both analyses would use the same pupils. Sample size is not the issue; what changes is how the variation in those pupils is divided up.","Causal claims come from random ASSIGNMENT to conditions, not from how many factors are in the model. A one-way experiment with random assignment supports a causal claim perfectly well.","","Post-hoc comparisons are still needed for any factor with three or more levels, and for following up a significant interaction. The factorial design does not remove them."],"trap":"Answering only \"you can test the interaction\" and stopping. The examiner usually puts a half-right option next to a fully right one. The error term argument is the second half of the answer.","tri":"Symptom: one two-factor design against two separate one-way analyses. -&gt; Diagnosis: the advantage of a factorial design. -&gt; Treatment: name the two gains. 1 The interaction becomes testable; two one-way analyses have no way to ask it. 2 The variance due to the other factor moves out of the error term and into its own row, so MS error drops and both main effect tests gain power."},{"n":9,"q":"Mateo Quiroga compares two kinds of therapy given in either short or long sessions and plots the four cell means, printed below, with session length along the bottom and one line per therapy. In his plot the two lines cross. Which one of these statements is FALSE?","o":["The lines have to cross before an interaction can be said to be present.","The picture points to an interaction, because the gap between the two therapies changes with session length.","Reporting a single overall effect of therapy type would be misleading here.","If the two lines had been parallel, that would have pointed to no interaction."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"read_plot","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. \"Which statement is FALSE\" plus an interaction plot. One of the four options is nearly always the crossing myth: that lines must cross for an interaction. They need only stop being parallel.","plain":"In plain words: three of these sentences are fair readings of the picture and one is a rule that does not exist. Find the invented rule.","work":"Step 1. Measure the gaps in the table. In short sessions mindfulness is ahead by 20.0 - 12.0 = 8.0. In long sessions talking therapy is ahead by 22.0 - 18.0 = 4.0.\nStep 2. The gap has changed size AND flipped sign, so the lines are far from parallel. There is an interaction here, and the lines do happen to cross.\nStep 3. Now test each statement. \"The gap changes with session length\" is exactly what the numbers show, so that is TRUE.\nStep 4. \"A single overall therapy effect would be misleading\" is TRUE: averaged over session length, talking therapy gives (12.0 + 22.0) / 2 = 17.0 and mindfulness gives (20.0 + 18.0) / 2 = 19.0, a difference of only 2.0, which describes neither session length honestly.\nStep 5. \"Parallel lines would have pointed to no interaction\" is TRUE, and it is the standard rule.\nStep 6. \"Lines have to cross\" is FALSE. Lines that both rise but at clearly different rates never cross and still show an interaction. So that is the answer.\nMemory hook: crossing is sufficient, not necessary. Non-parallel is the test.","wrongs":["","True. In short sessions the gap favours mindfulness by 8.0 points, in long sessions it favours talking therapy by 4.0. A changing gap is an interaction.","True. The two therapies average out to 17.0 and 19.0, which makes them look almost equal, yet they are 8.0 apart one way in short sessions and 4.0 the other way in long sessions.","True. Parallel lines are the textbook picture of no interaction, and that is the only rule you need."],"trap":"Believing that no crossing means no interaction. Two lines that fan apart are not parallel, so the interaction is there even though they never meet.","tri":"Symptom: an interaction plot and \"which is false\". -&gt; Diagnosis: reading an interaction plot, with a planted myth. -&gt; Treatment: the only rule is PARALLEL means no interaction, NOT PARALLEL means interaction. Crossing is one way of not being parallel, never a requirement.","out":{"cap":"Mean improvement score in four cells","h":["Therapy","Short sessions","Long sessions"],"r":[["Talking therapy","12.0","22.0"],["Mindfulness training","20.0","18.0"]]}},{"n":10,"q":"Sanne Vermeulen tests a new painkiller against a placebo in men and women. The main effect of the painkiller is significant and so is the painkiller by sex test. Her supervisor asks how she should write up the results. What should she do?","o":["Report the main effect of the painkiller and leave the interaction out, because the main effect is the more important test.","Report the interaction first and then describe the painkiller effect separately for men and for women, because the single overall painkiller effect averages over two different results.","Report that the painkiller does not work at all, because the significant interaction cancels out the main effect.","Report that being male or female causes the painkiller to work, because the interaction is significant."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"interaction_interp","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. A significant main effect AND a significant interaction involving that same factor, plus \"how should she write it up\". The order of reporting is the point: the interaction qualifies the main effect, so the interaction comes first.","plain":"In plain words: the painkiller works on average, but not by the same amount in the two groups. So \"it works\" is not the whole story, and the average on its own is a half-truth.","work":"Step 1. What does the main effect say? Averaged over men and women, the painkiller beats the placebo.\nStep 2. What does the interaction say? The size of that benefit is not the same in the two groups.\nStep 3. So what is the average worth? It is a blend of two different numbers. It is not wrong, but on its own it describes nobody in the study, in the same way an average of 10 and 2 describes neither 10 nor 2.\nStep 4. The correct write-up: state the interaction, then split it. Report the painkiller against placebo in men, and the painkiller against placebo in women, with their own tests.\nStep 5. Note what she may NOT say. She may not claim sex CAUSES the difference. Sex was not assigned, it was measured, so the interaction tells her the effect varies with sex, not why.\nExplain it to a friend: saying \"the pill works\" when it works twice as well in one group is like saying \"the bus takes 20 minutes\" when it takes 10 minutes one way and 30 the other.","wrongs":["The interaction is the finding that qualifies the main effect. Leaving it out is exactly the misleading report the interaction warns you about.","","A significant interaction does not cancel a significant main effect. Both are real. The painkiller does beat the placebo on average; the interaction says the size of the win varies.","This reads causation into a measured grouping variable. Sex was not randomly assigned, so she may say the painkiller effect DIFFERS between men and women, not that sex causes it. The mechanism, such as body mass or metabolism, is untested."],"trap":"Choosing between the main effect and the interaction as if only one survives. Both are reported. The interaction simply decides how the main effect must be described.","tri":"Symptom: a significant main effect sitting next to a significant interaction that involves it. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting an interaction, reporting order. -&gt; Treatment: whenever a factor is in a significant interaction, its main effect may not be quoted alone. Lead with the interaction, then give the simple effects: the effect of the factor at each level of the other factor."}]},{"id":"t7b","name":"Two-factor ANOVA - Set B","short":"Two-factor ANOVA - Set B","blurb":"Ten more two-factor questions in different shapes: df forwards and the error df on its own, an F ratio and a sum of squares recovered from printed output, MS turned back into SS, parallel and fanning interaction plots, what a non-significant interaction permits, and the false statement about the three F ratios. The df question appears twice again.","theme":7,"icon":"🔀","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Kwame Asante studies how long people persist at a puzzle. He uses four kinds of background music and three room temperatures, with 9 different volunteers in each combination. For the music by temperature test, what pair of degrees of freedom should he report? (whole numbers)","o":["12 and 96","11 and 96","6 and 96","6 and 107"],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Levels of two factors plus a number per cell plus \"what pair of degrees of freedom\". Always the same three ingredients: a, b and N. Write them in the margin before you touch the options.","plain":"In plain words: the first number is how much freedom the combination effect has, the second is how much is left over for the noise.","work":"Step 1. a = 4 kinds of music, b = 3 temperatures.\nStep 2. Cells: ab = 4 x 3 = 12.\nStep 3. People: N = 12 cells x 9 volunteers = 108.\nStep 4. Interaction df: (4 - 1)(3 - 1) = 3 x 2 = 6.\nStep 5. Error df: N - ab = 108 - 12 = 96.\nStep 6. So report F(6, 96).\nStep 7. Free check: the df column of the whole table must add to N - 1 = 107. Indeed 3 (music) + 2 (temperature) + 6 (interaction) + 96 (error) = 107.\nMemory hook: minus one, minus one, multiply. Then everybody minus the cells.","wrongs":["12 is the number of cells, 4 x 3. The formula subtracts one from each level count BEFORE multiplying, so it is 3 x 2 = 6. This is the single most common wrong answer on this question type.","11 is ab - 1 = 12 - 1, the df you would use for a one-way ANOVA treating the twelve combinations as twelve groups. It is not the interaction df.","","107 is N - 1, the Total df for the whole analysis. The error df has to give up one df per cell as well, so it is 108 - 12 = 96."],"trap":"Stopping at 4 x 3. The product in the formula is of (a - 1) and (b - 1), not of a and b.","tri":"Symptom: two factors with their level counts and a number per cell, df requested. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a two-factor ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: interaction df = (a - 1)(b - 1), a PRODUCT. Error df = N - ab, where ab counts the cells."},{"n":2,"q":"Beatrix Kovacs runs a study with two types of instruction and five task difficulties, placing 7 different participants in each of the combinations. Her supervisor asks only for the degrees of freedom of the error term. What is it? (whole number)","o":["60","63","64","69"],"a":0,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Same ingredients as the full df question, but only the ERROR df is wanted. Do not be tempted to compute the interaction df and hand that in. Read what is asked.","plain":"In plain words: of all the people in the study, how many degrees of freedom are left once every cell has been given its own mean.","work":"Step 1. a = 2 types of instruction, b = 5 difficulties.\nStep 2. Cells: ab = 2 x 5 = 10.\nStep 3. People: N = 10 cells x 7 participants = 70.\nStep 4. Error df: N - ab = 70 - 10 = 60.\nStep 5. Check it against the rest of the table. The effect df are (a - 1) = 1 for instruction, (b - 1) = 4 for difficulty and (a - 1)(b - 1) = 4 for the interaction, which is 9 in total. Add the error: 9 + 60 = 69 = N - 1. The table balances, so 60 is right.\nMemory hook: every cell costs you one degree of freedom, because each cell gets its own mean. Ten cells, ten df gone.","wrongs":["","63 comes from N - a - b = 70 - 2 - 5. The formula takes away the PRODUCT of the level counts (10), not their sum (7).","64 comes from starting at N - 1 = 69 and taking off only the two main effect df: 69 - 1 - 4 = 64. The interaction df of 4 also has to come out.","69 is N - 1, the Total df. It is the df for the whole analysis before any effect has been accounted for, not the error df."],"trap":"Reaching for N - 1 because it is the df you use most often elsewhere. In a two-factor ANOVA the error df is N minus the number of CELLS.","tri":"Symptom: a two-factor design with a number per cell, error df requested. -&gt; Diagnosis: error df in a two-factor ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: error df = N - ab. Count the cells (ab), count the people (N), subtract. Do not use N - 1 and do not use N - a - b."},{"n":3,"q":"Johan Mkhize tests how many puzzles children solve when rewarded with points or with badges, at three levels of task difficulty. The six cell means are printed below. He plots them with difficulty along the bottom and one line for each reward type. What does the picture tell him?","o":["There is an interaction, because one line sits above the other throughout.","There is no interaction and there are no main effects, because the lines never cross.","There is an interaction, because both lines fall as the task gets harder.","There is no interaction, because the lines are parallel. The constant gap of 10 puzzles shows a main effect of reward type and the downward slope shows a main effect of difficulty."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"read_plot","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. A grid of cell means and \"what does the picture tell him\". Subtract one row from the other across the columns. A constant difference means parallel lines means no interaction.","plain":"In plain words: badges beat points by exactly 10 puzzles whatever the difficulty, and everybody does worse as the task gets harder. Two clean, separate effects.","work":"Step 1. Subtract across. Badges minus points: 65.0 - 55.0 = 10.0, 58.0 - 48.0 = 10.0, 50.0 - 40.0 = 10.0.\nStep 2. The gap is 10.0 at every difficulty level, so the lines are parallel. No interaction.\nStep 3. The gap is not zero and it holds everywhere, so badges beat points overall. Main effect of reward type.\nStep 4. Within each line the means fall: 55.0, 48.0, 40.0 and 65.0, 58.0, 50.0. Harder tasks mean fewer puzzles. Main effect of difficulty.\nStep 5. So the full reading is: two main effects, no interaction, and because the lines are parallel both main effects can be reported as single numbers.\nMemory hook: subtract one line from the other. If the answer is the same number every time, the lines are parallel.","wrongs":["One line sitting above the other is a MAIN EFFECT of reward type. An interaction needs the GAP to change, and here it is 10.0 throughout.","Crossing is not the test. And there plainly are main effects: a steady 10.0 gap between the reward types and a fall of 15.0 puzzles from easy to hard.","Both lines falling together is a main effect of difficulty. It would only be an interaction if one line fell more steeply than the other.",""],"trap":"Treating separation or slope as evidence of an interaction. Separation is one main effect, slope is the other main effect, and only a CHANGE in the gap is an interaction.","tri":"Symptom: cell means or an interaction plot. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading main effects and the interaction off the picture. -&gt; Treatment: parallel means no interaction; a constant vertical gap means a main effect of the LINE factor; a slope means a main effect of the AXIS factor. Check parallel first, because it decides whether the main effects may be quoted on their own at all.","out":{"cap":"Mean number of puzzles solved (six cell means)","h":["Reward","Easy","Medium","Hard"],"r":[["Points","55.0","48.0","40.0"],["Badges","65.0","58.0","50.0"]]}},{"n":4,"q":"Rafael Montoya compares three packaging designs across three shelf positions in a supermarket, with 6 stores in each combination. His output lists the sums of squares and the degrees of freedom only. What is the F ratio for packaging design? (2 decimals)","o":["0.30","13.33","15.70","26.67"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"ms_from_ss","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. SS and df printed, an F wanted. Two mean squares, then one division. The row you are asked about supplies the numerator; the Error row always supplies the denominator.","plain":"In plain words: squeeze each sum of squares down to a per-degree-of-freedom figure, then ask how many times bigger the packaging figure is than the noise figure.","work":"Step 1. MS for packaging design: 240.0 / 2 = 120.00.\nStep 2. MS error: 405.0 / 45 = 9.00.\nStep 3. F = 120.00 / 9.00 = 13.3333, which is 13.33 to 2 decimals.\nStep 4. Sense check. 13.33 on df 2 and 45 is a long way above 1, so packaging design clearly matters (p is well below .001).\nStep 5. The same MS error of 9.00 serves the other two tests: shelf position gives (90.0 / 2) / 9.00 = 5.00 and the interaction gives (160.0 / 4) / 9.00 = 4.44.\nMemory hook: one denominator for the whole table. Compute MS error once and reuse it three times.","wrongs":["0.30 comes from dividing the packaging MS by the error SS instead of the error MS: 120.00 / 405.0 = 0.296. The denominator of an F is always a mean square, so the error SS must be divided by 45 first.","","15.70 comes from using the Total df to make the error mean square: 405.0 / 53 = 7.6415, then 120.00 / 7.6415 = 15.70. The error row has 45 df. The 53 in the Total row is never a denominator df.","26.67 comes from forgetting to turn the packaging SS into an MS: 240.0 / 9.00 = 26.67. That is the \"SS divided instead of MS\" slip, and it doubles the answer here because the packaging df is 2."],"trap":"Using the wrong df on one of the two divisions. Write \"MS design = 240 / 2\" and \"MS error = 405 / 45\" as two separate lines before you make the fraction.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way table with SS and df, F requested for one row. -&gt; Diagnosis: MS and F in a two-way table. -&gt; Treatment: MS effect = SS effect / df effect. MS error = SS error / df error. F = MS effect / MS error. Never divide an SS by an MS.","out":{"cap":"Two-way ANOVA: packaging design by shelf position","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Packaging design","240.0","2"],["Shelf position","90.0","2"],["Design x Position","160.0","4"],["Error","405.0","45"],["Total","895.0","53"]]}},{"n":5,"q":"Fatima Zahra el Amrani studies how long customers wait at a help desk under two staffing levels and four times of day, with 15 customers in each combination. Her printout is missing the error sum of squares, shown as a question mark below. What is it? (1 decimal)","o":["1440.0","1800.0","1200.0","2880.0"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"ss_from_table","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. A gap in the SS column of a printed table. The column adds down to the Total, so the gap is Total minus every other entry in that column. Nothing is computed from the data.","plain":"In plain words: the total variation was cut into four pieces. Three pieces are printed. The error is whatever is left.","work":"Step 1. Write the identity. 180.0 + 420.0 + 240.0 + SS error = 2040.0.\nStep 2. Add the three effects. 180.0 + 420.0 + 240.0 = 840.0.\nStep 3. Subtract from the total. SS error = 2040.0 - 840.0 = 1200.0.\nStep 4. Check on the df column: 1 + 3 + 3 + 112 = 119, and 119 is the Total df. The column adds, so the subtraction was the right move. (119 = N - 1 with N = 120, which matches 15 customers in each of the 2 x 4 = 8 cells.)\nStep 5. A quick sanity test: the error is usually the largest single slice. Here 1200.0 out of 2040.0 is just under 60 per cent of the total, which is normal.\nMemory hook: the Error row is the leftovers row. Total minus everything explained.","wrongs":["1440.0 comes from 2040.0 - 180.0 - 420.0, which forgets the interaction row. The interaction is a source line like any other and its 240.0 must come out too.","1800.0 comes from subtracting only the interaction: 2040.0 - 240.0. All three effect rows have to be removed.","","2880.0 comes from ADDING the three effect sums of squares to the total: 2040.0 + 840.0. The Total is the biggest number in the column by definition, so any answer larger than the Total must be wrong. Use that as an instant check."],"trap":"Dropping the interaction row when you subtract. In a two-factor table there are always THREE effect rows to remove, not two.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way table with one SS missing. -&gt; Diagnosis: recovering a sum of squares. -&gt; Treatment: SS A + SS B + SS AxB + SS error = SS total, so SS error = SS total minus the three effect SS. Verify on the df column, which adds down the same way.","out":{"cap":"Two-way ANOVA with the error sum of squares missing","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Staffing level","180.0","1"],["Time of day","420.0","3"],["Staffing x Time","240.0","3"],["Error","?","112"],["Total","2040.0","119"]]}},{"n":6,"q":"Astrid Halvorsen compares two revision apps across three year groups. Both main effects come out significant and the app by year group test does not. What may she conclude?","o":["Nothing can be concluded until the interaction also reaches significance.","The two factors must be independent of one another in the population, so they cannot be correlated.","The two factors together explain none of the variation in revision scores.","The effect of each factor looks much the same at every level of the other, so the two main effects can be reported on their own as single numbers."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"interaction_interp","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Significant main effects and a NON-significant interaction. This is the mirror image of the usual interaction question. A non-significant interaction is permission: it means the main effects may be quoted as they stand.","plain":"In plain words: the apps differ, the year groups differ, and the app difference is about the same size in every year group. So one number describes the app effect honestly.","work":"Step 1. What does a significant main effect of app mean? Averaged over the three year groups, one app beats the other.\nStep 2. What does a non-significant interaction mean? There is no evidence that the size of the app effect changes from year group to year group.\nStep 3. Put them together. Because the app effect does not appear to vary, the average app effect describes all three year groups reasonably well. So she may report it on its own, which is exactly what she could NOT do if the interaction had been significant.\nStep 4. Keep the wording honest. \"No evidence of an interaction\" is not proof that the effects are identical. With a small sample she may simply have missed a real interaction, so she should not write \"the app effect is exactly the same in every year\".\nMemory hook: a significant interaction is a warning label on the main effects. No interaction means no warning label.","wrongs":["The main effects are valid tests in their own right. A non-significant interaction does not block them; it clears the way for them.","This confuses an interaction with a correlation between the two factors. An interaction is about the OUTCOME behaving differently in different combinations, not about the factors being related to each other.","Both main effects were significant, so the factors clearly do explain some variation. Only the combination effect is absent.",""],"trap":"Upgrading \"no evidence of an interaction\" into \"the effects are identical\". That is the \"do not reject means accept\" mistake wearing a two-factor costume.","tri":"Symptom: a non-significant interaction next to significant main effects. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting the absence of an interaction. -&gt; Treatment: no evidence of an interaction means the two factors behave additively, so each main effect may be reported as a single overall effect. Phrase it as \"no evidence that the effect differs\", never as \"the effects are exactly equal\"."},{"n":7,"q":"Dmitri Sokolov is revising for his statistics exam and has written four sentences about what a two-factor ANOVA with both factors between subjects produces. Which one of them is FALSE?","o":["Each of the F tests has its own separate error term.","The analysis produces three F ratios: one for each factor and one for the interaction.","All of the F ratios are divided by the same mean square error.","A significant interaction can be present even when neither main effect is significant."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"two_way_concept","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Four sentences about the structure of a two-factor ANOVA and \"which is FALSE\". Look at the table in your head: it has ONE Error row. Any sentence that gives the effects separate error terms is the false one.","plain":"In plain words: three of these are true facts about the shape of the output and one is wrong. The output has one error line, not three.","work":"Step 1. Count the Error rows in a between-subjects two-factor table. There is exactly one.\nStep 2. So every F ratio must use that one MS error as its denominator: F_A = MS_A / MS error, F_B = MS_B / MS error, F_AB = MS_AB / MS error. The sentence that gives each test its own error term is therefore FALSE, and that is the answer.\nStep 3. Check the other three. Three effects give three F ratios: TRUE.\nStep 4. All divided by the same MS error: TRUE, as just established.\nStep 5. An interaction with no main effects: TRUE. If a treatment helps one group by 5 and harms the other by 5, the two averages are equal, so no main effect, but the lines cross steeply, so a strong interaction.\nExplain it to a friend: one Error row means one measuring stick for noise, shared by all three tests.","wrongs":["","True. The three effects are factor A, factor B and the A by B interaction, so three F ratios.","True. A between-subjects two-factor table has a single Error row, and all three F ratios use its mean square.","True, and it is worth remembering. Equal and opposite effects in the two groups cancel in the averages, leaving no main effect while producing a strong interaction."],"trap":"Carrying over the idea of separate error terms from repeated-measures designs, where different effects really can have different error terms. In a plain between-subjects two-factor ANOVA there is only one.","tri":"Symptom: statements about the structure of a two-factor ANOVA, one false. -&gt; Diagnosis: what a two-factor ANOVA produces. -&gt; Treatment: picture the table. Rows for A, B, A x B, Error, Total. Three effect rows, so three F ratios. ONE Error row, so one shared MS error. Each effect is tested independently of the others."},{"n":8,"q":"Grace Mwangi has collected data on sleep quality from participants who were given either a morning or an evening exercise slot, and who used either a sleep app or no app. She plans to analyse the exercise slot in one analysis and the app in a completely separate one. What does she lose by doing it that way?","o":["Nothing, because the two main effects come out the same either way.","She cannot test whether the exercise slot and the app work differently in combination, and each analysis leaves the other factor's variation inside its own error term, which makes both tests less sensitive.","She loses the ability to make a causal claim about either factor.","She loses half of her data, because each of the two analyses can use only half of the participants."],"a":1,"pct":30,"typ":"two_way_adv","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. Two one-way analyses offered instead of one two-factor analysis, and the question is what is LOST. Two losses, every time: the interaction, and the sharpness that comes from pulling the other factor out of the error term.","plain":"In plain words: splitting the analysis throws away the question of whether the two things work together, and it also dumps each factor's variation into the other analysis as noise.","work":"Step 1. What survives the split? Both main effects are still estimated, roughly as before. So the main effects are not the loss.\nStep 2. First loss. In the exercise-only analysis there is no app factor, so there is no row in which to ask whether the app matters more in the morning than in the evening. The interaction question simply cannot be asked.\nStep 3. Second loss. In the exercise-only analysis, the differences caused by app use have nowhere to sit except the error term. That inflates SS error and so MS error. Since F = MS effect / MS error, a bigger denominator gives a smaller F and less power. The same happens in reverse in the app-only analysis.\nStep 4. Third, smaller loss. Two separate tests at .05 give a family-wise error rate above .05, while the factorial analysis handles everything in one model.\nMemory hook: splitting the analysis loses a QUESTION and gains NOISE.","wrongs":["The main effects survive, but the interaction does not, and both tests lose power because the other factor stays in the error term. So something real is lost.","","Causal claims come from random assignment to conditions, not from how many factors sit in one model. Splitting the analysis does not touch the design.","Both analyses use all of the participants. What changes is how their variation is divided between the model and the error, not how many people are counted."],"trap":"Thinking the only cost is the missing interaction. The hidden cost is the inflated error term, which weakens the main effect tests you did keep.","tri":"Symptom: a factorial data set about to be analysed as two separate one-way ANOVAs. -&gt; Diagnosis: the advantage of a factorial design. -&gt; Treatment: name both losses. 1 No interaction test is possible. 2 The unmodelled factor stays in the error term, so MS error is bigger, F is smaller and power falls. A third, smaller point: two tests instead of one raises the family-wise error rate."},{"n":9,"q":"Elena Petrova measures braking reaction times of experienced and novice drivers, once with a phone conversation running and once without. The four cell means in milliseconds are printed below. When she plots them, the two lines do not cross. Is there an interaction?","o":["No, because the two lines do not cross.","No, because both groups of drivers are slower with the phone on.","Yes, but only because the novices are slower than the experienced drivers overall.","Yes, almost certainly, because the lines are not parallel: the phone costs the novices 65 ms and the experienced drivers almost nothing. Crossing is not required."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"read_plot","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. The stem tells you the lines do NOT cross and then asks about the interaction. That is a trap being set. Ignore crossing entirely and test for parallel by subtracting within each row.","plain":"In plain words: the phone barely touches the experienced drivers but costs the novices two thirds of a tenth of a second. The phone does different things to the two groups, which is what an interaction is.","work":"Step 1. Simple effect of the phone for experienced drivers: 318.0 - 320.0 = -2.0 ms. Essentially nothing.\nStep 2. Simple effect of the phone for novices: 395.0 - 330.0 = +65.0 ms. A large cost.\nStep 3. Compare. -2.0 against +65.0. The phone effect is not the same in the two groups, so the lines are not parallel and there is an interaction.\nStep 4. Notice that the lines never cross: the novice line starts higher (330.0 against 320.0) and stays higher (395.0 against 318.0). Non-crossing lines can still fan apart, and fanning is an interaction.\nStep 5. The write-up: lead with the interaction, then report the phone effect separately for each group, because \"the phone costs 31.5 ms on average\" would describe neither group.\nMemory hook: parallel or not parallel is the only question. Crossing is just one dramatic way of not being parallel.","wrongs":["Crossing is not a requirement. These lines fan apart without meeting, and that is already an interaction.","Only the novices are meaningfully slower with the phone on. The experienced drivers change by -2.0 ms, which is the wrong direction and far too small to count. And even if both had slowed, different AMOUNTS of slowing would still be an interaction.","Being slower overall is a MAIN EFFECT of driver group. The interaction is a separate thing: it is the fact that the phone costs 65.0 ms in one group and nothing in the other.",""],"trap":"Using crossing as the test. Examiners deliberately give non-crossing fans to catch this. Subtract within each row and compare the two differences.","tri":"Symptom: cell means, a remark about crossing, and \"is there an interaction\". -&gt; Diagnosis: reading an interaction plot. -&gt; Treatment: compute the simple effect of one factor within each level of the other. If those two numbers differ, the lines are not parallel and there is an interaction. Crossing is irrelevant.","out":{"cap":"Mean braking reaction time in milliseconds","h":["Driver group","Phone off","Phone on"],"r":[["Experienced","320.0","318.0"],["Novice","330.0","395.0"]]}},{"n":10,"q":"Hugo Bernard ran a study with three types of lighting and two desk heights, with 11 different office workers in each combination. His output has been cropped: all he can still read is that the error mean square was 12.50 on 60 degrees of freedom. What was the error sum of squares? (1 decimal)","o":["750.0","4.8","825.0","812.5"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"ms_from_ss","theme":7,"spot":"HINT. An MS and a df are given and an SS is wanted. Same relationship as always, read the other way: if MS = SS / df then SS = MS x df. Watch which df the question hands you.","plain":"In plain words: a mean square is a sum of squares shared out over its degrees of freedom. To get the total back, multiply the share by the number of shares.","work":"Step 1. Write the relationship. MS error = SS error / df error.\nStep 2. Rearrange. SS error = MS error x df error.\nStep 3. Substitute. SS error = 12.50 x 60 = 750.0.\nStep 4. Check that 60 really is the error df, so that you know it is the right multiplier. a = 3 lighting types, b = 2 desk heights, so ab = 6 cells. N = 6 x 11 = 66 workers. Error df = N - ab = 66 - 6 = 60. It matches the printed value, so 60 is the right df to multiply by.\nMemory hook: MS is \"per df\". To undo a \"per\", multiply.","wrongs":["","4.8 comes from dividing the wrong way round: 60 / 12.50. An SS is always bigger than its MS when df is above 1, so an answer smaller than the MS cannot be an SS.","825.0 comes from multiplying by N = 66 instead of by the error df of 60: 12.50 x 66. The error row has 60 df, because 6 df are used up by the six cell means.","812.5 comes from multiplying by the Total df, N - 1 = 65: 12.50 x 65. The Total df belongs to the Total row and never multiplies an error mean square."],"trap":"Multiplying by the wrong df. Every row in an ANOVA table has its own df, and SS = MS x df only works when the two come from the SAME row.","tri":"Symptom: an MS and its df printed, the SS requested. -&gt; Diagnosis: MS and SS in a two-way table, worked backwards. -&gt; Treatment: SS = MS x df, using the df of that SAME row. For an error row, that df is N - ab, which is the 60 printed here, not N and not N - 1."}]},{"id":"t8a","name":"Repeated measures - Set A","short":"Repeated measures - Set A","blurb":"Ten questions on designs where the same people are measured again and again: how the total sum of squares splits into between persons and within persons and then into model and residual, which analysis a described design actually needs, what counterbalancing is for, how to read Mauchly, how to get the number of PERSONS back out of the df, why repeated measures has more power, and how a paired t test fits in.","theme":8,"icon":"🔁","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Veerle Jansen asks 15 office workers to rate how comfortable they find each of four chair designs, so every worker rates all four. Part of her output is printed below, with two sums of squares missing. What is the residual sum of squares? (1 decimal)","o":["420.0","660.0","940.0","280.0"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_partition","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. One group of people, every person measured several times, and a table whose rows are indented under \"Within persons\". That indentation is the signature of a repeated-measures partition. There are TWO splits to make, not one.","plain":"In plain words: the total variation first splits into \"people differ from each other\" and \"each person differs across the conditions\". The second of those then splits again into \"the conditions did something\" and \"leftover\".","work":"Step 1. Draw the two splits. SS total splits into SS between persons and SS within persons. SS within persons then splits into SS model (the conditions) and SS residual.\nStep 2. First split. SS within persons = SS total - SS between persons = 1180.0 - 520.0 = 660.0.\nStep 3. Second split. SS residual = SS within persons - SS model = 660.0 - 240.0 = 420.0.\nStep 4. Check on the df, which split in exactly the same way. Between persons: n - 1 = 15 - 1 = 14. Within persons: n(k - 1) = 15 x 3 = 45. Model: k - 1 = 3. Residual: (n - 1)(k - 1) = 14 x 3 = 42. And 3 + 42 = 45, and 14 + 45 = 59 = nk - 1. Everything balances.\nMemory hook: TWO cuts. Cut one takes the PEOPLE out. Cut two splits what is left into the CONDITIONS and the LEFTOVER. SSR = SSW - SSM.","wrongs":["","660.0 is SS within persons, the answer after the FIRST cut only. It is the number you need on the way, not the number asked for. Make the second cut.","940.0 comes from 1180.0 - 240.0, which subtracts the model straight from the total and forgets to take the people out first. That is the one-way ANOVA partition, not the repeated-measures one, and it is why repeated measures looks so much more powerful: the 520.0 for persons has to come out.","280.0 comes from 520.0 - 240.0, subtracting the model from the BETWEEN-persons sum of squares. The model sits inside the within-persons part, so it must be taken out of 660.0, not out of 520.0."],"trap":"Making one cut instead of two. Write SST = SSB + SSW and then SSW = SSM + SSR on your scrap paper before touching the numbers.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated-measures table with between-persons and within-persons rows. -&gt; Diagnosis: partitioning the sums of squares. -&gt; Treatment: two steps. First SS within = SS total minus SS between persons. Then SS residual = SS within minus SS model. Never go straight from the total to the residual.","out":{"cap":"Chair comfort rated by 15 workers in 4 designs","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Between persons","520.0","14"],["Within persons","?","45"],["   Conditions (model)","240.0","3"],["   Residual","?","42"],["Total","1180.0","59"]]}},{"n":2,"q":"Sophie Lambert asks 30 volunteers to type the same passage on three different keyboard layouts and records a typing speed for each layout, so there are 90 speeds in her file. She wants to know whether the three layouts differ. Which analysis does her design call for?","o":["A one-way independent ANOVA, because there are three conditions to compare.","Three paired t tests, one for each pair of layouts, with no correction.","A repeated-measures ANOVA, because the same 30 volunteers provide a score in all three conditions.","An independent-samples t test, because there are more scores than volunteers."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"which_test","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. \"Which analysis\" questions are decided by one count. Count the SCORES and count the PEOPLE. If there are more scores than people, the same people appear more than once, so the design is within subjects. If the two counts match, it is between subjects.","plain":"In plain words: 90 scores but only 30 people means each person gave three scores. The three columns are not independent of each other, so the analysis has to account for the person.","work":"Step 1. Count the people: 30 volunteers.\nStep 2. Count the scores: 90.\nStep 3. Divide. 90 / 30 = 3 scores per person, so every volunteer appears in every condition. The design is within subjects.\nStep 4. Count the conditions: 3, which is more than 2. So it is a repeated-measures ANOVA, not a paired t test.\nStep 5. Why the person matters. Some people are simply fast typists in every layout. A repeated-measures analysis pulls that person-to-person variation out of the error term, which an independent ANOVA cannot do, and it respects the fact that the three scores from one person are related.\nMemory hook: scores divided by people equals scores per person. If that is more than 1, think \"repeated\".","wrongs":["An independent ANOVA assumes every score comes from a different person. Here each person contributes three scores, so the independence assumption is broken and the person-to-person variation would be left in the error term.","Three tests at .05 push the family-wise error rate up to 1 - 0.95 cubed = 0.143, so about 14 per cent rather than 5. Run the overall repeated-measures F first, and only then follow up with corrected comparisons.","","A t test compares two things. There are three layouts here, so a t test cannot answer the question, and \"more scores than volunteers\" is the clue for repeated measures rather than for an independent test."],"trap":"Reading \"three conditions\" and jumping to a one-way independent ANOVA. The number of conditions tells you how many groups; whether the same people appear in all of them tells you which ANOVA.","tri":"Symptom: a described design and a choice of analyses. -&gt; Diagnosis: which test. -&gt; Treatment: count scores against people. More scores than people AND more than two conditions means a repeated-measures ANOVA. Equal counts with more than two groups means a one-way independent ANOVA. Two conditions within the same people means a paired t test."},{"n":3,"q":"Ravi Chandrasekhar runs a taste test in which every participant tries three fruit drinks and rates each one. In his first run everyone tried them in the order apple, pear, cherry. A reviewer tells him to vary the order across participants in his next study. What is the purpose of that change?","o":["To make the three groups of participants equal in size.","So that practice, fatigue and carry-over effects are spread evenly over the three drinks instead of always piling up on the same one, which would make an order effect look like a drink effect.","To remove the need to check the sphericity assumption.","To increase the number of ratings, so that power rises."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"counterbalancing","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Everyone receives the conditions in the same fixed order, and the question is about varying it. That is counterbalancing. Its purpose is always about untangling POSITION in the sequence from the CONDITION itself.","plain":"In plain words: if cherry is always last, then \"last\" and \"cherry\" are the same thing in the data. You could never tell a tired palate from a disliked drink.","work":"Step 1. Spot what moves together. In the first run, apple was always first, pear always second, cherry always third. Position and drink are locked to each other.\nStep 2. Name the order effects that come with position. Practice: raters get better at using the scale. Fatigue and palate fatigue: later drinks are judged by a tired mouth. Carry-over: the taste of pear lingers into cherry.\nStep 3. See the damage. If cherry scores lowest, is cherry worse, or is third place worse? With a fixed order the data cannot tell you.\nStep 4. The fix. Vary the order across participants, for example by using all six possible orders of three drinks equally often. Then each drink appears first, second and third equally often, so any order effect is spread evenly over all three drinks and no longer favours or penalises any one of them.\nExplain it to a friend: counterbalancing stops \"when you tasted it\" from pretending to be \"what you tasted\".","wrongs":["Counterbalancing is about the ORDER within each person, not about splitting people into groups. In a within-subjects design everyone is in every condition.","","Sphericity is about whether the differences between pairs of conditions vary in spread. Counterbalancing does not address it, and Mauchly still has to be read.","Every participant already rates all three drinks, so varying the order does not add a single rating. It improves the interpretation, not the sample size."],"trap":"Describing counterbalancing as a way to remove order effects altogether. It does not remove them, it DISTRIBUTES them equally so that they no longer line up with the conditions.","tri":"Symptom: a within-subjects design with a fixed order, and a question about varying the order. -&gt; Diagnosis: counterbalancing. -&gt; Treatment: say that in a fixed order the position in the sequence is confounded with the condition, so order effects (practice, fatigue, carry-over) cannot be separated from the treatment effect. Varying the order breaks that link and spreads the order effects evenly across conditions."},{"n":4,"q":"Amara Nwosu measures mood in the same 22 participants under four lighting conditions. Before reading her F ratio she looks at the assumption test her software prints, which gives p = .62. What should she do?","o":["Treat the assumption as violated and read the Greenhouse-Geisser corrected row.","Conclude that the assumption has been proved true, so her data are perfectly spherical.","Abandon the repeated-measures analysis, because p = .62 is far above .05.","Treat the assumption as intact and read the F from the sphericity-assumed row, with no correction."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"sphericity","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. A repeated-measures design with three or more conditions and a p-value for an assumption test printed before the F. That is Mauchly. The trick is that its null hypothesis is the thing you WANT, so a large p is good news.","plain":"In plain words: this test asks \"is the assumption in order?\". A big p-value means no evidence against it, so you carry on with the ordinary, uncorrected F.","work":"Step 1. Write down the null hypothesis of Mauchly's test: the variances of the differences between every pair of conditions are equal, that is, sphericity holds.\nStep 2. Compare p with alpha. p = .62, which is much larger than .05.\nStep 3. Decision. Do not reject H0. There is no evidence against sphericity.\nStep 4. Action. Read the F and p from the sphericity-assumed row. No epsilon correction is needed, so the df stay at k - 1 = 3 and (n - 1)(k - 1) = 21 x 3 = 63.\nStep 5. Keep the wording careful. Not rejecting does not prove sphericity, it just means her data give no reason to doubt it.\nMemory hook: Mauchly is back to front. Big p is GOOD. Small p means SHRINK the df with a correction.","wrongs":["This has the direction reversed. You correct when Mauchly is significant, that is when p is SMALL. Here p = .62, so nothing needs correcting.","You never prove a null hypothesis. Failing to reject sphericity means there is no evidence against it, which is weaker than proof. This is the \"accept H0\" mistake in sphericity clothing.","A large p is reassuring, not disqualifying. There is no reason to abandon the analysis; she should run it in its standard, uncorrected form.",""],"trap":"Reading Mauchly like an ordinary significance test, where small p is the \"positive\" result. Here small p is the BAD news, because the null is the assumption you were hoping to keep.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated-measures design and a printed p-value for sphericity. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading Mauchly. -&gt; Treatment: H0 says sphericity HOLDS. A large p (above .05) means do not reject, so the assumption stands and you read the sphericity-assumed row. A small p (below .05) means reject, so the assumption fails and you read a corrected row such as Greenhouse-Geisser."},{"n":5,"q":"Henrik Olsen finds an old printout from a colleague. The analysis compared several conditions measured in the same people, and the only numbers still legible are 3 for the model row and 51 for the error row. How many people took part, and how many conditions were there? (whole numbers)","o":["52 people in 4 conditions","18 people in 3 conditions","18 people in 4 conditions","17 people in 4 conditions"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"backout_n_rm","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Repeated-measures df given, design wanted. Two small equations, solved in a fixed order. Do the MODEL row first, because it gives you k, and you need k before the error row can give you n.","plain":"In plain words: the model df tells you how many conditions there were, and then the error df tells you how many people filled them.","work":"Step 1. Model row. df model = k - 1 = 3, so k = 4. There were 4 conditions.\nStep 2. Error row. df error = (n - 1)(k - 1) = 51, and k - 1 = 3, so (n - 1) x 3 = 51.\nStep 3. Divide. n - 1 = 51 / 3 = 17.\nStep 4. Add one. n = 18. There were 18 people.\nStep 5. Check. With n = 18 and k = 4 the total number of scores is 72, so the total df is 71. Between persons gives 17, within persons gives 18 x 3 = 54, and 17 + 54 = 71. Inside the within-persons part, 3 + 51 = 54. Everything balances, so 18 and 4 are right.\nMemory hook: the model row hands you k. Only then can the error row hand you n. And finish with \"plus one\", because the df is n MINUS one.","wrongs":["This reads the error df as n - 1 and gives n = 52. The error df is a PRODUCT, (n - 1)(k - 1), so the 51 has to be divided by k - 1 = 3 first.","This reads the model df as k rather than k - 1. df model = k - 1, so 3 means 4 conditions, not 3. With only 3 conditions the error df would be 17 x 2 = 34, not 51.","","17 is n - 1, not n. The last step of this question type is always to add one. Getting 17 means the division was right and the final step was forgotten."],"trap":"Dividing 51 by the wrong number, or forgetting the final \"plus one\". Both slips come from not writing the formula down first. Write df error = (n - 1)(k - 1) before you do any arithmetic.","tri":"Symptom: repeated-measures df printed, n and k wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: recovering n from the df. -&gt; Treatment: df model = k - 1, so k = df model + 1. Then df error = (n - 1)(k - 1), so n - 1 = df error / (k - 1) and n = that plus 1. Remember n is PERSONS, not scores."},{"n":6,"q":"Marieke de Vries is told that measuring the same people under all of her conditions will give her a better chance of detecting an effect than using separate groups of people. Why is that so?","o":["Because the variation between persons is taken out of the error term, so the residual sum of squares is smaller and the same difference between conditions gives a larger F.","Because every participant produces several scores, so the effective sample size is the number of people multiplied by the number of conditions.","Because a repeated-measures analysis uses a one-tailed test, which is easier to pass.","Because carry-over effects from one condition to the next add to the size of the treatment effect."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_power","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. A question about why within-subjects designs are more sensitive. The answer is always about the ERROR TERM, never about the sample size and never about the test being easier. Look for the option that mentions person variation leaving the error.","plain":"In plain words: people differ a lot from each other, and in a between-groups design all of that difference counts as noise. When the same person appears in every condition, that noise can be identified and set aside, so what is left to compare against is much smaller.","work":"Step 1. In a between-groups one-way ANOVA, everything that is not explained by the group, including the fact that some people are simply higher scorers, lands in the error term.\nStep 2. In a repeated-measures design each person appears in every condition, so their personal level can be estimated and pulled out as SS between persons.\nStep 3. What is left, SS within persons, splits into the conditions (SS model) and the residual. So SS residual no longer contains the person differences.\nStep 4. Consequence for the test. MS residual is smaller, so F = MS model / MS residual is larger for the very same difference between condition means, and p is smaller. More power for the same number of people.\nStep 5. Note the price. The residual df is (n - 1)(k - 1), which is smaller than the error df of a comparable between-groups design, so the critical F is a little higher. The gain from the shrinking error term normally outweighs that.\nMemory hook: same people means the person is a known quantity, and known quantities leave the noise pile.","wrongs":["","The number of PERSONS, not the number of scores, drives the error df: (n - 1)(k - 1). Taking more measurements from the same person does not create new independent people, and the gain comes from the smaller error term, not from a larger n.","Repeated-measures ANOVA uses an F test, which is one-tailed in its own right but is not \"easier\". Nothing about the tail of the test changes between the two designs.","Carry-over effects are a PROBLEM in within-subjects designs, not a benefit. They distort the comparison, which is exactly why orders are counterbalanced."],"trap":"Thinking the advantage is a bigger sample. It is not: n is still the number of people. The advantage is a smaller error term.","tri":"Symptom: \"why does repeated measures have more power\". -&gt; Diagnosis: power in a within-subjects design. -&gt; Treatment: SS total splits into between persons and within persons, and only the within-persons part is used for the test. The between-persons variation is REMOVED from the error, so SS residual and therefore MS residual are smaller. Since F = MS model / MS residual, a smaller denominator gives a bigger F."},{"n":7,"q":"Camille Rousseau wants to compare mood under three lighting levels. She knows that mood is easily affected by what happened earlier in a session. If she measures the same people under all three lighting levels, what is the main drawback she takes on?","o":["She will need more participants than she would with separate groups.","Order and carry-over effects: each person's later ratings can be influenced by the conditions they have already been through, which is why the order has to be counterbalanced.","The design cannot produce an F ratio, so she would be limited to descriptive statistics.","The differences between persons stay inside the error term, which lowers her power."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"within_between","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. A comparison of within-subject and between-subject designs, asking for the DRAWBACK of the within-subject one. Its strength is the smaller error term; its weakness is always order and carry-over, plus the sphericity assumption.","plain":"In plain words: using the same people saves participants and cuts noise, but the people carry their earlier conditions with them. What happened in the first condition can colour the second.","work":"Step 1. Name what she gains, so you can rule those options out. Each person is their own control, so stable differences between people leave the error term and power rises. She also needs fewer people.\nStep 2. Name what she pays. Each person meets the conditions in some sequence, so practice, boredom and fatigue build up, and the mood created by one lighting level can linger into the next. Those effects are tied to POSITION in the sequence, not to the lighting, but they land in the data as if they were lighting effects.\nStep 3. Note that the stem has flagged exactly this: mood is sensitive to what came earlier, so carry-over is the specific danger here.\nStep 4. Name the remedy. Counterbalance the order across participants so that each lighting level appears in each position equally often, and leave a gap between conditions so the previous mood can wash out.\nMemory hook: within-subject designs trade NOISE for ORDER. You lose the person differences from the error and you gain a sequence problem.","wrongs":["This is the wrong way round. A within-subject design normally needs FEWER participants, because the error term is smaller so the same power is reached with less people.","","A repeated-measures ANOVA produces an F ratio in the ordinary way: MS model / MS residual, on df k - 1 and (n - 1)(k - 1).","This is the drawback of the BETWEEN-subject design. In a within-subject design the person differences are pulled out of the error term, which is the main reason to use it."],"trap":"Picking the option that describes the other design's weakness. Before you answer, say to yourself which design each statement is really about.","tri":"Symptom: a choice between a within-subject and a between-subject design, drawback requested. -&gt; Diagnosis: comparing the two designs. -&gt; Treatment: list the trade. Within-subject gains: person variation leaves the error, fewer participants needed. Within-subject costs: order effects (practice, fatigue), carry-over effects, the sphericity assumption, and dropout across sessions. Pick the cost, and name counterbalancing as the remedy for order."},{"n":8,"q":"Bilal Haddadi measures concentration in the same 20 students under four noise levels and is about to write his hypotheses. Let mu1, mu2, mu3 and mu4 be the population mean concentration scores under the four noise levels. Which pair of hypotheses is correct?","o":["H0: all four means differ from one another. H1: all four means are equal.","H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4. H1: all four means differ from one another.","H0: the correlation between the four conditions is zero. H1: the correlation is not zero.","H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4. H1: not all four means are equal."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_hyp","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Hypotheses for an ANOVA, repeated measures or not. Two fixed features: H0 always says all the means are EQUAL, and H1 always says NOT ALL are equal. H1 never says they all differ.","plain":"In plain words: the null is \"noise level makes no difference anywhere\". The alternative is simply \"that is not true\", which is satisfied if even one pair differs.","work":"Step 1. Write the null. The conditions do nothing, so every population mean is the same: H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4.\nStep 2. Write the alternative as the exact opposite of the null. The opposite of \"all equal\" is \"not all equal\", so H1: not all four means are equal.\nStep 3. Check why \"all four differ\" is wrong. If mu1 = mu2 but mu3 is higher, the null is false, so H1 must be true. But \"all four differ\" would be false in that situation. Two statements that can both be false cannot be a null and an alternative, because together they have to cover every possibility.\nStep 4. Note that the repeated-measures setting changes nothing about the hypotheses. The same people being measured four times changes the ERROR TERM and the df, not what is being hypothesised about the means.\nMemory hook: H0 is \"all equal\", H1 is \"not all equal\". Never \"all different\".","wrongs":["The null and the alternative are swapped, and \"all differ\" is not a valid H1 anyway. H0 is always the statement of no difference, because it is the one you can test against.","The null is right but the alternative is the classic \"all means differ\" trap. A single mean standing apart from three identical ones is already enough to make H0 false.","An ANOVA is about means, not correlations. The conditions in a repeated-measures design are correlated by construction, since they come from the same people, and that is a feature of the design rather than the hypothesis under test.",""],"trap":"Writing H1 as \"all of the means differ\". The alternative to \"all equal\" is \"at least one differs\", which is much weaker.","tri":"Symptom: hypotheses offered for a repeated-measures ANOVA. -&gt; Diagnosis: hypotheses for an ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: H0 sets every population mean equal. H1 is the logical negation: at least one mean differs from the others, written as \"not all means are equal\". Reject any option where H1 claims that ALL of them differ, and any option about correlations."},{"n":9,"q":"Isabel Navarro measured anxiety in the same 25 students before and after a relaxation session and reported t = 2.80. A colleague says the same comparison could have been run as a repeated-measures analysis of variance. What would that analysis have given? (2 decimals)","o":["F = 7.84 on 1 and 24 degrees of freedom, with exactly the same p-value.","F = 2.80 on 1 and 24 degrees of freedom, because F and t are the same statistic.","F = 7.84 on 1 and 23 degrees of freedom, because two means were estimated.","Nothing comparable, because an analysis of variance needs at least three conditions."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_concept","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Two conditions in the same people, a t statistic quoted, and an ANOVA mentioned. This is the \"they are the same test\" question. With exactly TWO conditions, F = t squared.","plain":"In plain words: a paired t test is just the two-condition version of a repeated-measures ANOVA. Square the t and you get the F, and the p-value does not move at all.","work":"Step 1. Count the conditions. Before and after, so k = 2.\nStep 2. Square the t. F = t squared = 2.80 x 2.80 = 7.84.\nStep 3. Work out the df. df model = k - 1 = 1. df error = (n - 1)(k - 1) = (25 - 1) x 1 = 24.\nStep 4. The p-value. The two-tailed p for t = 2.80 on 24 df is .0099, and the p for F = 7.84 on 1 and 24 df is also .0099. Identical, because squaring the statistic and using the F distribution with 1 numerator df is the same calculation.\nStep 5. Remember the limit. F = t squared holds only when there are exactly two conditions (or, in regression, exactly one predictor). With three conditions there is no single t to square.\nMemory hook: two conditions, one squaring. F on 1 and (n - 1) df IS the paired t test.","wrongs":["","F equals t SQUARED, not t. An F is a ratio of mean squares and so cannot be negative, while a t can be; squaring is what reconciles the two.","The F is right but the df is wrong. In a repeated-measures design the error df is (n - 1)(k - 1) = 24, which comes from the PERSONS, not from n - 2. The n - 2 belongs to an INDEPENDENT-samples t test on two separate groups.","An ANOVA runs perfectly well on two conditions; it simply reduces to the t test. The three-condition rule is about when a t test stops being ENOUGH, not about when an ANOVA becomes possible."],"trap":"Using n - 2 for the error df out of habit from the independent-samples t test. With the same people measured twice, the error df is n - 1 = 24.","tri":"Symptom: a paired t test with two conditions, and a question about the ANOVA equivalent. -&gt; Diagnosis: a paired t test as a special case of repeated measures. -&gt; Treatment: F = t squared when there are exactly two conditions. The df are k - 1 = 1 and (n - 1)(k - 1) = n - 1. The p-value is identical, because it is the same test written two ways."},{"n":10,"q":"Thabo Molefe measures hand grip strength in the same 12 athletes at three points in a training season. His output is printed below with two sums of squares missing. What is the sum of squares for the conditions (the model)? (1 decimal)","o":["66.0","504.0","174.0","570.0"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_ss","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. A repeated-measures table with gaps in the SS column. The same two-step partition as always, just with a different row missing. Find the within-persons total first, then take the residual out of it.","plain":"In plain words: take the people out of the total to get the within-persons part, then take the leftover out of that to see what the training points explained.","work":"Step 1. First cut. SS within persons = SS total - SS between persons = 900.0 - 396.0 = 504.0.\nStep 2. Second cut. The two indented rows must add to 504.0, so SS model = SS within persons - SS residual = 504.0 - 330.0 = 174.0.\nStep 3. Check on the df. Between persons: n - 1 = 11. Within persons: n(k - 1) = 12 x 2 = 24. Model: k - 1 = 2. Residual: (n - 1)(k - 1) = 11 x 2 = 22. And 2 + 22 = 24, and 11 + 24 = 35 = nk - 1. The table balances.\nStep 4. Optional extra, to see what the number is for. MS model = 174.0 / 2 = 87.00 and MS residual = 330.0 / 22 = 15.00, so F = 87.00 / 15.00 = 5.80 on 2 and 22 df, which is significant at the 5 per cent level.\nMemory hook: the indented rows always add up to the row they are indented under.","wrongs":["66.0 comes from 396.0 - 330.0, taking the residual out of the BETWEEN-persons row. The model and the residual both live inside the WITHIN-persons part, so the 330.0 must come out of 504.0.","504.0 is SS within persons, the result of the first cut only. It is the number you need on the way to the answer, not the answer.","","570.0 comes from 900.0 - 330.0, which skips the between-persons step altogether. The 396.0 belonging to the athletes themselves has to be removed from the total first."],"trap":"Going straight from the Total row to the answer. In a repeated-measures table there is always a between-persons row in between, and it must come out first.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated-measures table with an SS missing. -&gt; Diagnosis: recovering a repeated-measures sum of squares. -&gt; Treatment: SS within = SS total minus SS between persons. Then SS model = SS within minus SS residual. The indented rows always add up to the Within persons row above them.","out":{"cap":"Grip strength in 12 athletes at 3 points in the season","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Between persons","396.0","11"],["Within persons","?","24"],["   Conditions (model)","?","2"],["   Residual","330.0","22"],["Total","900.0","35"]]}}]},{"id":"t8b","name":"Repeated measures - Set B","short":"Repeated measures - Set B","blurb":"Ten more repeated-measures questions in new shapes: a sum of squares recovered from a printed table, a design that only looks like repeated measures, the partition written as an equation, the number of persons worked back out of the df, the df computed forwards, Mauchly significant this time, what an epsilon correction actually changes, a fixed order left uncounterbalanced, and the within against between trade-off.","theme":8,"icon":"🔁","group":"theme","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Louise Fontaine records how many words 20 participants recall after each of three study methods, with every participant using all three. Her printout is missing two sums of squares. What is the between-persons sum of squares? (1 decimal)","o":["940.0","1000.0","1580.0","820.0"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_ss","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. A repeated-measures table with the BETWEEN-persons row missing. Work the partition in reverse: add the two indented rows to rebuild the within-persons total, then subtract that from the grand total.","plain":"In plain words: the model and the residual together make up everything that happens inside people. Whatever is left of the total after that is the differences between people.","work":"Step 1. Rebuild the within-persons row by ADDING the two indented rows: SS within = 180.0 + 760.0 = 940.0.\nStep 2. Subtract from the total. SS between persons = 1760.0 - 940.0 = 820.0.\nStep 3. Check on the df. n = 20 people and k = 3 conditions. Between persons: n - 1 = 19. Within persons: n(k - 1) = 20 x 2 = 40, and indeed 2 + 38 = 40. Total: nk - 1 = 59, and 19 + 40 = 59. The table balances.\nStep 4. Read what the answer means. 820.0 out of 1760.0, about 47 per cent of all the variation, is simply that some people recall more words than others whatever the method. In a between-groups design all 820.0 of that would have sat in the error term. Here it is lifted out, which is exactly where the extra power of a repeated-measures design comes from.\nMemory hook: indented rows ADD UP to the row above them. Use that to rebuild any missing total.","wrongs":["940.0 is SS within persons, the number you build on the way. It is the sum of the two indented rows, not the between-persons row.","1000.0 comes from 1760.0 - 760.0, subtracting only the residual. The model SS of 180.0 also lives inside the within-persons part and must come out too.","1580.0 comes from 1760.0 - 180.0, subtracting only the model. The residual of 760.0 is also part of the within-persons variation.",""],"trap":"Subtracting just one of the two indented rows. They both belong to the within-persons part, so both have to be removed from the total.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated-measures table missing the between-persons SS. -&gt; Diagnosis: recovering a repeated-measures sum of squares. -&gt; Treatment: SS within = SS model plus SS residual (the indented rows add up). Then SS between persons = SS total minus SS within.","out":{"cap":"Words recalled by 20 participants after 3 study methods","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Between persons","?","19"],["Within persons","?","40"],["   Conditions (model)","180.0","2"],["   Residual","760.0","38"],["Total","1760.0","59"]]}},{"n":2,"q":"Dilan Aydin gives 75 students one of three revision methods, allocating each student to a single method at random, and then records one exam mark per student. He wants to know whether the three methods differ. Which analysis does his design call for?","o":["A repeated-measures ANOVA, because there are three conditions to compare.","A one-way independent ANOVA, because each of the 75 students appears in only one of the three conditions.","A paired t test for each pair of methods.","A repeated-measures ANOVA, because all 75 students sat the same exam."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"which_test","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Three conditions can mean either ANOVA, so three conditions is not the clue. The clue is whether the SAME people appear in all of them. Count the scores and count the people: 75 marks from 75 students is one each, so the groups are separate.","plain":"In plain words: each student did only one method, so the three sets of marks come from three different sets of people. Nobody can be their own control here.","work":"Step 1. Count the people: 75 students.\nStep 2. Count the scores: 75 exam marks.\nStep 3. Divide. 75 / 75 = 1 score per person, so nobody appears twice. The design is between subjects.\nStep 4. Count the groups: 3, which is more than 2, so a t test is not enough. The right analysis is a one-way independent ANOVA with df k - 1 = 2 and N - k = 75 - 3 = 72.\nStep 5. Notice what it costs him. Because the students are different people, all of the natural variation between students stays in the error term. He cannot pull it out, which is precisely the advantage he would have had by letting every student try all three methods.\nMemory hook: one score per person means independent groups. Several scores per person means repeated measures.","wrongs":["The number of conditions does not decide which ANOVA. Repeated measures needs the same people in every condition, and here each student is in exactly one.","","A paired t test needs pairs, which means the same person measured twice. These are three separate groups of students, so there is nothing to pair. Running three tests would also push the family-wise error rate up to 1 - 0.95 cubed = 0.143.","Sitting the same exam is not the same as being measured repeatedly. What makes a design repeated measures is one person supplying a score in several conditions, not several people sharing a measuring instrument."],"trap":"Treating \"everybody did the same test\" as repeated measures. The repetition has to be within a PERSON, across conditions.","tri":"Symptom: a described design and a choice of analyses. -&gt; Diagnosis: which test. -&gt; Treatment: count scores against people. One score per person with three or more groups means a one-way INDEPENDENT ANOVA. More scores than people means repeated measures. Two conditions in the same people means a paired t test."},{"n":3,"q":"Ayesha Siddiqui is writing out how the variation is divided up in a design where the same people are measured in every condition. Which statement describes the division correctly?","o":["The residual sum of squares is the total sum of squares minus the model sum of squares.","The within-persons sum of squares is the total sum of squares plus the between-persons sum of squares.","The total sum of squares splits into between persons and within persons, and the within-persons part splits again into the model and the residual, so the residual is the within-persons sum of squares minus the model sum of squares.","The model sum of squares is the between-persons sum of squares minus the residual sum of squares."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_partition","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Four equations about sums of squares and no numbers at all. Picture the table: Between persons, then Within persons with the model and the residual indented underneath it. Any equation that mixes an indented row with the between-persons row is wrong.","plain":"In plain words: cut the cake twice. First into \"people differ\" and \"things happen inside people\". Then cut the second piece into \"the conditions did it\" and \"leftover\".","work":"Step 1. First cut. Every score differs from the grand mean partly because of who the person is and partly because of what happened to them across conditions. So SS total = SS between persons + SS within persons.\nStep 2. Second cut. Inside a person, the scores differ partly because the conditions really differ and partly for no systematic reason. So SS within persons = SS model + SS residual.\nStep 3. Rearrange for the residual. SS residual = SS within persons - SS model. That is the statement to look for.\nStep 4. See why this matters for the F test. The F for the conditions is MS model / MS residual, and the residual no longer contains the between-persons variation. That is the whole reason repeated measures is more sensitive than a between-groups design on the same number of scores.\nMemory hook: SST = SSB + SSW, then SSW = SSM + SSR. Two equations, four letters, in that order.","wrongs":["This is the one-way between-groups partition, where there is no person row at all. In a repeated-measures design the between-persons sum of squares has to be taken out of the total first, so this overstates the residual.","The within-persons part is a SLICE of the total, so it must be smaller than the total. Subtracting, not adding, is what is needed: SSW = SST minus SSB.","","The model and the residual both sit inside the within-persons part. The between-persons sum of squares is a separate slice and never appears in an equation with them."],"trap":"Using SSR = SST - SSM, which is correct for a one-way between-groups ANOVA and wrong here. The between-persons slice has to come out of the total before the model and residual are separated.","tri":"Symptom: competing equations for the sums of squares in a repeated-measures design. -&gt; Diagnosis: the partition. -&gt; Treatment: SS total = SS between persons + SS within persons. Then SS within persons = SS model + SS residual. So SSR = SSW minus SSM. Two cuts, in that order, and the residual comes out of the WITHIN part."},{"n":4,"q":"Pedro Castillo is reading a published paper on a study in which the same people were measured under several conditions. The authors report 4 and 84 as the degrees of freedom of their F test but never say how many people took part. How many people were there, how many times was each measured, and how many scores were there in total? (whole numbers)","o":["22 people measured 5 times, giving 110 scores","85 people measured 5 times, giving 425 scores","21 people measured 5 times, giving 105 scores","22 people measured 4 times, giving 88 scores"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"backout_n_rm","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. An F reported as F(4, 84) in a within-subjects study, with the sample size missing. Work backwards through the two df formulas, numerator row first.","plain":"In plain words: the first df tells you how many conditions there were, and the second then tells you how many people were cycled through them.","work":"Step 1. Numerator row. df model = k - 1 = 4, so k = 5. Each person was measured 5 times.\nStep 2. Denominator row. df error = (n - 1)(k - 1) = 84 with k - 1 = 4, so (n - 1) x 4 = 84.\nStep 3. Divide. n - 1 = 84 / 4 = 21.\nStep 4. Add one. n = 22 people.\nStep 5. Total scores. n x k = 22 x 5 = 110.\nStep 6. Check the whole table. Total df = nk - 1 = 109. Between persons = n - 1 = 21. Within persons = n(k - 1) = 22 x 4 = 88, and 4 + 84 = 88. And 21 + 88 = 109. Everything balances.\nMemory hook: numerator gives k, denominator gives n, and never forget the final \"plus one\".","wrongs":["","This reads the denominator df as n - 1 and gives n = 85. The denominator is a PRODUCT, (n - 1)(k - 1), so the 84 must be divided by k - 1 = 4 first. Notice how badly it fails the check: with 85 people and 5 conditions the error df would be 84 x 4 = 336.","21 is n - 1, not n. The division was right and the last step was skipped. The df is n MINUS one, so you always add one back.","This reads the numerator df as k rather than k - 1. df model = k - 1, so a 4 means 5 conditions. With only 4 conditions the error df would be 21 x 3 = 63, not 84."],"trap":"Dividing by k instead of by k - 1, or stopping at n - 1. Write the two formulas down before you touch the numbers, and finish by checking that the df add up.","tri":"Symptom: repeated-measures df given, sample size wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: recovering n from the df. -&gt; Treatment: numerator df = k - 1, so k = numerator + 1. Denominator df = (n - 1)(k - 1), so n - 1 = denominator / (k - 1), then add 1. Total scores = n times k. Remember n counts PEOPLE, not scores."},{"n":5,"q":"Keiko Tanaka measures hand steadiness in the same 19 participants under five different levels of background vibration. What degrees of freedom should she report for her F test? (whole numbers)","o":["4 and 90","5 and 72","4 and 94","4 and 72"],"a":3,"pct":30,"typ":"rm_df","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. One group of people, each measured under every condition, and df wanted. Repeated measures, so the error df is built from the number of PERSONS, not from the number of scores. Two numbers to extract from the stem: n (people) and k (conditions).","plain":"In plain words: the first df counts how many conditions there are, less one. The second multiplies \"one less than the number of people\" by \"one less than the number of conditions\".","work":"Step 1. Read off n and k. n = 19 participants, k = 5 vibration levels.\nStep 2. df model = k - 1 = 5 - 1 = 4.\nStep 3. df error = (n - 1)(k - 1) = (19 - 1) x (5 - 1) = 18 x 4 = 72.\nStep 4. So she reports F(4, 72).\nStep 5. Check against the rest of the table. Total scores = 19 x 5 = 95, so total df = 94. Between persons = n - 1 = 18. Within persons = n(k - 1) = 19 x 4 = 76, and 4 + 72 = 76. Finally 18 + 76 = 94. The table balances, so 4 and 72 is right.\nMemory hook: both df are built from \"one less\". One less condition for the model, and then one less person TIMES one less condition for the error.","wrongs":["90 is N - k = 95 - 5, the error df of a one-way INDEPENDENT ANOVA on 95 separate people. These are not 95 people, they are 19 people measured 5 times each, and the person-to-person variation has already been removed into its own row.","5 is k, not k - 1. The model df is always one less than the number of conditions. The 72 is right, which shows the error formula was applied correctly.","94 is N - 1 = 95 - 1, the TOTAL df for all the scores. It is the top of the table, not the error row.",""],"trap":"Letting the error df be built from the number of SCORES. In repeated measures the n in the formula is the number of people, which is why the error df (72) is noticeably smaller than it would be in a between-groups design (90).","tri":"Symptom: a within-subjects design, df requested. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a repeated-measures ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: df model = k - 1. df error = (n - 1)(k - 1), with n = PERSONS. Do not use N - k and do not use N - 1, which belong to between-groups designs."},{"n":6,"q":"Nour Khalil measures reaction time in the same 24 participants under four screen brightness settings. The assumption test printed above her F table gives p = .008. What should she do?","o":["Read the sphericity-assumed row, because a small p means the assumption holds.","Read the Greenhouse-Geisser corrected row instead of the sphericity-assumed row, because the assumption has been rejected.","Switch to a one-way independent ANOVA, which has no sphericity assumption.","Read the sphericity-assumed row, because the assumption test only applies when there are exactly two conditions."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"sphericity","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. More than two conditions in the same people and a printed p for an assumption test. That is Mauchly. Its null is the assumption itself, so a SMALL p is the bad news that forces a correction.","plain":"In plain words: this test asks \"is the assumption in order?\" and the answer came back \"no\". So the ordinary row cannot be trusted and she has to read the corrected one underneath it.","work":"Step 1. State the null of Mauchly's test: the variances of the differences between all pairs of conditions are equal, that is, sphericity holds.\nStep 2. Compare p with alpha. p = .008, which is below .05.\nStep 3. Decision. Reject H0. There is evidence that sphericity does NOT hold.\nStep 4. Why it matters. When sphericity fails, the uncorrected F test is too liberal: its real Type I error rate is above .05, so it rejects too often. Using it would overstate the evidence.\nStep 5. Action. Read the Greenhouse-Geisser row, where both degrees of freedom have been multiplied by an estimate of epsilon. The F value itself is unchanged; the smaller df make the p-value larger and so the test more conservative.\nMemory hook: small p, shrink the df. Big p, business as usual.","wrongs":["The direction is reversed. A small p means the assumption has been REJECTED. Mauchly is one of the few tests where you are hoping for a large p.","","An independent ANOVA would be the wrong analysis altogether, because the same people appear in all four conditions. You do not change the design to escape an assumption; you apply the correction that exists for it.","Sphericity only becomes an issue when there are THREE or more conditions, because with two there is just one set of differences and so nothing to compare. Here there are four conditions, so the test applies."],"trap":"Reading Mauchly as though a small p were a good result. For every assumption test of this kind the null is the assumption, so a significant result is the problem, not the finding.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated-measures design with three or more conditions and a p-value for sphericity. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading Mauchly. -&gt; Treatment: H0 is \"sphericity holds\". p below .05 means reject, so the assumption fails and a corrected row (Greenhouse-Geisser, or Huynh-Feldt when epsilon is large) must be read. p above .05 means the assumption stands and the uncorrected row is fine."},{"n":7,"q":"Sven Lindqvist has measured the same 16 participants under four conditions. His uncorrected output reports F = 4.10 on 3 and 45 degrees of freedom, and his software prints an epsilon estimate of 0.72. What does applying that correction do to his results? (2 decimals)","o":["The degrees of freedom stay at 3 and 45 and F falls to 2.95.","The degrees of freedom become 2.16 and 32.40 and F rises to 5.69.","The degrees of freedom become 2.16 and 32.40, F stays at 4.10, and the p-value goes up.","The degrees of freedom become 4.17 and 62.50 and the p-value goes down."],"a":2,"pct":30,"typ":"sphericity_fix","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. An epsilon value quoted next to a repeated-measures F. The correction does exactly one arithmetical thing: it MULTIPLIES both degrees of freedom by epsilon. It never touches F.","plain":"In plain words: the correction does not change the F you calculated. It changes the yardstick you compare it against, by shrinking both df. A smaller df makes the same F less impressive, so the p-value rises.","work":"Step 1. Corrected numerator df: (k - 1) x epsilon = 3 x 0.72 = 2.16.\nStep 2. Corrected denominator df: (n - 1)(k - 1) x epsilon = 45 x 0.72 = 32.40.\nStep 3. The F ratio. Nothing in the F has changed: the sums of squares and the mean squares are the same numbers as before, so F is still 4.10.\nStep 4. The p-value. With df 3 and 45 the p for F = 4.10 is .012. With df 2.16 and 32.40 it is .023. So p has roughly doubled.\nStep 5. The logic. Epsilon measures how badly sphericity is broken and lies between 1 / (k - 1) and 1. Multiplying by a number below 1 shrinks the df, which fattens the tail of the F distribution, which raises p. The test becomes harder to pass, which is exactly the protection you wanted.\nMemory hook: epsilon moves the GOALPOSTS, not the ball. Both df shrink, F stands still, p goes up.","wrongs":["The correction does not touch F. 2.95 is 4.10 x 0.72, which is epsilon applied to the wrong quantity. Epsilon multiplies the degrees of freedom.","The df are right but F does not move. 5.69 is 4.10 / 0.72, which again applies epsilon to the F. And a correction that made the result MORE significant would defeat its own purpose.","","This DIVIDES the df by epsilon: 3 / 0.72 = 4.17 and 45 / 0.72 = 62.50. The correction multiplies, so the df must get smaller, not larger. And since epsilon is at most 1, the p-value always rises; it can never fall."],"trap":"Applying epsilon to F instead of to the df. The F ratio is fixed by the data. Epsilon only adjusts how extreme that F has to be before you believe it.","tri":"Symptom: an epsilon estimate and a repeated-measures F. -&gt; Diagnosis: what an epsilon correction does. -&gt; Treatment: multiply BOTH df by epsilon, leave F unchanged, and expect p to RISE because epsilon is always at most 1. The correction makes the test more conservative, which is the point: it undoes the inflated Type I error caused by the broken assumption."},{"n":8,"q":"Giorgos Papadopoulos has every participant complete four problem-solving tasks, and all of them do the tasks in the order A, B, C, D. He is confident the design is fine because everyone did every task. What is the specific risk he has taken, and how would it be dealt with?","o":["Position in the sequence moves together with the task, so an order effect such as fatigue cannot be told apart from a genuine difference between tasks. Varying the order across participants breaks that link.","The residual degrees of freedom will be too small, which can be dealt with by adding more participants.","Sphericity is certain to be violated, which can be dealt with by applying a Greenhouse-Geisser correction.","Nothing is at risk, because every participant receives every condition, so the design is balanced by definition."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"counterbalancing","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Every participant gets every condition, but always in the SAME order. That is the uncounterbalanced design. Name the confound between position and condition, then name varying the order as the remedy.","plain":"In plain words: task D is always the one people do when they are most tired, and task A is always the one they do when they are freshest. So \"being fourth\" and \"being task D\" are the same thing in his data.","work":"Step 1. Identify what varies together. Task A is always first, D always fourth, for everybody. Position and task never come apart.\nStep 2. Name the order effects attached to position. Practice: people get better at the format. Fatigue and boredom: later tasks are done by a tired participant. Carry-over: a strategy learned on task B is still being used on task C.\nStep 3. See what that does to the result. If scores drop across the four tasks, he cannot tell whether D is a harder task or simply the one done last. The two explanations fit the data equally well, which is the definition of a confound.\nStep 4. The remedy. Vary the order across participants, either with all possible orders or with a balanced scheme such as a Latin square, so that each task appears in each position equally often. Order effects then spread evenly across all four tasks and stop favouring any one of them.\nStep 5. Note what he got right. Every participant doing every task is indeed the strength of the design: it is what removes person differences from the error term. The sequence is a separate issue, and it is the one he has overlooked.\nExplain it to a friend: if the last exam of the day is always maths, you can never tell whether maths is hard or whether people are just tired by then.","wrongs":["","The residual df is (n - 1)(k - 1), which depends on how many people and how many conditions there are, not on the order they come in. More participants would not touch the order confound at all.","A fixed order does not guarantee that sphericity fails, and a Greenhouse-Geisser correction adjusts the degrees of freedom. It does nothing about a confound between position and condition.","\"Everyone gets every condition\" is what makes the design within subjects. It says nothing about the ORDER, and it is the order that is unbalanced here."],"trap":"Thinking that giving everyone all the conditions is enough. Balance across participants and balance across POSITIONS are two different things, and only the second is counterbalancing.","tri":"Symptom: a within-subjects design run in one fixed order. -&gt; Diagnosis: order effects and counterbalancing. -&gt; Treatment: say that position in the sequence is confounded with the condition, so practice, fatigue and carry-over cannot be separated from the treatment effect. Remedy: vary the order across participants so that each condition occupies each position equally often."},{"n":9,"q":"Lotte Bakker tested 10 people under three conditions, with every person taking part in all three. A colleague asks what would have happened to the analysis if those same 30 scores had instead come from 30 different people, one score each. What would change?","o":["Nothing would change, because the 30 numbers themselves would be the same.","The variation between persons would stay inside the error term instead of being lifted out of it, so the error mean square would be larger and the F smaller.","The error term would be smaller, because 30 people is a bigger sample than 10.","The F would be identical, because F depends only on the differences between the condition means."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_power","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. The same numbers presented as a within-subjects and then as a between-subjects design. The question is about the ERROR TERM. In the within version the person differences come out; in the between version they cannot.","plain":"In plain words: with the same people in every condition you can work out how much of the spread is just \"this person scores high in general\" and set it aside. With 30 different people there is no way to tell that apart from noise, so it all counts against you.","work":"Step 1. The within-subjects analysis. SS total splits into SS between persons and SS within persons. The conditions and the residual are then carved out of the within-persons part only, so the residual contains no person differences.\nStep 2. The between-subjects analysis. Each person contributes one score, so there is no way to separate \"this person is a high scorer\" from random noise. All of that variation lands in SS error.\nStep 3. Compare the denominators. The between version has a LARGER sum of squares in its error term than the within version, for the very same 30 numbers.\nStep 4. Consequence. F = MS conditions / MS error. The numerator is driven by the same three condition means in both analyses, but the denominator is bigger in the between version. So F is smaller and p larger, with the identical data.\nStep 5. The qualification. The within version pays for this with fewer error df: (10 - 1)(3 - 1) = 18 rather than 30 - 3 = 27. The gain from the smaller error term usually outweighs the loss of df, which is why the within design is normally preferred.\nMemory hook: the person is either a known quantity or part of the noise. Repeated measures makes them known.","wrongs":["The numbers would be the same, but how they are DIVIDED UP would not. The partition, and so the error term, depends on the design, not only on the values.","","Having more different people does not shrink the error term. It does the opposite here: with one score per person there is no person row, so the person-to-person spread has nowhere to go but the error.","F depends on the numerator AND the denominator. The condition means would be the same, but the error mean square would be larger, so F would be smaller."],"trap":"Assuming the same numbers must give the same F. The design decides how the variation is split, and the split decides the size of the error term.","tri":"Symptom: one data set imagined as within subjects and then as between subjects. -&gt; Diagnosis: why repeated measures has more power. -&gt; Treatment: in the within version SS total splits into between persons plus within persons, and only the within part feeds the test, so the person variation leaves the error. In the between version there is no person row, so that variation stays in the error. Bigger MS error means smaller F and less power."},{"n":10,"q":"Mariam Toure is comparing two ways of running a study on how three kinds of music affect concentration. Consider these two statements.\nI. Measuring the same people under all three kinds of music takes the stable differences between people out of the error term.\nII. A design with three separate groups of people is more vulnerable to carry-over effects than a design in which everyone hears all three.\nWhich is true?","o":["Both statements are true.","Statement I is false, statement II is true.","Both statements are false.","Statement I is true, statement II is false."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"within_between","theme":8,"spot":"HINT. Two numbered statements and the both/one/other/neither grid. Judge each statement on its own and write T or F beside it BEFORE you look at the options. A true statement I is not a reason to tick \"both true\".","plain":"In plain words: decide separately whether each sentence is right, then find the option that matches your two answers.","work":"Step 1. Statement I. When the same person appears in every condition, their personal level can be estimated and removed as SS between persons, so it never enters the residual. That is the central advantage of a within-subjects design. TRUE.\nStep 2. Statement II. Carry-over means the effect of one condition lingering into the next for the same person. With three separate groups, nobody experiences a second condition, so there is nothing to carry over. Carry-over is a risk of the WITHIN design, not the between one. The statement has the two designs swapped. FALSE.\nStep 3. I true, II false, so pick the row that says exactly that.\nMemory hook: carry-over needs someone to carry it. Only a person who meets more than one condition can do that.","wrongs":["Statement II is false, because carry-over effects require the same person to meet more than one condition.","Statement I is true. Removing stable person differences from the error term is precisely what a within-subjects design does.","Statement I is true, so they cannot both be false.",""],"trap":"Statement II sounds like a sensible warning about a weakness, so students tick \"both true\" without checking which design the weakness belongs to. Carry-over is the within design's problem.","tri":"Symptom: \"I. ... II. ... Which is true?\" about within and between designs. -&gt; Diagnosis: a two-statement design item. -&gt; Treatment: mark each statement independently. Keep the trade-off straight: WITHIN gains the smaller error term and pays with order and carry-over effects; BETWEEN avoids order effects and pays with a bigger error term."}]}];
